(function(){
window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES = window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES || {};
window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES["liberian-law"] = {"M1:Sources of Liberian Law and the Rule of Law": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 1</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: SOURCES OF LIBERIAN LAW AND THE RULE OF LAW</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The Liberian Legal System</td><td>Sources of Liberian Law and the Rule of Law</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain sources of liberian law and the rule of law using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A source of law is an authority from which a legal rule derives. In Liberia, begin with the Constitution, then identify applicable legislation, lawful regulations, and relevant Supreme Court decisions. Rule of law means that public power must have a lawful basis and be subject to legal controls.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Constitution</td><td>Superior framework for public power and rights</td></tr><tr><td>Policy</td><td>Administrative guidance; cannot simply override superior law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>A rule is not valid merely because a powerful person announces it. Ask who made it, under what power, when it took effect, and whether a higher rule limits it. A constitution is different from a policy statement. A regulation is different from an Act passed by the Legislature. A judgment explains how a court resolved a dispute; its controlling reasoning matters more than a newspaper headline. Keep the text and the interpretation separate in your notes.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a county office announces that residents may never question its decisions. A weak answer says officials can do whatever they want. A strong answer first identifies the decision, obtains the written rule, and tests its authority against constitutional protections and applicable statutes. The absence of a legal source is a problem to investigate, not proof that every decision is automatically void. State which document is missing before concluding.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Articles 2 and 3. Identify the supremacy clause and the three branches of government.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both constitution and policy. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A source of law is an authority from which a legal rule derives. In Liberia, begin with the Constitution, then identify applicable legislation, lawful regulations, and relevant Supreme Court decisions. Rule of law means that public power must have a lawful basis and be subject to legal controls.</li><li>Separate constitution from policy.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Constitution</td><td>Superior framework for public power and rights</td></tr><tr><td>Policy</td><td>Administrative guidance; cannot simply override superior law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Constitution</summary><p>Superior framework for public power and rights</p></details><details><summary>Policy</summary><p>Administrative guidance; cannot simply override superior law</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which source is the starting point when a Liberian statute conflicts with the Constitution?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Constitution</li><li>A social media poll</li><li>A private policy</li><li>The oldest newspaper</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 2 establishes constitutional supremacy.</p></details><p><strong>A ministry issues a regulation. What should a researcher check first?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Its enabling legal authority</li><li>Its logo colour</li><li>Its popularity</li><li>The number of copies</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Delegated rules need a lawful source of power.</p></details><p><strong>Why is a news report insufficient as the rule for an exam answer?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It may summarise rather than establish the law</li><li>It is always false</li><li>It replaces a statute</li><li>It is a court order</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Trace the report to the primary legal text.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Constitution and Policy.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a source hierarchy chart and a 400-word analysis of the fictional county directive. Cite Articles 2 and 3 and list the facts still needed.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M1:Common Law, Statutory Law and Customary Justice": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 1</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: COMMON LAW, STATUTORY LAW AND CUSTOMARY JUSTICE</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The Liberian Legal System</td><td>Common Law, Statutory Law and Customary Justice</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain common law, statutory law and customary justice using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Common law reasoning uses judicial decisions and legal principles developed through cases. Statutory law comes from legislation. Customary law concerns recognised community practices. These sources can interact, but a custom is not automatically enforceable simply because it is longstanding.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Civil law system</td><td>A legal tradition with extensive codification</td></tr><tr><td>Civil claim</td><td>A non-criminal dispute seeking a remedy</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Liberian study requires attention to formal institutions and customary settings. Separate the question of what a community does from whether the practice is legally recognised. Identify the people, subject matter and competent institution. A foreign common-law case can illustrate reasoning but is not automatically a binding Liberian precedent. A civil-law legal system is also different from a civil lawsuit: one describes a tradition of law, the other a type of dispute.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a family says a land issue must be decided only by a village elder; another party has a court claim. Map each forum and identify the legal basis of its authority. Then check the land category, the parties, statutory safeguards and any constitutional conflict. Do not assume that customary proceedings remove access to formal justice or that all customary practices are invalid.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Identify courts and jurisdiction in the Judiciary Law. Compare these with Article 2 of the Constitution.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both civil law system and civil claim. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Common law reasoning uses judicial decisions and legal principles developed through cases. Statutory law comes from legislation. Customary law concerns recognised community practices. These sources can interact, but a custom is not automatically enforceable simply because it is longstanding.</li><li>Separate civil law system from civil claim.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Civil law system</td><td>A legal tradition with extensive codification</td></tr><tr><td>Civil claim</td><td>A non-criminal dispute seeking a remedy</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Civil law system</summary><p>A legal tradition with extensive codification</p></details><details><summary>Civil claim</summary><p>A non-criminal dispute seeking a remedy</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What makes a foreign judgment useful but not automatically controlling in Liberia?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Its jurisdiction and authority must be assessed</li><li>Its English language makes it binding</li><li>Its age makes it legislation</li><li>Every internet case binds every court</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Jurisdiction determines the status of authority.</p></details><p><strong>Which statement correctly distinguishes civil law from a civil claim?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>One can describe a legal system; the other a dispute</li><li>Both always mean criminal prosecution</li><li>Both mean customary ceremony</li><li>Neither concerns law</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A legal tradition and a type of proceeding are different concepts.</p></details><p><strong>How should a longstanding customary practice be analysed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Check recognition, scope and higher-law limits</li><li>Assume it overrides all statutes</li><li>Treat it as automatically unlawful</li><li>Ignore affected persons</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> History alone does not settle enforceability.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Civil law system and Civil claim.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a two-column comparison of statutory and customary processes for a fictional land dispute, identifying the authority you would need before choosing a forum.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M2:Finding, Checking and Citing Legal Authorities": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 2</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: FINDING, CHECKING AND CITING LEGAL AUTHORITIES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal Research and Writing</td><td>Finding, Checking and Citing Legal Authorities</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain finding, checking and citing legal authorities using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Primary authority contains the law itself, such as a statute or judgment. Secondary material explains or comments on that law. Legal research is an iterative process: define the issue, find sources, check their status, and record how they answer the question.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Primary authority</td><td>Law-producing or law-declaring text</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary source</td><td>Explanation used to locate and understand primary law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Start a research log with jurisdiction, issue, search terms, source, pinpoint reference and date checked. Use a textbook to develop vocabulary, then retrieve the cited primary material. For cases, check later treatment; for statutes, check commencement, amendments and repeal. An AI answer is a research lead, never evidence that a quoted case exists. Open each authority before citing it. Save enough citation information for another learner to find the same page or provision.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: an online article says a notice must be given within seven days but does not name the law. Search by the legal issue and relevant statute rather than copying the number. Record whether the article concerns Liberia and whether the text is current. If the law cannot be verified, write that the deadline is unconfirmed. Inventing a section number makes an answer less reliable than honestly identifying a research gap.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the Library of Congress guide to distinguish primary and secondary sources; locate a Liberian primary authority through the Judiciary.</p><p><a href=\"https://guides.loc.gov/law-secondary-resources\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal research: secondary resources — Library of Congress</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both primary authority and secondary source. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Primary authority contains the law itself, such as a statute or judgment. Secondary material explains or comments on that law. Legal research is an iterative process: define the issue, find sources, check their status, and record how they answer the question.</li><li>Separate primary authority from secondary source.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Primary authority</td><td>Law-producing or law-declaring text</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary source</td><td>Explanation used to locate and understand primary law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Primary authority</summary><p>Law-producing or law-declaring text</p></details><details><summary>Secondary source</summary><p>Explanation used to locate and understand primary law</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which item is primary authority?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The text of a court judgment</li><li>A student blog</li><li>A revision mnemonic</li><li>A news comment</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The court text provides the decision itself.</p></details><p><strong>An AI supplies a case citation you cannot find. What should you do?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Verify or omit it and disclose the gap</li><li>Invent the missing paragraph</li><li>Treat fluency as proof</li><li>Attribute it to any court</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A plausible citation can still be fabricated.</p></details><p><strong>Why record a pinpoint citation?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It identifies the exact relevant provision or passage</li><li>It replaces legal analysis</li><li>It guarantees victory</li><li>It makes all sources binding</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A reader must be able to check the precise support.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Primary authority and Secondary source.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create a research log for a fictional unpaid-invoice claim with five entries. Include one statute, one judgment, and a clear note on any unverified amendment.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M2:Case Briefing and IRAC Legal Analysis": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 2</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CASE BRIEFING AND IRAC LEGAL ANALYSIS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal Research and Writing</td><td>Case Briefing and IRAC Legal Analysis</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain case briefing and irac legal analysis using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A case brief records material facts, procedural history, issue, holding and reasoning. IRAC means Issue, Rule, Application and Conclusion. A brief explains a decision; IRAC helps organise your own analysis of a problem.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Holding</td><td>The court decision on the issue before it</td></tr><tr><td>Application</td><td>Explanation connecting the rule to the material facts</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Select facts that affect the legal test. State the issue as a question, not the whole story. Give a rule with authority, then connect each legal element to evidence and competing arguments. Application is usually the longest part. Identify uncertainty rather than hiding it. Distinguish the holding from incidental comments. In a problem question, a careful conditional conclusion is better than claiming certainty when key facts are absent.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a seller promises delivery Friday; the buyer pays; delivery never occurs. Issue: did the failure breach an enforceable obligation? Rule: establish the applicable contract terms and rule for performance. Application: payment and messages may show agreement, but an extension or excuse must be investigated. Conclusion: a breach claim may exist if formation, the due date and unexcused nonperformance are established. This is reasoning; merely writing “buyer wins” is not.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the IRAC guide, then practise on a published Liberian judgment located through the Judiciary.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.monash.edu/student-academic-success/excel-at-writing/annotated-assessment-samples/law/law-legal-problem-solving-irac\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal problem solving with IRAC — Monash University</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both holding and application. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A case brief records material facts, procedural history, issue, holding and reasoning. IRAC means Issue, Rule, Application and Conclusion. A brief explains a decision; IRAC helps organise your own analysis of a problem.</li><li>Separate holding from application.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Holding</td><td>The court decision on the issue before it</td></tr><tr><td>Application</td><td>Explanation connecting the rule to the material facts</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Holding</summary><p>The court decision on the issue before it</p></details><details><summary>Application</summary><p>Explanation connecting the rule to the material facts</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which part of IRAC connects evidence to each legal element?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Application</li><li>Title page</li><li>Citation list alone</li><li>Conclusion alone</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Application explains why the facts satisfy or fail the rule.</p></details><p><strong>What belongs in the material-facts section of a brief?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Facts relevant to the legal issue</li><li>Every detail regardless of relevance</li><li>Only the winning party opinions</li><li>Only the judge biography</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Materiality depends on the rule being applied.</p></details><p><strong>How should an uncertain outcome be stated?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Conditionally, explaining the missing facts</li><li>As guaranteed success</li><li>Without any reasoning</li><li>By inventing evidence</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A conclusion should reflect the available record.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Holding and Application.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a one-page case brief and a 600-word IRAC response to the delivery scenario. Include the strongest counterargument and two missing facts.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M3:Constitutional Supremacy and Separation of Powers": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 3</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CONSTITUTIONAL SUPREMACY AND SEPARATION OF POWERS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Constitutional Law</td><td>Constitutional Supremacy and Separation of Powers</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain constitutional supremacy and separation of powers using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Separation of powers allocates governmental functions among institutions. Constitutional supremacy requires those institutions to operate within the Constitution. Judicial review examines consistency with constitutional requirements; it is different from voting on whether a policy is popular.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Judicial review</td><td>Legal assessment of an act against superior legal requirements</td></tr><tr><td>Appeal</td><td>Review through the procedure and court authorised by law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>When analysing an institutional dispute, name the actor and function first. Then locate the provision granting power and any provision limiting it. Consider whether the alleged act is legislative, executive or judicial. Do not import a foreign constitution clause into Liberia. A court structure diagram should show who decides cases and how review works, without inventing appellate routes or jurisdictional thresholds.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a government department announces that it may reverse any court judgment by letter. The problem is institutional authority, not whether the department agrees with the outcome. Compare its claimed power with Articles 65 and 66 and the relevant procedural rules. Ask whether a lawful appeal or other remedy exists. A strong answer identifies the constitutional tension and the proper source for review rather than encouraging disobedience to an order.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Articles 2, 3 and 65–66; identify judicial power and final appellate jurisdiction.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both judicial review and appeal. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Separation of powers allocates governmental functions among institutions. Constitutional supremacy requires those institutions to operate within the Constitution. Judicial review examines consistency with constitutional requirements; it is different from voting on whether a policy is popular.</li><li>Separate judicial review from appeal.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Judicial review</td><td>Legal assessment of an act against superior legal requirements</td></tr><tr><td>Appeal</td><td>Review through the procedure and court authorised by law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Judicial review</summary><p>Legal assessment of an act against superior legal requirements</p></details><details><summary>Appeal</summary><p>Review through the procedure and court authorised by law</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which source should define the powers of Liberia government branches?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Liberian Constitution</li><li>A foreign election poster</li><li>A company handbook</li><li>An anonymous message</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The local constitutional text supplies the institutional framework.</p></details><p><strong>What is the focus of judicial review?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Legality under applicable superior law</li><li>Whether a judge likes a minister</li><li>How many people agree online</li><li>A private marketing campaign</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Review concerns lawful authority and limits.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish an appeal from political disagreement with a judgment?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Review must follow an authorised legal route</li><li>Disagreement automatically cancels judgments</li><li>Only newspapers can review courts</li><li>No legal review exists anywhere</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Institutional disagreement does not create review powers.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Judicial review and Appeal.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draw a Liberian institutions chart with constitutional article references and write 500 words on the fictional departmental reversal.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M3:Fundamental Rights and Due Process in Liberia": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 3</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: FUNDAMENTAL RIGHTS AND DUE PROCESS IN LIBERIA</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Constitutional Law</td><td>Fundamental Rights and Due Process in Liberia</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain fundamental rights and due process in liberia using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A fundamental right protects an interest recognised by the Constitution. Due process concerns legally required fairness in decisions affecting protected interests. A rights analysis identifies the holder, state action, protected interest, restriction and available legal response.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Due process</td><td>Required legal fairness in the decision-making process</td></tr><tr><td>Merits</td><td>The substantive question whether a claim is justified</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Read the full article rather than a slogan about freedom. Identify whether the text protects persons, citizens or another specified group. Notice exceptions and linked procedures. Analyse equality using the treatment being compared and the justification offered. A right to a fair hearing does not predetermine the hearing result. Constitutional protections should guide procedure even when an allegation is unpopular.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a public institution removes a student from its programme after an accusation without explaining the case or offering a response. Identify the decision and the student interest, obtain the governing rules, and assess the constitutional and procedural protections. Separate whether misconduct occurred from whether the process was fair. A remedy for procedural failure may involve a lawful reconsideration rather than automatic permanent reinstatement.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Articles 11, 20 and 21 and record the rights, procedures and qualifications in the text.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both due process and merits. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A fundamental right protects an interest recognised by the Constitution. Due process concerns legally required fairness in decisions affecting protected interests. A rights analysis identifies the holder, state action, protected interest, restriction and available legal response.</li><li>Separate due process from merits.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Due process</td><td>Required legal fairness in the decision-making process</td></tr><tr><td>Merits</td><td>The substantive question whether a claim is justified</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Due process</summary><p>Required legal fairness in the decision-making process</p></details><details><summary>Merits</summary><p>The substantive question whether a claim is justified</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Does a fair hearing guarantee that the claimant wins?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, it concerns fair process</li><li>Yes, in every case</li><li>Only if the claimant is famous</li><li>It eliminates factual disputes</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Fair procedure and substantive entitlement are separate questions.</p></details><p><strong>Before saying a right was violated, what should be identified?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The protected interest and challenged action</li><li>Only a political slogan</li><li>Only the claimant age</li><li>Only foreign case names</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A legal claim needs an applicable right and facts.</p></details><p><strong>Why read the whole constitutional article?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Qualifications and procedures can change its application</li><li>Every article says the same thing</li><li>Headlines replace the text</li><li>Exceptions are never relevant</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Partial reading can distort scope.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Due process and Merits.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a rights-analysis table for the fictional student removal. Quote no more than a short phrase; paraphrase and cite the relevant article.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M4:Civil Jurisdiction, Venue and Service of Process": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 4</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CIVIL JURISDICTION, VENUE AND SERVICE OF PROCESS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Civil Procedure</td><td>Civil Jurisdiction, Venue and Service of Process</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain civil jurisdiction, venue and service of process using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Jurisdiction is a court legal authority over a matter or person. Venue concerns the authorised location for proceedings. Service of process gives notice by the legally required method. These requirements are related but are not interchangeable.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jurisdiction</td><td>Legal competence of a court</td></tr><tr><td>Venue</td><td>Legally proper place for the proceeding</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Before drafting a claim, identify the court competence, the parties and how notice must be given. A meritorious claim can encounter procedural barriers. Make a separate checklist for subject matter, parties, venue and service. Foreign federal jurisdiction rules in a video illustrate distinctions only; do not transplant dollar thresholds or filing routes into Liberia. For real proceedings, use the applicable statute, current rules and professional advice.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a Paynesville supplier files an invoice claim and sends the defendant only an informal text. The defendant lives in another county. Investigate court competence and venue, then the authorised service method. The text may show actual awareness but does not by itself prove legally sufficient service. Keep proof of each procedural step in a dated record.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Chapters 3 and 4 and check the applicable Judiciary Law and current court rules.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both jurisdiction and venue. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Jurisdiction is a court legal authority over a matter or person. Venue concerns the authorised location for proceedings. Service of process gives notice by the legally required method. These requirements are related but are not interchangeable.</li><li>Separate jurisdiction from venue.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jurisdiction</td><td>Legal competence of a court</td></tr><tr><td>Venue</td><td>Legally proper place for the proceeding</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Jurisdiction</summary><p>Legal competence of a court</p></details><details><summary>Venue</summary><p>Legally proper place for the proceeding</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which concept concerns court authority over the type of claim?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Subject-matter jurisdiction</li><li>Venue alone</li><li>The claimant preference</li><li>Court decoration</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Competence over the matter differs from location.</p></details><p><strong>What does venue principally concern?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The legally proper place for proceedings</li><li>The truth of testimony</li><li>The damages amount alone</li><li>The lawyer nationality</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Venue addresses geographical placement under law.</p></details><p><strong>Why retain proof of service?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Notice must be shown through the authorised process</li><li>It proves every allegation</li><li>It replaces a judgment</li><li>It is a tax receipt</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Service is an independent procedural requirement.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Jurisdiction and Venue.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Build a four-part filing checklist for the invoice scenario. Cite the chapter you would consult for each question; do not invent filing deadlines.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M4:Pleadings, Disclosure, Judgment and Civil Appeals": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 4</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: PLEADINGS, DISCLOSURE, JUDGMENT AND CIVIL APPEALS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Civil Procedure</td><td>Pleadings, Disclosure, Judgment and Civil Appeals</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain pleadings, disclosure, judgment and civil appeals using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Pleadings state claims and responses. Disclosure concerns obtaining relevant information through lawful procedure. A judgment decides an issue or case. An appeal asks an authorised reviewing court to examine alleged errors under the applicable process.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pleading</td><td>Formal statement of a claim or response</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Material offered to establish disputed facts</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>A complaint should organise parties, material facts, legal grounds and relief. An answer separates admissions, denials and defences. Disclosure is not permission to take private records without authority. After judgment, identify the reviewable issue, relevant record and procedural route. Deadlines and required steps must be researched directly; a foreign video or general course note is not a safe filing calendar.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: the supplier alleges nonpayment; the customer has a receipt but no delivery record. In a mock answer, admit the agreement if true, dispute nonpayment with the receipt, and identify whether delivery is contested. Plan requests for the invoice, payment ledger and delivery acknowledgement. On appeal, identify an actual alleged legal error instead of repeating that the result feels unfair.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Chapters 9, 13 and 51 together with the current rules for the relevant court.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both pleading and evidence. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Pleadings state claims and responses. Disclosure concerns obtaining relevant information through lawful procedure. A judgment decides an issue or case. An appeal asks an authorised reviewing court to examine alleged errors under the applicable process.</li><li>Separate pleading from evidence.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pleading</td><td>Formal statement of a claim or response</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Material offered to establish disputed facts</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Pleading</summary><p>Formal statement of a claim or response</p></details><details><summary>Evidence</summary><p>Material offered to establish disputed facts</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What is the principal job of an answer?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Respond to allegations and state applicable defences</li><li>Automatically erase the complaint</li><li>Replace witness testimony</li><li>Publish private records</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> An answer joins and clarifies the disputed issues.</p></details><p><strong>Why separate pleading from evidence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An allegation is not itself proof</li><li>Every allegation proves itself</li><li>Receipts are pleadings only</li><li>Evidence is never relevant</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A party must support contested facts with admissible material.</p></details><p><strong>A mock appeal says only “the decision is unfair”. What is missing?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An identified error tied to the record and law</li><li>A brighter cover page</li><li>The judge home address</li><li>A social media petition</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Appellate analysis needs a legally relevant ground.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Pleading and Evidence.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft an educational complaint outline, answer outline and disclosure list for the supplier dispute. Mark all documents “fictional classroom exercise”.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M5:Contract Formation and Enforceability": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 5</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CONTRACT FORMATION AND ENFORCEABILITY</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Contracts</td><td>Contract Formation and Enforceability</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain contract formation and enforceability using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A contract is an agreement that the applicable law will enforce. Formation analysis distinguishes an offer, acceptance, the exchange or consideration where required, capacity, legality and any formal requirements. A conversation about a possible deal is not necessarily a completed agreement.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Offer</td><td>Proposal sufficiently definite to invite acceptance</td></tr><tr><td>Negotiation</td><td>Discussion that may not yet create binding obligations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Read words and conduct objectively in context. Identify the parties, goods or services, price, timing and acceptance method. Consider whether a message is an invitation to negotiate rather than an offer. A changed term can require further agreement. Do not assume every oral agreement is unenforceable or that a signature cures illegality. Check the local law applicable to the particular transaction.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a Monrovia printer quotes $200 for 500 booklets; the buyer replies “I accept at $150”. The changed price raises a counteroffer issue, not clear acceptance of the original terms. A later message agreeing $150 may establish agreement. Assemble the whole exchange and payment evidence. Distinguish proof of negotiation from proof of final assent.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Study the breach-of-contract pleading form in Appendix I; find Liberian judgments on formation through the Judiciary.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both offer and negotiation. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A contract is an agreement that the applicable law will enforce. Formation analysis distinguishes an offer, acceptance, the exchange or consideration where required, capacity, legality and any formal requirements. A conversation about a possible deal is not necessarily a completed agreement.</li><li>Separate offer from negotiation.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Offer</td><td>Proposal sufficiently definite to invite acceptance</td></tr><tr><td>Negotiation</td><td>Discussion that may not yet create binding obligations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Offer</summary><p>Proposal sufficiently definite to invite acceptance</p></details><details><summary>Negotiation</summary><p>Discussion that may not yet create binding obligations</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A buyer accepts only at a different price. What issue arises?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether this is a counteroffer rather than acceptance</li><li>Automatic acceptance of every term</li><li>Automatic criminal guilt</li><li>No need for evidence</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A changed essential term can prevent matching assent.</p></details><p><strong>Why identify capacity in formation analysis?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Some parties may lack legally sufficient ability to contract</li><li>Capacity means the contract page size</li><li>All adults must refuse agreements</li><li>It replaces agreement</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Enforceability can depend on legal capacity.</p></details><p><strong>Does a signature alone make an unlawful bargain enforceable?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, legality must still be checked</li><li>Yes, always</li><li>Only if written in blue</li><li>Only if printed twice</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Formal execution does not remove legal prohibitions.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Offer and Negotiation.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Produce a formation timeline from six fictional messages and write an IRAC answer on the changed-price reply.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M5:Contract Breach, Defences and Remedies": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 5</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CONTRACT BREACH, DEFENCES AND REMEDIES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Contracts</td><td>Contract Breach, Defences and Remedies</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain contract breach, defences and remedies using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A breach is failure to perform an enforceable contractual obligation without a legally sufficient excuse. Remedies respond to established liability; damages, restitution, rescission and specific performance serve different purposes and depend on applicable law.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Damages</td><td>Money awarded for legally recoverable loss</td></tr><tr><td>Specific performance</td><td>A court-directed performance remedy where legally available</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Analyse formation before breach. State the obligation, performance date and actual conduct, then examine any variation, payment, prevention or recognised excuse. Link loss to the breach and explain evidence. Mitigation asks what reasonable steps could limit loss, not whether the injured party must accept any substitute. Avoid treating every disappointing transaction as fraud or promising a particular remedy without local authority.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a tailor agrees to supply uniforms for $300; late delivery makes a documented $40 replacement expense necessary. Distinguish recovery of money paid from compensation for additional loss. A claim for $10,000 based only on anger lacks a reasoned evidential basis. If the buyer accepted a new deadline, analyse that change before calling the delivery late.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate contract-remedy cases and compare the civil-law forms for damages and specific performance.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both damages and specific performance. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A breach is failure to perform an enforceable contractual obligation without a legally sufficient excuse. Remedies respond to established liability; damages, restitution, rescission and specific performance serve different purposes and depend on applicable law.</li><li>Separate damages from specific performance.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Damages</td><td>Money awarded for legally recoverable loss</td></tr><tr><td>Specific performance</td><td>A court-directed performance remedy where legally available</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Damages</summary><p>Money awarded for legally recoverable loss</p></details><details><summary>Specific performance</summary><p>A court-directed performance remedy where legally available</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What should be established before alleging a contractual breach?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An enforceable obligation and its terms</li><li>Only disappointment</li><li>Only a verbal accusation</li><li>Only a large loss estimate</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> There must first be an obligation capable of breach.</p></details><p><strong>Which remedy aims at carrying out an obligation rather than only paying money?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Specific performance</li><li>An invoice reminder</li><li>A newspaper notice</li><li>A private apology</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Specific performance concerns ordered performance where available.</p></details><p><strong>Why document replacement expenses?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>To connect claimed loss to the breach</li><li>To guarantee any amount requested</li><li>To prove every defence fails</li><li>To avoid checking terms</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Remedy analysis depends on causation and evidence.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Damages and Specific performance.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a loss schedule with supporting evidence and two possible remedies for the uniform scenario. Explain one defence and its effect.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M6:Negligence: Duty, Breach, Causation and Loss": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 6</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: NEGLIGENCE: DUTY, BREACH, CAUSATION AND LOSS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Torts</td><td>Negligence: Duty, Breach, Causation and Loss</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain negligence: duty, breach, causation and loss using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Negligence is a civil wrong involving failure to meet an applicable standard of reasonable care. Analyse duty, breach, causation and legally recoverable damage separately. An accident alone does not establish every element.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Breach of duty</td><td>Failure to meet the applicable care standard</td></tr><tr><td>Causation</td><td>The legally relevant connection between conduct and loss</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Duty asks whether the defendant owed the relevant obligation. Breach compares conduct with the standard in the circumstances, without judging solely through hindsight. Factual causation asks whether the breach caused the loss; legal causation concerns the limits of responsibility. List evidence for each element. Defences and the treatment of claimant fault depend on applicable Liberian law; do not import a foreign percentage rule.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a shop leaves a leaking container beside its entrance; a customer slips and incurs a verified clinic expense. Identify who controlled the entrance, whether the danger was known or reasonably discoverable, and whether the spill caused the fall. A witness who saw the customer trip elsewhere changes causation. A good answer tests competing explanations before proposing compensation.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Study the negligence pleading form in Appendix I and locate a Liberian negligence judgment.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both breach of duty and causation. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Negligence is a civil wrong involving failure to meet an applicable standard of reasonable care. Analyse duty, breach, causation and legally recoverable damage separately. An accident alone does not establish every element.</li><li>Separate breach of duty from causation.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Breach of duty</td><td>Failure to meet the applicable care standard</td></tr><tr><td>Causation</td><td>The legally relevant connection between conduct and loss</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Breach of duty</summary><p>Failure to meet the applicable care standard</p></details><details><summary>Causation</summary><p>The legally relevant connection between conduct and loss</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which statement best describes an accident in negligence analysis?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It requires an element-by-element investigation</li><li>It automatically proves liability</li><li>It proves intention</li><li>It eliminates causation</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> An accident is a fact, not a complete legal test.</p></details><p><strong>A spill existed but did not cause the fall. Which element is especially problematic?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Causation</li><li>The shop name</li><li>The claimant citizenship alone</li><li>The paper size</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A breach must connect to the claimed loss.</p></details><p><strong>What does the reasonable-care comparison assess?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Conduct in the relevant circumstances</li><li>Only the final injury severity</li><li>Only how wealthy the defendant is</li><li>Only popularity</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The standard concerns what reasonable care required at the time.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Breach of duty and Causation.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create an element-by-element evidence table for the shop accident and a 600-word balanced analysis including claimant fault.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M6:Intentional Torts and Civil Defences": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 6</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: INTENTIONAL TORTS AND CIVIL DEFENCES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Torts</td><td>Intentional Torts and Civil Defences</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain intentional torts and civil defences using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>An intentional tort concerns deliberate conduct meeting the elements of a civil wrong. Civil liability and criminal liability are separate inquiries even when they arise from the same event. Consent and lawful justification are possible issues, with limits set by applicable law.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tort</td><td>Civil wrong for which law may provide a remedy</td></tr><tr><td>Defence</td><td>Legally relevant answer to an otherwise established claim</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the particular wrong rather than calling every harmful act negligence. For contact, restraint or property interference, ask what was done, with what intention, and with what lawful authority. Consent has a scope: agreeing to one act does not automatically authorise another. A defence must be supported by facts and a legal rule. Remedies require a separate assessment of harm and available relief.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a shop guard locks a visitor inside after a payment argument. Investigate restraint, duration, consent and authority, and distinguish a civil claim from any criminal allegation. A store policy alone does not prove authority to detain. Do not physically test detention powers for an assignment; work only with fictional facts and lawful research.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Consult Appendix I forms on assault, battery, false imprisonment, fraud and trespass; research relevant local decisions.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both tort and defence. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>An intentional tort concerns deliberate conduct meeting the elements of a civil wrong. Civil liability and criminal liability are separate inquiries even when they arise from the same event. Consent and lawful justification are possible issues, with limits set by applicable law.</li><li>Separate tort from defence.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tort</td><td>Civil wrong for which law may provide a remedy</td></tr><tr><td>Defence</td><td>Legally relevant answer to an otherwise established claim</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Tort</summary><p>Civil wrong for which law may provide a remedy</p></details><details><summary>Defence</summary><p>Legally relevant answer to an otherwise established claim</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Can one event raise both civil and criminal issues?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Yes, the legal tests and remedies must be separated</li><li>No event can ever do so</li><li>Civil claims always replace prosecution</li><li>Criminal charges automatically award damages</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The same facts can engage distinct legal processes.</p></details><p><strong>A visitor agrees to a bag check. What must be assessed before relying on consent to detention?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The scope of the actual consent</li><li>Only the guard job title</li><li>Only the shop policy font</li><li>The visitor favourite colour</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Consent to one act does not necessarily cover another.</p></details><p><strong>What is wrong with treating a store policy as sufficient detention authority?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A private policy does not by itself supply lawful power</li><li>Policies are always criminal laws</li><li>Every policy is a court judgment</li><li>No law applies in shops</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Authority must come from applicable law and facts.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Tort and Defence.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a mock case note on the guard scenario, analysing the alleged wrong, one defence, evidence needed and possible remedy.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M7:Criminal Offences, Conduct and Mental Elements": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 7</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CRIMINAL OFFENCES, CONDUCT AND MENTAL ELEMENTS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Criminal Law</td><td>Criminal Offences, Conduct and Mental Elements</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain criminal offences, conduct and mental elements using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A criminal offence is conduct prohibited by penal law with legally specified elements and consequences. Actus reus describes required conduct, circumstances or results; mens rea describes the required mental state where the offence requires one.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Actus reus</td><td>External elements of an offence</td></tr><tr><td>Mens rea</td><td>Required mental element, where specified by law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Begin with the offence text, not a moral judgement. Break it into elements and identify what evidence addresses each. Intention, knowledge, recklessness and negligence are different ideas. Do not assume every offence has the same mental requirement. Omission liability needs an applicable duty. A person accusation, arrest or social media confession must still be analysed through the governing evidential and procedural rules.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a worker carries away a bag believing it is their own. An analysis of a theft allegation must examine ownership, taking, knowledge or intent as required by the actual offence. A genuine mistake can matter, but saying “mistake” is not proof. The answer should identify what the worker saw, what the bag contained and how the claim could be tested.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the general principles in Title 26 and a selected offence provision; check subsequent amendments.</p><p><a href=\"https://assets.tobaccocontrollaws.org/uploads/legislation/Liberia/Liberia-Penal-Law-native.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Penal Law, Title 26 — primary statute reproduction (check amendments)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both actus reus and mens rea. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A criminal offence is conduct prohibited by penal law with legally specified elements and consequences. Actus reus describes required conduct, circumstances or results; mens rea describes the required mental state where the offence requires one.</li><li>Separate actus reus from mens rea.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Actus reus</td><td>External elements of an offence</td></tr><tr><td>Mens rea</td><td>Required mental element, where specified by law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Actus reus</summary><p>External elements of an offence</p></details><details><summary>Mens rea</summary><p>Required mental element, where specified by law</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why start criminal analysis with the offence provision?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It specifies the elements to be proved</li><li>It guarantees the accused is guilty</li><li>It replaces all evidence</li><li>It eliminates defence rights</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Criminal liability depends on the applicable legal definition.</p></details><p><strong>Which term describes a required mental element?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Mens rea</li><li>Venue</li><li>Probate</li><li>Consideration</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Mental state is distinct from external conduct.</p></details><p><strong>An accused took the wrong bag honestly. What issue needs analysis?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether the required mental element is established</li><li>Whether a bag has a handle</li><li>Whether all mistakes prove guilt</li><li>Whether civil filing fees were paid</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Mistake may affect the mental requirement under the offence.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Actus reus and Mens rea.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Select one offence in the Penal Law and prepare an elements table with fictional evidence for and against each element.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M7:Criminal Defences, Participation and Sentencing": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 7</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CRIMINAL DEFENCES, PARTICIPATION AND SENTENCING</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Criminal Law</td><td>Criminal Defences, Participation and Sentencing</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain criminal defences, participation and sentencing using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A criminal defence can negate an element or provide a legally recognised justification or excuse. Participation concerns liability for involvement in another person conduct. Sentencing follows conviction and applies legally available penalties and relevant factors.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Defence</td><td>Argument affecting whether liability is established</td></tr><tr><td>Mitigation</td><td>Factors relevant to a lawful penalty after conviction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Do not confuse innocence with mitigation: mitigation addresses consequences after liability, while a defence may prevent liability. Analyse the precise role of each person in a group. Presence alone and active assistance are different facts, but the governing participation rule must be checked. For a justification, identify its conditions and limits. Never assume that revenge or a private instruction creates lawful authority.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: three people are nearby during damage to a stall; one breaks the lock, one allegedly encourages it, and one only watches. Separate their conduct and evidence rather than treating the group as a single defendant. If one claims protection from an immediate threat, analyse necessity and the applicable justification conditions. Punishment cannot be invented to match public anger.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read general responsibility and justification provisions and the penalty for the selected offence; verify amendments.</p><p><a href=\"https://assets.tobaccocontrollaws.org/uploads/legislation/Liberia/Liberia-Penal-Law-native.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Penal Law, Title 26 — primary statute reproduction (check amendments)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both defence and mitigation. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A criminal defence can negate an element or provide a legally recognised justification or excuse. Participation concerns liability for involvement in another person conduct. Sentencing follows conviction and applies legally available penalties and relevant factors.</li><li>Separate defence from mitigation.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Defence</td><td>Argument affecting whether liability is established</td></tr><tr><td>Mitigation</td><td>Factors relevant to a lawful penalty after conviction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Defence</summary><p>Argument affecting whether liability is established</p></details><details><summary>Mitigation</summary><p>Factors relevant to a lawful penalty after conviction</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which distinction separates a defence from mitigation?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Liability versus factors affecting penalty</li><li>Page numbering versus font</li><li>Civil court versus printing</li><li>Only the accused wealth</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Defences and mitigation answer different legal questions.</p></details><p><strong>Why analyse each group member separately?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Their conduct and mental involvement may differ</li><li>Everyone nearby is automatically guilty</li><li>Groups cannot be investigated</li><li>Witnesses become defendants automatically</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Individual responsibility requires individual evidence.</p></details><p><strong>What limits a proposed criminal sentence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The applicable law and lawful sentencing process</li><li>Only public anger</li><li>Only a private agreement</li><li>Only a newspaper poll</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Legal punishment must have a legal foundation.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Defence and Mitigation.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare three separate liability analyses for the stall scenario and a sentencing-research checklist without recommending an unverified penalty.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M8:Arrest, Detention, Bail and Access to Counsel": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 8</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: ARREST, DETENTION, BAIL AND ACCESS TO COUNSEL</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Criminal Procedure</td><td>Arrest, Detention, Bail and Access to Counsel</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain arrest, detention, bail and access to counsel using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Arrest brings a person under legal restraint. Detention is continuing custody. Bail concerns conditional release under applicable law; it is not a judgment of innocence or guilt. Constitutional protections and criminal procedure must be read together.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bail</td><td>Conditional release under a lawful process</td></tr><tr><td>Conviction</td><td>Determination of criminal guilt through the required process</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>For a detention timeline, record the basis, arrest time, information given, access to counsel, court appearance and orders. Identify the competent authority and the applicable safeguards. Article 21 contains important protections, but read its full qualifications before stating an absolute rule. Never substitute a foreign bail amount or timetable for Liberian law. Use authorised counsel and court processes for actual cases.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a learner hears that a relative was arrested but has only a forwarded voice note. Separate confirmed information from rumour and prepare neutral questions about the legal basis, location, counsel and court order. Do not publish accusations or obstruct officers. A classroom memo should state what the record establishes and what cannot yet be determined.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Article 21 alongside the current Criminal Procedure Law and relevant court rules.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://natlex.ilo.org/dyn/natlex2/natlex2/files/download/42875/LBR42875.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Criminal Procedure Law, Title 2 — primary code reproduction hosted by ILO (check amendments)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both bail and conviction. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Arrest brings a person under legal restraint. Detention is continuing custody. Bail concerns conditional release under applicable law; it is not a judgment of innocence or guilt. Constitutional protections and criminal procedure must be read together.</li><li>Separate bail from conviction.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bail</td><td>Conditional release under a lawful process</td></tr><tr><td>Conviction</td><td>Determination of criminal guilt through the required process</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Bail</summary><p>Conditional release under a lawful process</p></details><details><summary>Conviction</summary><p>Determination of criminal guilt through the required process</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Does release on bail determine innocence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, release and guilt are separate decisions</li><li>Yes in every case</li><li>Only for rich defendants</li><li>It cancels the offence provision</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Bail addresses custody, not a final guilt determination.</p></details><p><strong>Which record helps analyse detention safeguards?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A verified chronology and legal basis</li><li>Only an anonymous voice note</li><li>Only a family rumour</li><li>Only the officer nickname</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Time and authority are central to procedural analysis.</p></details><p><strong>Why read constitutional detention qualifications carefully?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The legal protection may contain specific conditions</li><li>A headline is more binding</li><li>All qualifications are optional</li><li>Foreign rules automatically apply</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The full provision controls its scope.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Bail and Conviction.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create a fictional detention timeline and a safeguards checklist citing Article 21. Explain why a bail decision does not decide the accusation.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M8:Criminal Trials, Proof and Fair-Hearing Safeguards": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 8</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CRIMINAL TRIALS, PROOF AND FAIR-HEARING SAFEGUARDS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Criminal Procedure</td><td>Criminal Trials, Proof and Fair-Hearing Safeguards</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain criminal trials, proof and fair-hearing safeguards using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A criminal trial is a legally regulated process for determining a charge. The presumption of innocence and prosecution burden require proof through the applicable process. A charge is an allegation, not a conviction.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Charge</td><td>Formal accusation to be determined</td></tr><tr><td>Presumption of innocence</td><td>Protection against treating accusation alone as established guilt</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Map accusation, plea, evidence, defence response, adjudication and any lawful review. Examine each element of the charge against the evidence. A defendant silence must be analysed under the constitutional protection, not treated as a confession. Assess witness reliability without assuming that confident delivery proves truth. A fair hearing requires both institutional safeguards and meaningful opportunity to respond.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: an online crowd declares a trader guilty because the police arrested them. Explain the missing legal steps and the difference between suspicion and proof. If two witnesses disagree, identify what each could observe, the source of knowledge and corroborating records. A model answer should analyse evidence and the burden rather than count supportive comments.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Article 21 and the relevant trial provisions of the Criminal Procedure Law; compare with the current court rules.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://natlex.ilo.org/dyn/natlex2/natlex2/files/download/42875/LBR42875.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Criminal Procedure Law, Title 2 — primary code reproduction hosted by ILO (check amendments)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both charge and presumption of innocence. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A criminal trial is a legally regulated process for determining a charge. The presumption of innocence and prosecution burden require proof through the applicable process. A charge is an allegation, not a conviction.</li><li>Separate charge from presumption of innocence.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Charge</td><td>Formal accusation to be determined</td></tr><tr><td>Presumption of innocence</td><td>Protection against treating accusation alone as established guilt</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Charge</summary><p>Formal accusation to be determined</p></details><details><summary>Presumption of innocence</summary><p>Protection against treating accusation alone as established guilt</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What does an arrest by itself establish about guilt?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It does not replace a lawful determination of the charge</li><li>It proves every element</li><li>It is the final verdict</li><li>It eliminates defence rights</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Restraint and adjudication are distinct.</p></details><p><strong>How should conflicting witnesses be assessed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Examine knowledge, reliability and corroboration</li><li>Choose the louder witness</li><li>Count social media followers</li><li>Ignore all testimony</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Reliability depends on evidence, not performance alone.</p></details><p><strong>Which distinction is central to criminal proof?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Suspicion versus proof of the required elements</li><li>Formal shoes versus sandals</li><li>County size versus population</li><li>Ink colour versus paper</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A suspicion may justify inquiry without establishing guilt.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Charge and Presumption of innocence.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a fictional trial-stage diagram and a 500-word explanation correcting the crowd claim, with constitutional references.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M9:Relevance, Burdens and Standards of Proof": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 9</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: RELEVANCE, BURDENS AND STANDARDS OF PROOF</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Relevance, Burdens and Standards of Proof</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain relevance, burdens and standards of proof using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Relevance concerns whether evidence bears on a legally material fact. Admissibility asks whether the applicable rules permit it. Weight concerns persuasive value. The burden identifies who must prove what; the standard identifies the required degree of proof.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Admissibility</td><td>Whether evidence may legally be received</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>How persuasive the admitted material is</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Begin with the disputed legal element and explain why an item matters. A relevant item can still face an exclusion rule. An admissible item can carry little weight. Standards vary by proceeding and issue; do not apply a criminal standard mechanically to every civil question. Distinguish the burden of producing evidence from the ultimate burden of persuasion when the governing law does so.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a claimant proves the defendant owns an expensive car but alleges an unpaid invoice. Wealth may have no direct connection to whether this invoice was paid. A bank transfer record linked to the invoice is more informative. Explain the link, then test authenticity and whether another payment could account for the record. Do not treat volume of documents as proof quality.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Chapter 25 on evidence and consult Liberian decisions on proof in the relevant type of proceeding.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both admissibility and weight. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Relevance concerns whether evidence bears on a legally material fact. Admissibility asks whether the applicable rules permit it. Weight concerns persuasive value. The burden identifies who must prove what; the standard identifies the required degree of proof.</li><li>Separate admissibility from weight.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Admissibility</td><td>Whether evidence may legally be received</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>How persuasive the admitted material is</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Admissibility</summary><p>Whether evidence may legally be received</p></details><details><summary>Weight</summary><p>How persuasive the admitted material is</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Can relevant evidence still be inadmissible?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Yes, another applicable rule may exclude it</li><li>No, relevance guarantees admission</li><li>Only if it is short</li><li>Only if it is printed</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Relevance is one part of admissibility.</p></details><p><strong>What does evidential weight concern?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Persuasive value</li><li>Document kilograms</li><li>The filing fee</li><li>The witness nationality alone</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Weight describes strength in proving a point.</p></details><p><strong>A defendant expensive car is offered to prove nonpayment. What must be explained?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Its connection to a material disputed fact</li><li>Its colour alone</li><li>Its brand popularity</li><li>The claimant favourite model</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Evidence needs a logical connection to the issue.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Admissibility and Weight.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Classify ten fictional items by relevance, admissibility issues and likely weight for the invoice dispute.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M9:Hearsay, Documents, Digital Evidence and Authentication": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 9</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: HEARSAY, DOCUMENTS, DIGITAL EVIDENCE AND AUTHENTICATION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Hearsay, Documents, Digital Evidence and Authentication</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain hearsay, documents, digital evidence and authentication using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Hearsay analysis asks whether an out-of-court statement is being used to prove what it asserts under the governing evidence rules. Authentication concerns showing an item is what its proponent claims. A screenshot content and its provenance are separate issues.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Authentication</td><td>Showing an item is what it is claimed to be</td></tr><tr><td>Provenance</td><td>The recorded origin and history of material</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>State the purpose for which a statement is offered. The same words can raise different issues if offered as notice instead of truth. Then check the local rule and any exception. For documents, preserve originals, source, dates and lawful access. For digital material, record the account, export method and continuity. A printout does not automatically prove who created or sent it.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: “My neighbour said the vendor received payment” is offered to prove payment. Identify the original speaker and truth-purpose issue, then seek first-hand or documentary support. A cropped transfer screenshot also needs account and transaction verification. Do not edit images to make them appear clearer evidence; keep the original and distinguish any annotated classroom copy.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the applicable evidence provisions and find local decisions on documentary proof; compare rather than import foreign hearsay rules.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both authentication and provenance. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Hearsay analysis asks whether an out-of-court statement is being used to prove what it asserts under the governing evidence rules. Authentication concerns showing an item is what its proponent claims. A screenshot content and its provenance are separate issues.</li><li>Separate authentication from provenance.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Authentication</td><td>Showing an item is what it is claimed to be</td></tr><tr><td>Provenance</td><td>The recorded origin and history of material</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Authentication</summary><p>Showing an item is what it is claimed to be</p></details><details><summary>Provenance</summary><p>The recorded origin and history of material</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why identify the purpose of an out-of-court statement?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The evidential use affects the hearsay analysis</li><li>All statements are automatically excluded</li><li>Purpose never matters</li><li>It determines the witness salary</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Truth and other purposes may be treated differently under the rule.</p></details><p><strong>Does a printed screenshot prove its author automatically?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, authentication remains necessary</li><li>Yes, every time</li><li>Only in colour</li><li>Only on expensive paper</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Format does not establish provenance.</p></details><p><strong>Which practice best preserves digital evidence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Retain the original and record lawful source and handling</li><li>Overwrite the file with edits</li><li>Delete all context</li><li>Change the timestamp</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Integrity and traceability help verification.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Authentication and Provenance.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare an evidence-preservation log and a hearsay-purpose analysis using fictional messages; include two reliability limitations.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M10:Land Categories and Customary Land Rights in Liberia": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 10</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: LAND CATEGORIES AND CUSTOMARY LAND RIGHTS IN LIBERIA</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Property and Land</td><td>Land Categories and Customary Land Rights in Liberia</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain land categories and customary land rights in liberia using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The Land Rights Act distinguishes private, customary, government and public land. Land classification helps identify ownership, management and transaction requirements. Customary land should not simply be treated as ownerless land because no individual deed is produced.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Customary land</td><td>Land rights recognised through the statutory customary-land framework</td></tr><tr><td>Public land</td><td>A distinct statutory category; not a synonym for all rural land</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the parcel and claimed category before discussing transfer. Separate ownership from occupation and management authority. Community land analysis needs evidence of the community, boundaries, recognised rights and lawful decision process. Do not assume a chief signature alone settles every community consent issue. Record women and other affected members as participants in the analysis, not as invisible background.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: an investor is offered a rural parcel by one person who claims authority for the whole community. Request the land classification, boundary evidence and legal basis for representation. Compare the proposed process with the Act. A promise of jobs is not itself proof of valid consent or title. A balanced memo identifies both development interests and unresolved rights.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Land Rights Act definitions and provisions on categories and customary land; check implementation measures with the LLA.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=79:land-rights-act&amp;start=40\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Land Rights Act 2018 — Liberia Land Authority</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both customary land and public land. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The Land Rights Act distinguishes private, customary, government and public land. Land classification helps identify ownership, management and transaction requirements. Customary land should not simply be treated as ownerless land because no individual deed is produced.</li><li>Separate customary land from public land.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Customary land</td><td>Land rights recognised through the statutory customary-land framework</td></tr><tr><td>Public land</td><td>A distinct statutory category; not a synonym for all rural land</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Customary land</summary><p>Land rights recognised through the statutory customary-land framework</p></details><details><summary>Public land</summary><p>A distinct statutory category; not a synonym for all rural land</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which classification belongs to the Land Rights Act framework?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Customary land</li><li>Social-media land</li><li>Unlimited land</li><li>Foreign-video land</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Act recognises customary land as a distinct category.</p></details><p><strong>Does absence of an individual deed prove community land is ownerless?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, customary rights require analysis</li><li>Yes in every situation</li><li>Only in rural counties</li><li>Only when an investor asks</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Documentation and rights must be assessed under the Act.</p></details><p><strong>Why check authority before accepting one signature for a community?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Representation and consent must have a lawful basis</li><li>A signature always binds every resident</li><li>Only the pen brand matters</li><li>Communities cannot have rights</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A transaction needs competent authority and required participation.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Customary land and Public land.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create a fictional parcel-classification and community-stakeholder table. Cite the exact provisions found in the Act for each entry.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M10:Deeds, Leases, Boundaries and Property Due Diligence": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 10</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: DEEDS, LEASES, BOUNDARIES AND PROPERTY DUE DILIGENCE</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Property and Land</td><td>Deeds, Leases, Boundaries and Property Due Diligence</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain deeds, leases, boundaries and property due diligence using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Due diligence is structured investigation before a transaction. A deed records a transfer claim; a lease grants specified use or possession for a term. Boundaries identify the parcel, while title analysis asks whether the seller can lawfully convey the claimed interest.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Deed</td><td>Instrument documenting a claimed conveyance</td></tr><tr><td>Lease</td><td>Grant of specified possession or use for a term</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Check seller identity and authority, the chain of title, parcel description, occupation, encumbrances and competing claims. A survey does not by itself resolve every ownership issue. Registration, execution and tax questions need the current legal process. A lease should identify premises, term, rent, permitted use, repairs and lawful termination arrangements. Explain uncertainty before anyone relies on the transaction.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: two sellers present documents for the same Paynesville lot. Compare grantor, dates, description and transaction history rather than choosing the newer photocopy automatically. Inspect only through lawful arrangements and use competent professionals for real title work. In a fictional lease exercise, distinguish the landlord ownership claim from the tenant right to occupy for a fixed term.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read transfer provisions in the Land Rights Act and examine applicable real-property procedures and current LLA guidance.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=79:land-rights-act&amp;start=40\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Land Rights Act 2018 — Liberia Land Authority</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both deed and lease. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Due diligence is structured investigation before a transaction. A deed records a transfer claim; a lease grants specified use or possession for a term. Boundaries identify the parcel, while title analysis asks whether the seller can lawfully convey the claimed interest.</li><li>Separate deed from lease.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Deed</td><td>Instrument documenting a claimed conveyance</td></tr><tr><td>Lease</td><td>Grant of specified possession or use for a term</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Deed</summary><p>Instrument documenting a claimed conveyance</p></details><details><summary>Lease</summary><p>Grant of specified possession or use for a term</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What does a survey principally help establish?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The parcel location and boundaries</li><li>Automatic unchallengeable title</li><li>All tax exemptions</li><li>The seller identity by itself</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Boundary evidence and ownership authority are distinct.</p></details><p><strong>Which item belongs in title due diligence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The seller authority and chain of title</li><li>Only the purchase price</li><li>Only the seller confidence</li><li>Only a photograph of a fence</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A seller must be able to convey the relevant interest.</p></details><p><strong>What distinguishes a lease from full ownership transfer?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A lease grants a defined use or possession interest for a term</li><li>A lease never has obligations</li><li>A lease is always a criminal charge</li><li>A lease proves every title claim</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The interest conveyed depends on the instrument and law.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Deed and Lease.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a fictional title-risk register and a one-page lease term sheet with five unresolved due-diligence questions.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M11:Marriage, Family Obligations and Child Protection": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 11</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: MARRIAGE, FAMILY OBLIGATIONS AND CHILD PROTECTION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Family Law and Estates</td><td>Marriage, Family Obligations and Child Protection</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain marriage, family obligations and child protection using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Family law regulates legal relationships and obligations involving spouses, children and other family members. Marriage status, support, custody and protection are separate questions. A family disagreement does not remove a child independent interests.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Support</td><td>Legally relevant provision for a dependent needs</td></tr><tr><td>Custody</td><td>Authority and arrangements concerning a child care under applicable law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the form of relationship, legal recognition, parties and issue. Do not assume one marriage rule applies to every statutory or customary setting. In child matters, examine applicable protection and welfare requirements, evidence of care, and competent authority. Avoid unsupported age or inheritance rules from old summaries. Distinguish family support from ownership of all household assets. Use confidential fictional facts in assignments.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: separated parents dispute school expenses and where their child should live. List the child needs, caregiving arrangements, relevant records and legal questions without declaring one parent entitled merely by gender. If violence is alleged, safety and appropriate professional referral come before a classroom negotiation plan. The learning exercise is legal analysis, not an instruction to handle a real child case alone.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Domestic Relations Law sections relevant to marriage and support; also consult the Children’s Law and current amendments through the Ministry of Justice.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=59:domestic-relations-law&amp;start=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Domestic Relations Law, Title 9 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both support and custody. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Family law regulates legal relationships and obligations involving spouses, children and other family members. Marriage status, support, custody and protection are separate questions. A family disagreement does not remove a child independent interests.</li><li>Separate support from custody.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Support</td><td>Legally relevant provision for a dependent needs</td></tr><tr><td>Custody</td><td>Authority and arrangements concerning a child care under applicable law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Support</summary><p>Legally relevant provision for a dependent needs</p></details><details><summary>Custody</summary><p>Authority and arrangements concerning a child care under applicable law</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why separate child support from custody?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They concern different obligations and arrangements</li><li>Support automatically decides every custody issue</li><li>Children have no separate interests</li><li>Custody is only a property sale</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Related family questions still require distinct analysis.</p></details><p><strong>Which approach to family-law research is sound?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Identify the recognised relationship and applicable law</li><li>Assume all marriage systems are identical</li><li>Use only a foreign video</li><li>Ignore later amendments</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Relationship type and governing law matter.</p></details><p><strong>What belongs first in an exercise involving alleged family violence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Safety and appropriate professional referral</li><li>Forced reconciliation</li><li>Publication of private names</li><li>A competition about blame</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Safety concerns should not be reduced to classroom bargaining.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Support and Custody.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a fictional family-law issue map and a neutral information checklist, including child welfare and confidentiality.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M11:Decedents’ Estates, Probate and Trust Fundamentals": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 11</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: DECEDENTS’ ESTATES, PROBATE AND TRUST FUNDAMENTALS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Family Law and Estates</td><td>Decedents’ Estates, Probate and Trust Fundamentals</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain decedents’ estates, probate and trust fundamentals using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>An estate comprises rights and obligations left by a deceased person. Probate and estate administration concern lawful authority to manage succession. A will directs distribution subject to legal requirements; a trust involves holding or administering assets for specified purposes or beneficiaries.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Probate</td><td>Court-related process concerning wills and estate administration</td></tr><tr><td>Intestacy</td><td>Succession where an effective will does not govern the property</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify assets, debts, claimed beneficiaries, documents and the proper court. Distinguish a proposed personal representative from someone lawfully authorised. A will must satisfy applicable validity requirements and may not dispose of rights the deceased never owned. Intestacy means no effective will governs the relevant property; it does not mean any relative may seize it. Do not import foreign tax exemptions or automatic trust advantages.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a family member withdraws estate money because they found an unsigned note. Investigate authority, the note status, debts and relevant succession law. Prepare an inventory and preserve records. A fair classroom answer distinguishes the family relationship from a legal appointment and explains why distributions should not be assumed before the estate obligations are identified.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Consult the current Monthly and Probate Court rules and locate the applicable Decedents Estates Law; distinguish statutory and customary issues.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/court-rules/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Court rules — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both probate and intestacy. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>An estate comprises rights and obligations left by a deceased person. Probate and estate administration concern lawful authority to manage succession. A will directs distribution subject to legal requirements; a trust involves holding or administering assets for specified purposes or beneficiaries.</li><li>Separate probate from intestacy.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Probate</td><td>Court-related process concerning wills and estate administration</td></tr><tr><td>Intestacy</td><td>Succession where an effective will does not govern the property</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Probate</summary><p>Court-related process concerning wills and estate administration</p></details><details><summary>Intestacy</summary><p>Succession where an effective will does not govern the property</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What does intestacy describe?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Succession without an effective governing will</li><li>Automatic government ownership of all property</li><li>A criminal sentence</li><li>A land survey</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Its effect depends on applicable succession law.</p></details><p><strong>Why identify estate debts before distribution?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Obligations affect what can lawfully be distributed</li><li>Debts disappear automatically at death</li><li>Only beneficiaries can create debts</li><li>An inventory is unnecessary</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Estate administration concerns obligations as well as assets.</p></details><p><strong>Does family relationship alone prove authority to administer an estate?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, lawful authority must be established</li><li>Yes in every case</li><li>Only if the relative is eldest</li><li>Only if the family agrees informally</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Appointment and applicable legal procedures require verification.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Probate and Intestacy.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Build a fictional estate inventory, debt list and authority checklist; compare a will and trust without claiming automatic tax or probate outcomes.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M12:Decent Work: Employment Terms and Workplace Rights": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 12</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: DECENT WORK: EMPLOYMENT TERMS AND WORKPLACE RIGHTS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Labour Law</td><td>Decent Work: Employment Terms and Workplace Rights</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain decent work: employment terms and workplace rights using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Employment law regulates the relationship between worker and employer under its applicable scope. A contract records agreed terms, while legislation can establish protections that cannot simply be removed by a private agreement.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Employment terms</td><td>Agreed and legally required conditions of work</td></tr><tr><td>Contractor label</td><td>Description that must be tested against the actual relationship</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Begin by determining the Act applicability rather than assuming every public or private arrangement is identical. Distinguish an employee from an independent contractor by the actual relationship and applicable test, not the label alone. Keep records of work, remuneration, hours, leave, safety and instructions. Research current wage rules directly; this course does not prescribe a static rate that could become outdated.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a business labels all staff “volunteers” but sets full working hours and pays regular amounts. Investigate the relationship before accepting the label. Record supervision, duties and payment terms, then compare the statutory definitions and protections. A worker signature on a form is evidence, but not conclusive proof that every statutory protection can be waived.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Decent Work Act introductory provisions and relevant employment-contract and protection sections; verify its application to the worker.</p><p><a href=\"https://csa.gov.lr/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Decent-Work-Act-2015.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Decent Work Act 2015 — Civil Service Agency</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both employment terms and contractor label. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Employment law regulates the relationship between worker and employer under its applicable scope. A contract records agreed terms, while legislation can establish protections that cannot simply be removed by a private agreement.</li><li>Separate employment terms from contractor label.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Employment terms</td><td>Agreed and legally required conditions of work</td></tr><tr><td>Contractor label</td><td>Description that must be tested against the actual relationship</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Employment terms</summary><p>Agreed and legally required conditions of work</p></details><details><summary>Contractor label</summary><p>Description that must be tested against the actual relationship</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why examine the real work relationship rather than only its label?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Legal classification may depend on substance</li><li>Every label is conclusive</li><li>Only the company name matters</li><li>Payment never matters</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The governing test concerns actual circumstances.</p></details><p><strong>Can a private employment agreement automatically remove every statutory protection?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, applicable mandatory protections must be checked</li><li>Yes always</li><li>Only if signed twice</li><li>Only if written abroad</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Contract terms are subject to applicable law.</p></details><p><strong>Where should a current minimum wage rule be checked?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Current official law and guidance</li><li>An undated foreign video</li><li>A guessed amount</li><li>A social-media comment</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Wage requirements can change and need authoritative verification.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Employment terms and Contractor label.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a fictional employment term sheet and a statute-applicability memo; identify wage and leave provisions to verify, without inventing rates.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M12:Termination, Workplace Disputes and Labour Remedies": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 12</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: TERMINATION, WORKPLACE DISPUTES AND LABOUR REMEDIES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Labour Law</td><td>Termination, Workplace Disputes and Labour Remedies</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain termination, workplace disputes and labour remedies using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Termination ends an employment relationship, but the legal basis, notice, procedure and consequences require separate analysis. A workplace complaint may follow a specialised process rather than an ordinary civil route.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Termination ground</td><td>The reason asserted for ending employment</td></tr><tr><td>Procedure</td><td>The lawful steps for reaching and challenging the decision</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Distinguish misconduct, capability, redundancy, resignation and expiry of a defined term. Identify evidence and the applicable procedure before evaluating fairness. An allegation is different from a substantiated ground. Keep a chronology of notices, responses, payments and referrals. A proposed remedy should follow the local law and competent forum; do not promise automatic reinstatement or fixed compensation from a foreign example.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a worker is dismissed after being absent, but had submitted medical documentation. Examine the reason, records, employer response and statutory procedure. If the alleged absence is disputed, a timeline and attendance register matter more than assumptions. In a learning memo, list remedy options to research and state what evidence could change your conclusion.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read relevant termination and dispute-resolution sections of the Decent Work Act and current National Labour Court rules.</p><p><a href=\"https://csa.gov.lr/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Decent-Work-Act-2015.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Decent Work Act 2015 — Civil Service Agency</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both termination ground and procedure. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Termination ends an employment relationship, but the legal basis, notice, procedure and consequences require separate analysis. A workplace complaint may follow a specialised process rather than an ordinary civil route.</li><li>Separate termination ground from procedure.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Termination ground</td><td>The reason asserted for ending employment</td></tr><tr><td>Procedure</td><td>The lawful steps for reaching and challenging the decision</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Termination ground</summary><p>The reason asserted for ending employment</p></details><details><summary>Procedure</summary><p>The lawful steps for reaching and challenging the decision</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why analyse both dismissal grounds and process?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A reason and lawful decision procedure are distinct</li><li>A stated reason makes procedure irrelevant</li><li>Procedure alone proves every allegation</li><li>All dismissals are identical</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Legal fairness may depend on both dimensions.</p></details><p><strong>A worker has a medical record contradicting an absence allegation. What should be done?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Compare it with the timeline and governing rules</li><li>Destroy the record</li><li>Ignore it because an allegation exists</li><li>Assume guaranteed compensation</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Relevant contrary evidence must be assessed.</p></details><p><strong>What must be checked before choosing a labour-dispute forum?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The applicable specialised statutory process</li><li>Only the nearest building</li><li>Only the worker preference</li><li>Only a foreign court name</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Competence and procedural route come from law.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Termination ground and Procedure.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a fictional dismissal chronology, evidence list and forum-research note addressing both substantive grounds and procedure.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M13:Corporations, Separate Personality and Governance": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 13</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CORPORATIONS, SEPARATE PERSONALITY AND GOVERNANCE</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Corporations, Agency and Partnership</td><td>Corporations, Separate Personality and Governance</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain corporations, separate personality and governance using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A corporation recognised by law can have a legal identity separate from its members. Limited liability and separate personality are related but distinct. Governance allocates powers and responsibilities among owners, directors and officers.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Separate personality</td><td>Entity identity distinct from members</td></tr><tr><td>Governance</td><td>Rules allocating decision powers and duties</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the entity type, incorporation documents and governing statute. Directors must act within their powers and applicable duties; a shareholder vote does not automatically cure every defect. Keep business and personal assets and decisions appropriately recorded. Legal personality does not guarantee immunity for fraud or personal wrongdoing. For a Liberian enterprise, verify the registration and reporting regime applicable to its actual operations.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a shop company signs a supply contract, but the founder is asked to pay personally. Examine the named contracting party, any personal guarantee and relevant exceptions before assuming the founder is liable. Conversely, a company account is not permission for an officer to take money for personal use. Separate ownership, control, representation and responsibility.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate the Associations Law and amendments in the Registry library; distinguish domestic business registration and applicable local compliance requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.liscr.com/onlinelibrary/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian corporate and maritime legislation — Registry library</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both separate personality and governance. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A corporation recognised by law can have a legal identity separate from its members. Limited liability and separate personality are related but distinct. Governance allocates powers and responsibilities among owners, directors and officers.</li><li>Separate separate personality from governance.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Separate personality</td><td>Entity identity distinct from members</td></tr><tr><td>Governance</td><td>Rules allocating decision powers and duties</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Separate personality</summary><p>Entity identity distinct from members</p></details><details><summary>Governance</summary><p>Rules allocating decision powers and duties</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What does separate corporate personality mean?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The recognised entity has its own legal identity</li><li>Every owner is immune from all wrongdoing</li><li>The company cannot enter contracts</li><li>Directors have no duties</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Entity identity and personal immunity are different concepts.</p></details><p><strong>Why check the contracting party name?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It helps identify whose obligation was created</li><li>Names never matter</li><li>Every founder signs personally</li><li>Only the logo determines liability</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The agreement and authority inform responsibility.</p></details><p><strong>What can change an owner exposure on a company debt?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A valid personal guarantee or applicable exception</li><li>Only the company colour</li><li>Only the office rent</li><li>Only the owner friendship</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Liability needs specific legal and factual grounds.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Separate personality and Governance.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a fictional company governance chart and compare company debt with a founder personal guarantee.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M13:Agency Authority, Partnerships and Fiduciary Duties": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 13</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: AGENCY AUTHORITY, PARTNERSHIPS AND FIDUCIARY DUTIES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Corporations, Agency and Partnership</td><td>Agency Authority, Partnerships and Fiduciary Duties</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain agency authority, partnerships and fiduciary duties using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Agency involves one person acting on behalf of another with legally relevant authority. Actual authority comes from the principal authorisation; apparent authority concerns representations attributable to the principal under the applicable test. A partnership is a distinct business relationship requiring its own analysis.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Actual authority</td><td>Authority arising from the principal authorisation</td></tr><tr><td>Apparent authority</td><td>Authority assessed through relevant outward representations and reliance</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify principal, agent, third party, representation and scope. A person claiming to be authorised is not always enough; examine what the principal communicated or permitted. Internal restrictions and third-party knowledge may affect liability. Partnership obligations depend on the legal form and agreement. Fiduciary duties concern acting appropriately for another interest, including conflicts and improper benefit where law imposes those duties.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a procurement officer orders equipment above an internal limit. The supplier has received prior authorised orders from that officer. Compare actual authority, outward representations and what the supplier knew. Do not assume an internal memo automatically defeats a third-party claim. State which facts support each side and which local rule must be located.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate the applicable Associations Law provisions and Liberian cases on agency; compare foreign examples with local authority.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.liscr.com/onlinelibrary/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian corporate and maritime legislation — Registry library</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both actual authority and apparent authority. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Agency involves one person acting on behalf of another with legally relevant authority. Actual authority comes from the principal authorisation; apparent authority concerns representations attributable to the principal under the applicable test. A partnership is a distinct business relationship requiring its own analysis.</li><li>Separate actual authority from apparent authority.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Actual authority</td><td>Authority arising from the principal authorisation</td></tr><tr><td>Apparent authority</td><td>Authority assessed through relevant outward representations and reliance</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Actual authority</summary><p>Authority arising from the principal authorisation</p></details><details><summary>Apparent authority</summary><p>Authority assessed through relevant outward representations and reliance</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What is central to actual authority?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The principal authorisation</li><li>The agent confidence alone</li><li>A supplier wish</li><li>A social media title</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Actual authority derives from the principal-agent relationship.</p></details><p><strong>Whose representations are especially important to apparent authority?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Those attributable to the principal under the legal test</li><li>Only an agent unsupported boast</li><li>Only a stranger rumour</li><li>Only a buyer preference</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Apparent authority requires legally relevant manifestations.</p></details><p><strong>Why check a supplier knowledge of an internal limit?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It may affect the authority and reliance analysis</li><li>Knowledge never matters</li><li>It makes every purchase void</li><li>It replaces the contract</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The facts may change whether the principal is bound.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Actual authority and Apparent authority.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write an agency authority memo for the equipment purchase and a short partnership term sheet addressing decisions, contributions and disputes.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M14:Negotiable Instruments and Commercial Payment Risk": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 14</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: NEGOTIABLE INSTRUMENTS AND COMMERCIAL PAYMENT RISK</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Commercial Payments and Security</td><td>Negotiable Instruments and Commercial Payment Risk</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain negotiable instruments and commercial payment risk using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A negotiable instrument is a payment document meeting statutory requirements for transfer and enforcement. A promise to pay, an order to pay, an ordinary invoice and an electronic transfer are not necessarily the same legal instrument.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Promise to pay</td><td>Undertaking by its maker</td></tr><tr><td>Order to pay</td><td>Direction to another person or institution to pay</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify maker, drawer, drawee, payee and holder where relevant. Analyse form, amount, payment terms, transfer and signature using the applicable Commercial Code. A document label does not establish negotiability. Dishonour, forged signatures and third-party claims require specific legal treatment. A foreign Uniform Commercial Code video explains concepts but does not automatically state Liberia rules.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a trader writes “I will pay if my goods sell” and calls it a negotiable note. The condition creates a statutory-form question. Compare the actual requirements rather than assuming the title controls. For a cheque, separate issuance, presentment, payment and any dishonour. Preserve records and do not promise that possession always defeats earlier claims.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate the Commercial Code provisions on payment instruments; use the Central Bank as a starting point for the current framework.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.registry.cbl.org.lr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Collateral Registry — Central Bank of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both promise to pay and order to pay. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A negotiable instrument is a payment document meeting statutory requirements for transfer and enforcement. A promise to pay, an order to pay, an ordinary invoice and an electronic transfer are not necessarily the same legal instrument.</li><li>Separate promise to pay from order to pay.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Promise to pay</td><td>Undertaking by its maker</td></tr><tr><td>Order to pay</td><td>Direction to another person or institution to pay</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Promise to pay</summary><p>Undertaking by its maker</p></details><details><summary>Order to pay</summary><p>Direction to another person or institution to pay</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Does calling a document negotiable make it legally negotiable?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, statutory requirements must be satisfied</li><li>Yes automatically</li><li>Only if printed in capital letters</li><li>Only if signed twice</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The law assesses its substance and form.</p></details><p><strong>What distinguishes a payment promise from an order?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>One undertakes payment; the other directs payment</li><li>Both always transfer land</li><li>Neither names parties</li><li>Only an order can be written</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Their roles and liabilities may differ.</p></details><p><strong>Why examine a condition attached to payment?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It may affect negotiability under the applicable law</li><li>Conditions never matter</li><li>It always proves fraud</li><li>It automatically clears debt</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The statutory requirements need close comparison.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Promise to pay and Order to pay.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Annotate three fictional payment documents and identify the statutory questions affecting transfer and enforcement.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M14:Secured Transactions, Collateral and Priority": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 14</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: SECURED TRANSACTIONS, COLLATERAL AND PRIORITY</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Commercial Payments and Security</td><td>Secured Transactions, Collateral and Priority</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain secured transactions, collateral and priority using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A secured transaction gives a creditor a legally recognised interest in collateral to support an obligation. Creation of the interest, effectiveness against others, priority and enforcement are different questions. A registry entry is not proof that every underlying agreement is valid.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Collateral</td><td>Asset supporting a secured obligation</td></tr><tr><td>Priority</td><td>Ranking of competing legally recognised claims</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify debtor, secured creditor, obligation and described asset. Check the agreement and legally required steps. Search for prior interests and analyse priority under the statute rather than assuming possession or registration always wins. Distinguish movable collateral from land-security regimes. Default does not authorise threats or unlimited self-help; enforcement must follow the applicable law.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: two lenders each claim a shop generator as collateral. Build a chronology of agreements, asset descriptions and registration. The first payment received is not automatically the priority rule. Examine whether each security interest arose and the applicable effectiveness and priority provisions. A careful conclusion states assumptions and unresolved registry information.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Central Bank Collateral Registry explanation of Chapter 5 of the Commercial Code and locate the governing text.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.registry.cbl.org.lr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Collateral Registry — Central Bank of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both collateral and priority. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A secured transaction gives a creditor a legally recognised interest in collateral to support an obligation. Creation of the interest, effectiveness against others, priority and enforcement are different questions. A registry entry is not proof that every underlying agreement is valid.</li><li>Separate collateral from priority.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Collateral</td><td>Asset supporting a secured obligation</td></tr><tr><td>Priority</td><td>Ranking of competing legally recognised claims</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Collateral</summary><p>Asset supporting a secured obligation</p></details><details><summary>Priority</summary><p>Ranking of competing legally recognised claims</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What is collateral?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An asset supporting an obligation</li><li>A court nickname</li><li>A witness statement</li><li>A company slogan</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Security concerns an asset and the supported obligation.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish creation from priority?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An interest can exist while its rank against others needs separate analysis</li><li>Creation always decides every priority issue</li><li>Priority means the debt is cancelled</li><li>Registration replaces all contracts</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The legal questions are related but distinct.</p></details><p><strong>What helps analyse two claims over a generator?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Agreement and registration chronology plus the statutory rules</li><li>Only creditor wealth</li><li>Only generator colour</li><li>Only debtor promises</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Priority depends on the applicable rule and relevant events.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Collateral and Priority.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a fictional two-creditor priority table and lawful-enforcement research checklist. Do not use real debtor records.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M15:Administrative Powers and Review of Public Decisions": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 15</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: ADMINISTRATIVE POWERS AND REVIEW OF PUBLIC DECISIONS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Administrative Law, Legislation and Tax</td><td>Administrative Powers and Review of Public Decisions</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain administrative powers and review of public decisions using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Administrative law concerns how public bodies exercise legal powers. Review asks whether a decision was authorised and reached through the applicable legal process. A merits appeal, where provided, can have a different scope from legality review.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Discretion</td><td>Choice within legally authorised limits</td></tr><tr><td>Review</td><td>Examination through an authorised legal mechanism</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Start with the enabling law, decision-maker, facts, procedure and reasons. Ask whether the body considered the legally relevant matters and respected required participation. A discretionary power is not an unlimited power. Identify the remedy and competent forum separately. Use foreign judicial-review tests comparatively; a Liberia answer needs local authority rather than a transplanted foreign statute.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: an agency refuses a licence because of an unpublished requirement unrelated to its governing law. Obtain the application, decision and rule before analysing power and procedure. A court challenge should identify a legal defect, not merely dissatisfaction. If a statutory review route exists, research its conditions and timing before choosing another route.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Identify the relevant constitutional protections, agency enabling Act and available review procedure in the current court rules.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both discretion and review. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Administrative law concerns how public bodies exercise legal powers. Review asks whether a decision was authorised and reached through the applicable legal process. A merits appeal, where provided, can have a different scope from legality review.</li><li>Separate discretion from review.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Discretion</td><td>Choice within legally authorised limits</td></tr><tr><td>Review</td><td>Examination through an authorised legal mechanism</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Discretion</summary><p>Choice within legally authorised limits</p></details><details><summary>Review</summary><p>Examination through an authorised legal mechanism</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What should be located first when analysing an agency power?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The enabling legal provision</li><li>The agency slogan</li><li>A foreign politician statement</li><li>A staff personal preference</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Public authority must have a lawful foundation.</p></details><p><strong>Does discretion mean unlimited authority?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, it operates within legal limits</li><li>Yes always</li><li>Only during busy periods</li><li>Only when a licence is expensive</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Choice remains constrained by law.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish merits review from legality review?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They may examine different questions and remedies</li><li>Both always retry every fact</li><li>Neither needs authority</li><li>Only one has paperwork</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The available review scope depends on the legal route.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Discretion and Review.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a fictional licence-decision review memo covering power, process, reasons, forum and missing facts.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M15:Legislation, Interpretation and Tax-Law Research": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 15</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: LEGISLATION, INTERPRETATION AND TAX-LAW RESEARCH</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Administrative Law, Legislation and Tax</td><td>Legislation, Interpretation and Tax-Law Research</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain legislation, interpretation and tax-law research using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Legislation is law made through the authorised legislative process. Interpretation determines meaning in context. Tax analysis identifies the taxable event, person, base, rate, period and governing amendment; rates must be researched for the relevant date.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bill</td><td>Proposed legislation awaiting the required process</td></tr><tr><td>Commencement</td><td>When a legal provision starts to operate</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Read definitions, scope, exceptions and cross-references before applying a single sentence. Distinguish a bill from an enacted law and commencement from publication date. An amendment may alter part of a scheme without replacing all of it. For tax, verify the relevant period and current official guidance. Never quote a memorised rate for a real liability without checking whether it remains applicable.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a business relies on an old training slide for a tax calculation after the law has changed. Compare the transaction date and commencement provisions, then reconstruct the applicable rule. A drafting exercise should specify who must do what, when, and with what exceptions. Ambiguous definitions create disputes even when the policy goal is clear.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the LRA current laws-and-amendments page and read an amendment together with the provision it changes.</p><p><a href=\"https://revenue.lra.gov.lr/tax-laws-and-amendments/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Tax laws and amendments — Liberia Revenue Authority</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both bill and commencement. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Legislation is law made through the authorised legislative process. Interpretation determines meaning in context. Tax analysis identifies the taxable event, person, base, rate, period and governing amendment; rates must be researched for the relevant date.</li><li>Separate bill from commencement.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bill</td><td>Proposed legislation awaiting the required process</td></tr><tr><td>Commencement</td><td>When a legal provision starts to operate</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Bill</summary><p>Proposed legislation awaiting the required process</p></details><details><summary>Commencement</summary><p>When a legal provision starts to operate</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why distinguish a bill from an enacted Act?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A proposal is not automatically operative law</li><li>Every proposal is already binding</li><li>Bills can never become law</li><li>Only the title matters</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Legal effect depends on the authorised process.</p></details><p><strong>What should be checked before applying a tax rate?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The relevant date and current governing provisions</li><li>Only an old training slide</li><li>Only a competitor guess</li><li>Only a foreign tax website</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Time and amendments determine the applicable rule.</p></details><p><strong>Why read statutory definitions first?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They can give terms a specific legal meaning</li><li>Definitions are always optional</li><li>They replace every exception</li><li>They are never used in law</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Context and defined terms shape interpretation.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Bill and Commencement.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a mock amendment-reading table and a 300-word plain-language clause regulating a fictional permit; include definitions and commencement.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M16:Human Rights Instruments and Domestic Remedies": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 16</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: HUMAN RIGHTS INSTRUMENTS AND DOMESTIC REMEDIES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Law</td><td>Human Rights Instruments and Domestic Remedies</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain human rights instruments and domestic remedies using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Human rights are protections associated with human dignity. An international declaration, treaty, constitutional provision and court remedy have different legal functions. A persuasive moral claim still needs the applicable legal framework when presented as a legal claim.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Declaration</td><td>Formal statement of principles, distinct from a treaty</td></tr><tr><td>Remedy</td><td>Legally available response to an established wrong</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the protected right, duty bearer, alleged interference and remedy. A treaty status question requires ratification and entry-into-force checks; domestic enforceability requires separate local analysis. Do not assume every international body can hear every complaint. For documentation, use accurate facts and protect identifying information. Community advocacy is stronger when the requested action names a responsible actor and a realistic legal basis.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a public service excludes people with a disability. Compare the relevant constitutional protection, applicable legislation and international instruments. Separate the evidence of exclusion from legal interpretation. A classroom recommendation can request accessible procedures and lawful review while recording what must still be researched. Avoid promising that an international complaint automatically reverses a local decision.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the UDHR and identify corresponding protections in Liberia Constitution; distinguish declarations from binding treaty obligations.</p><p><a href=\"https://europe.ohchr.org/universal-declaration-human-rights\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Universal Declaration of Human Rights — UN Human Rights regional office</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both declaration and remedy. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Human rights are protections associated with human dignity. An international declaration, treaty, constitutional provision and court remedy have different legal functions. A persuasive moral claim still needs the applicable legal framework when presented as a legal claim.</li><li>Separate declaration from remedy.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Declaration</td><td>Formal statement of principles, distinct from a treaty</td></tr><tr><td>Remedy</td><td>Legally available response to an established wrong</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Declaration</summary><p>Formal statement of principles, distinct from a treaty</p></details><details><summary>Remedy</summary><p>Legally available response to an established wrong</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why distinguish a declaration from a treaty?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Their legal forms and effects differ</li><li>Every declaration requires ratification</li><li>Treaties are never binding</li><li>Both are court verdicts</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The instrument type affects the legal analysis.</p></details><p><strong>What should a rights memorandum identify?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Right, duty bearer, facts and remedy</li><li>Only emotional language</li><li>Only a photograph</li><li>Only an organisation name</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A claim needs a legal and factual structure.</p></details><p><strong>Does every international body accept every individual complaint?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, competence and admissibility must be checked</li><li>Yes automatically</li><li>Only if a post goes viral</li><li>Only if written in capitals</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Complaint mechanisms have specific conditions.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Declaration and Remedy.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a fictional rights memorandum with a domestic-law section, an international-reference section and a remedy-research table.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M16:Treaties, International Law and Cross-Border Disputes": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 16</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: TREATIES, INTERNATIONAL LAW AND CROSS-BORDER DISPUTES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Law</td><td>Treaties, International Law and Cross-Border Disputes</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain treaties, international law and cross-border disputes using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Public international law regulates relations among states and other recognised international actors. A treaty is an international agreement governed by international law. Private international law addresses cross-border issues such as jurisdiction, applicable law and recognition of judgments.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Governing law</td><td>Law used to decide the substantive issue</td></tr><tr><td>Jurisdiction clause</td><td>Provision addressing the forum for disputes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Ask three separate questions in a cross-border contract: which court may hear the matter, which law governs, and whether a resulting judgment can be recognised elsewhere. A choice-of-law clause is not necessarily a choice of court. For treaties, check party status, reservations and dates rather than assuming signature alone settles every obligation. Analyse local implementation separately; do not announce a universal rule about all treaties in Liberia.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a Liberian exporter and a Ghanaian buyer use a contract selecting one country law but naming no forum. Separate governing law from court jurisdiction, then examine enforcement if assets are elsewhere. For a treaty assignment, record the actual Liberia status entry, relevant article and verification date. Do not substitute another country reservation for Liberia position.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the UN Treaty Collection to check one treaty status for Liberia. Read the instrument itself and distinguish international obligations from domestic application.</p><p><a href=\"https://treaties.un.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">United Nations Treaty Collection</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both governing law and jurisdiction clause. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Public international law regulates relations among states and other recognised international actors. A treaty is an international agreement governed by international law. Private international law addresses cross-border issues such as jurisdiction, applicable law and recognition of judgments.</li><li>Separate governing law from jurisdiction clause.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Governing law</td><td>Law used to decide the substantive issue</td></tr><tr><td>Jurisdiction clause</td><td>Provision addressing the forum for disputes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Governing law</summary><p>Law used to decide the substantive issue</p></details><details><summary>Jurisdiction clause</summary><p>Provision addressing the forum for disputes</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Is a choice-of-law clause necessarily a choice of court?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, they answer different questions</li><li>Yes in every agreement</li><li>Only if typed</li><li>Only for exports</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Applicable law and adjudicative forum are distinct.</p></details><p><strong>What must be checked before claiming Liberia is bound by a treaty?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Its status, dates and relevant reservations</li><li>Only the treaty title</li><li>Only another country signature</li><li>Only an online slogan</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Party status and instrument conditions determine obligations.</p></details><p><strong>Which question concerns use of a foreign judgment in another country?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Recognition and enforcement</li><li>Only contract formation</li><li>Only citizenship</li><li>Only an office licence</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A judgment may require a separate enforcement analysis.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Governing law and Jurisdiction clause.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create a treaty-status research sheet and a fictional cross-border contract issues memo covering forum, applicable law and enforcement.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M17:Liberian Maritime Law and Shipping Transactions": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 17</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: LIBERIAN MARITIME LAW AND SHIPPING TRANSACTIONS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Maritime and Insurance Law</td><td>Liberian Maritime Law and Shipping Transactions</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain liberian maritime law and shipping transactions using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Maritime law regulates shipping-related relationships and obligations. Flag-state authority, port-state authority, ship ownership and contractual responsibility are different concepts. Liberia-specific analysis begins with its Maritime Law and applicable international instruments.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Flag state</td><td>State of vessel registration with relevant regulatory responsibilities</td></tr><tr><td>Carrier</td><td>Party undertaking carriage under the applicable arrangement</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify vessel, flag, location, parties and transaction. Distinguish shipowner, operator, carrier and cargo owner. A bill of lading, charterparty and insurance policy do not serve identical functions. Determine which law and forum govern the dispute and what convention provisions may apply. A United States video statement that a rule is federal describes that jurisdiction, not Liberia.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: cargo destined for Monrovia is damaged aboard a foreign-operated Liberian-flag vessel. Gather transport documents, the damage record, notice information and insurance details. Do not assume the flag alone answers every liability question. Prepare separate issues for carriage duties, governing law, forum and insurance recovery, with deadlines identified for professional verification.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Find the Maritime Law and relevant current notices in the Liberian Registry library; identify the version and any applicable convention.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.liscr.com/onlinelibrary/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian corporate and maritime legislation — Registry library</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both flag state and carrier. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Maritime law regulates shipping-related relationships and obligations. Flag-state authority, port-state authority, ship ownership and contractual responsibility are different concepts. Liberia-specific analysis begins with its Maritime Law and applicable international instruments.</li><li>Separate flag state from carrier.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Flag state</td><td>State of vessel registration with relevant regulatory responsibilities</td></tr><tr><td>Carrier</td><td>Party undertaking carriage under the applicable arrangement</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Flag state</summary><p>State of vessel registration with relevant regulatory responsibilities</p></details><details><summary>Carrier</summary><p>Party undertaking carriage under the applicable arrangement</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Does a vessel flag automatically decide every cargo-liability issue?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, contracts and other applicable law must be analysed</li><li>Yes in every case</li><li>Only if the cargo is valuable</li><li>Only if the vessel is new</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Shipping disputes can involve multiple legal connections.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish carrier from shipowner?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The roles and contractual duties may differ</li><li>They are always identical</li><li>Neither can have obligations</li><li>Only names matter</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Responsibility follows the relevant role and law.</p></details><p><strong>What is wrong with applying a US federal maritime statement directly as Liberia law?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It skips the applicable Liberian and international framework</li><li>Every US rule binds Liberia</li><li>Maritime law has no jurisdiction</li><li>A video is a treaty</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Comparative material needs jurisdictional limits.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Flag state and Carrier.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a fictional cargo-dispute document checklist and a one-page map distinguishing flag, contract, forum and insurance issues.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M17:Insurance Contracts, Coverage and Claims": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 17</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: INSURANCE CONTRACTS, COVERAGE AND CLAIMS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Maritime and Insurance Law</td><td>Insurance Contracts, Coverage and Claims</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain insurance contracts, coverage and claims using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Insurance transfers specified risks under a contract. Coverage depends on the insured event, policy period, conditions, exclusions and applicable law. A loss and an insured loss are not always the same thing.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Risks and circumstances insured under the policy and law</td></tr><tr><td>Exclusion</td><td>Provision limiting specified risks or circumstances</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Read the schedule, definitions, insuring clause, exclusions and claims conditions together. Identify policyholder, insured interest, insurer and beneficiary where relevant. Separate coverage from valuation of an accepted claim. Indemnity concerns compensation within the contract and law; subrogation can concern an insurer rights after payment. Do not assume every policy has identical disclosure or notification rules.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a shop flood occurs after a policy expires, while the owner assumes all previous insurance continues. Compare the dates, any renewal evidence and the coverage trigger. If an exclusion is invoked, read its actual wording and legal validity rather than accepting a verbal summary. A mock claim should accurately describe the event and attach genuine fictional records, without exaggeration.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Identify the applicable Liberian insurance regulatory requirements and the policy wording; research current regulator guidance before any real claim.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.liscr.com/onlinelibrary/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian corporate and maritime legislation — Registry library</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both coverage and exclusion. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Insurance transfers specified risks under a contract. Coverage depends on the insured event, policy period, conditions, exclusions and applicable law. A loss and an insured loss are not always the same thing.</li><li>Separate coverage from exclusion.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Risks and circumstances insured under the policy and law</td></tr><tr><td>Exclusion</td><td>Provision limiting specified risks or circumstances</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Coverage</summary><p>Risks and circumstances insured under the policy and law</p></details><details><summary>Exclusion</summary><p>Provision limiting specified risks or circumstances</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Does every business loss fall within an insurance policy?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, coverage depends on terms and applicable law</li><li>Yes automatically</li><li>Only if reported online</li><li>Only if the owner is insured once</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Insurance covers specified risks and periods.</p></details><p><strong>Which documents should be read with an insuring clause?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Definitions, schedule, exclusions and conditions</li><li>Only advertising copy</li><li>Only the insurer logo</li><li>Only a bank receipt</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Policy provisions operate together.</p></details><p><strong>Why separate coverage from valuation?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether a risk is insured differs from how much is payable</li><li>Valuation proves every exclusion invalid</li><li>Both always mean the premium</li><li>Neither needs evidence</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The two questions use related but distinct analysis.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Coverage and Exclusion.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create a fictional insurance coverage matrix and claims letter. State the policy period, event, disputed term and missing evidence.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M18:Environmental Protection, Permits and Community Participation": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 18</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: ENVIRONMENTAL PROTECTION, PERMITS AND COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Environmental and Intellectual Property Law</td><td>Environmental Protection, Permits and Community Participation</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain environmental protection, permits and community participation using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Environmental law regulates activities affecting environmental resources and public welfare. An environmental impact assessment examines expected effects and alternatives within the applicable process. A permit is a legal authorisation with scope and conditions, not a general exemption from responsibility.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Impact assessment</td><td>Structured examination of proposed environmental effects</td></tr><tr><td>Permit condition</td><td>Legally relevant limit attached to authorisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the proposed activity, site, affected people, risks and competent authority. Separate a landowner permission from environmental approval. Read participation, assessment and compliance provisions together. Evidence can include lawful observations, maps and official records; do not enter dangerous or restricted sites for an assignment. Analyse prevention and remedies without presuming a particular project must be approved or refused.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a workshop proposes discharging waste near a stream used by residents. A private lease does not answer the environmental-permit question. Identify the potential impacts, legal authority, information needed and participation process. A useful compliance memo assigns actions and records rather than saying only “protect nature”. Include the possibility that missing baseline data prevents a final conclusion.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the EPA Environmental Protection and Management Law, especially the framework for assessment and permitting; check current regulations.</p><p><a href=\"https://epa.gov.lr/epa-documents/the-environmental-protection-and-management-law-of-liberia/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Environmental Protection and Management Law — EPA Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both impact assessment and permit condition. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Environmental law regulates activities affecting environmental resources and public welfare. An environmental impact assessment examines expected effects and alternatives within the applicable process. A permit is a legal authorisation with scope and conditions, not a general exemption from responsibility.</li><li>Separate impact assessment from permit condition.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Impact assessment</td><td>Structured examination of proposed environmental effects</td></tr><tr><td>Permit condition</td><td>Legally relevant limit attached to authorisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Impact assessment</summary><p>Structured examination of proposed environmental effects</p></details><details><summary>Permit condition</summary><p>Legally relevant limit attached to authorisation</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Does a lease automatically supply environmental permission?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, environmental requirements need separate analysis</li><li>Yes everywhere</li><li>Only if rent is prepaid</li><li>Only if the site is rural</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Property permission and regulatory approval differ.</p></details><p><strong>What is a central purpose of environmental impact assessment?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Examine likely effects and alternatives under law</li><li>Guarantee approval</li><li>Replace every licence</li><li>Prove all risks are imaginary</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Assessment informs lawful decisions rather than predetermining them.</p></details><p><strong>Which fieldwork is appropriate for this course?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Safe fictional or publicly available information</li><li>Unauthorised entry into a hazardous site</li><li>Collecting chemicals without training</li><li>Confronting alleged polluters alone</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Learning tasks should not create physical or legal hazards.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Impact assessment and Permit condition.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a fictional workshop environmental-risk register with authority, evidence gaps, consultation steps and compliance actions.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M18:Intellectual Property, Copyright and Brand Protection": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 18</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY, COPYRIGHT AND BRAND PROTECTION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Environmental and Intellectual Property Law</td><td>Intellectual Property, Copyright and Brand Protection</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain intellectual property, copyright and brand protection using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Intellectual property concerns legally recognised interests in creations and identifiers. Copyright, trademarks and patents protect different subject matter. Owning a physical copy is different from holding rights to reproduce or commercially exploit protected material.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Copyright</td><td>Rights concerning qualifying creative expression</td></tr><tr><td>Trademark</td><td>Sign functioning to distinguish commercial source</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the work or sign, author or rights holder, proposed use and applicable territorial law. A trademark distinguishes commercial origin; copyright concerns protected expression rather than every idea; patents concern qualifying inventions under the relevant system. Permission, licence and statutory exceptions require separate analysis. Attribution is not automatically a licence. Avoid assuming that content found online is free for commercial reuse.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a startup copies a photographer image and a competitor logo for its website. Separate copyright and trademark issues, ownership and the proposed use. Contacting a rights holder for permission differs from merely adding a credit line. A classroom audit should record asset source, licence scope, restrictions and evidence instead of claiming “no copyright intended” resolves the issue.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use WIPO to distinguish types of IP, then locate the applicable Liberian IP law and registration guidance before local application.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.wipo.int/en/web/about-ip\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Intellectual property — WIPO</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both copyright and trademark. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Intellectual property concerns legally recognised interests in creations and identifiers. Copyright, trademarks and patents protect different subject matter. Owning a physical copy is different from holding rights to reproduce or commercially exploit protected material.</li><li>Separate copyright from trademark.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Copyright</td><td>Rights concerning qualifying creative expression</td></tr><tr><td>Trademark</td><td>Sign functioning to distinguish commercial source</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Copyright</summary><p>Rights concerning qualifying creative expression</p></details><details><summary>Trademark</summary><p>Sign functioning to distinguish commercial source</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Does crediting a creator automatically permit commercial reuse?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, permission or an applicable exception is still needed</li><li>Yes for every image</li><li>Only if the credit is large</li><li>Only if the site is new</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Attribution and authorisation are distinct.</p></details><p><strong>Which right principally concerns a sign identifying commercial source?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Trademark</li><li>Probate</li><li>Bail</li><li>Venue</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Trademarks concern source-identifying signs.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish a physical copy from copyright?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Possession does not automatically transfer exploitation rights</li><li>Buying a book transfers every right</li><li>Copies can never be owned</li><li>Copyright is always physical land</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Different interests can exist in the same item.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Copyright and Trademark.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a fictional website IP audit covering five assets, permission evidence, territorial questions and replacement options.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M19:Professional Ethics, Confidentiality and Conflicts": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 19</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: PROFESSIONAL ETHICS, CONFIDENTIALITY AND CONFLICTS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal Ethics and Trial Advocacy</td><td>Professional Ethics, Confidentiality and Conflicts</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain professional ethics, confidentiality and conflicts using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Legal ethics concerns duties governing professional conduct. Confidentiality limits disclosure of protected information; evidential privilege concerns legal protection against compelled disclosure under its applicable conditions. A conflict of interest can impair independent professional judgment.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Confidentiality</td><td>Professional restriction on disclosure of protected information</td></tr><tr><td>Privilege</td><td>Legal protection against disclosure when its conditions apply</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify who the client is, the role undertaken and the relevant duties. Keep information secure and share only through lawful authority or valid permission. Consent does not automatically cure every conflict. A learner must not present this course certificate as a licence or take on reserved legal work without authorisation. For real issues, consult a properly authorised Liberian legal professional and the current rules.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a student helping a lawyer learns facts about a family dispute and is asked to post a success story online. Remove identifying information only after considering whether people could still be recognised, and obtain proper approval before any publication. If the office is approached by the opposing party, flag the potential conflict before receiving more confidential information. Do not promise that every conversation is privileged.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Judiciary Code of Moral and Professional Ethics and current professional rules; identify the rule before asserting an exception.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/code-of-moral-and-professional-ethic/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Code of Moral and Professional Ethics — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both confidentiality and privilege. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Legal ethics concerns duties governing professional conduct. Confidentiality limits disclosure of protected information; evidential privilege concerns legal protection against compelled disclosure under its applicable conditions. A conflict of interest can impair independent professional judgment.</li><li>Separate confidentiality from privilege.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Confidentiality</td><td>Professional restriction on disclosure of protected information</td></tr><tr><td>Privilege</td><td>Legal protection against disclosure when its conditions apply</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Confidentiality</summary><p>Professional restriction on disclosure of protected information</p></details><details><summary>Privilege</summary><p>Legal protection against disclosure when its conditions apply</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Are professional confidentiality and evidential privilege identical?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, their scope and legal operation can differ</li><li>Yes in every situation</li><li>Neither concerns information</li><li>Both are court fees</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The governing rules must be analysed separately.</p></details><p><strong>What should happen when a potential opposing client contacts an office?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Screen the conflict before receiving unnecessary confidential details</li><li>Collect everything first</li><li>Publish the request</li><li>Guarantee representation</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Early screening reduces avoidable conflicts.</p></details><p><strong>What does a TIH course certificate establish?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Completion of this TIH educational course</li><li>Admission to the Liberian Bar</li><li>A law degree</li><li>A court appointment</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It is not professional authorisation.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Confidentiality and Privilege.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a fictional conflict-screening checklist and a secure information-handling plan. Explain the boundary between study and authorised practice.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M19:Trial Advocacy, Moot Court and Opinion Writing": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 19</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: TRIAL ADVOCACY, MOOT COURT AND OPINION WRITING</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal Ethics and Trial Advocacy</td><td>Trial Advocacy, Moot Court and Opinion Writing</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain trial advocacy, moot court and opinion writing using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Advocacy presents a supported legal position to a decision-maker. Moot court simulates legal argument for learning. An opinion explains the issues, applicable law, reasoning and result; it is different from a partisan submission.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Submission</td><td>A party supported argument and requested outcome</td></tr><tr><td>Opinion</td><td>Reasoned explanation by the decision-maker</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Build an issue list, record references, authorities and requested relief. In oral argument, answer the question asked before returning to the planned structure. Admit a factual or legal gap rather than inventing an authority. For witness exercises, distinguish open questions from leading assertions and follow applicable rules. A written opinion should address the strongest argument on each side and explain why one analysis prevails.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: counsel argues a land dispute and the bench asks where the seller authority appears in the record. A strong response supplies the exact fictional document reference or acknowledges that it is absent. Reading a prepared speech without answering weakens the argument. In the opinion exercise, state whether the missing authority prevents a final result and identify the lawful next step to research.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read current trial and Supreme Court rules. Use a published Liberian judgment for structure, not as a source of invented facts.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/court-rules/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Court rules — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both submission and opinion. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Advocacy presents a supported legal position to a decision-maker. Moot court simulates legal argument for learning. An opinion explains the issues, applicable law, reasoning and result; it is different from a partisan submission.</li><li>Separate submission from opinion.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Submission</td><td>A party supported argument and requested outcome</td></tr><tr><td>Opinion</td><td>Reasoned explanation by the decision-maker</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Submission</summary><p>A party supported argument and requested outcome</p></details><details><summary>Opinion</summary><p>Reasoned explanation by the decision-maker</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What should an advocate do when the bench asks a direct question?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Answer it accurately before returning to the structure</li><li>Ignore it and read faster</li><li>Invent a paragraph number</li><li>Ask social media to vote</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Responsive, candid argument supports credibility.</p></details><p><strong>How does a judicial opinion differ from a party submission?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It gives the decision-maker reasoned resolution</li><li>It must advocate only for one client</li><li>It replaces all evidence</li><li>It is always oral</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> An opinion explains the adjudicative outcome.</p></details><p><strong>What is proper when a needed authority cannot be verified?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Acknowledge the gap and avoid inventing it</li><li>Create a convincing case name</li><li>Quote a nonexistent paragraph</li><li>Claim a secret source</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Candour is essential to legal work.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Submission and Opinion.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a five-minute moot submission and a 900-word fictional reasoned opinion. Include adverse authority and record references.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M20:Jurisprudence: Legal Positivism and Natural Law": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 20</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: JURISPRUDENCE: LEGAL POSITIVISM AND NATURAL LAW</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jurisprudence and Dispute Resolution</td><td>Jurisprudence: Legal Positivism and Natural Law</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain jurisprudence: legal positivism and natural law using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Jurisprudence studies the nature, authority and interpretation of law. Legal positivist approaches commonly distinguish legal validity from moral merit. Natural-law approaches give moral reasoning a foundational role in understanding law. These broad traditions contain disagreements and should not be reduced to slogans.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal validity</td><td>Whether a norm qualifies as law under the relevant criteria</td></tr><tr><td>Moral merit</td><td>Whether a norm is ethically justified</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Distinguish describing what counts as law from evaluating whether it is just. A person may criticise a law morally while acknowledging its institutional status. Conversely, moral conviction alone does not identify a valid procedure or remedy. In an essay, define the theory, explain its reasoning, present a counterargument and connect it to a specific problem. Do not attribute an official school endorsement to this independent TIH course.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a rule validly adopted on its face treats one community unfairly. Compare institutional-validity questions, constitutional limits and moral criticism. A weak essay says “all law is good” or “any unfair rule may be ignored”. A stronger essay explains how theories differ, checks superior-law limits and separates lawful reform or challenge from personal disagreement.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the published subject list as a study map, then compare theories against a fictional rule and the Liberian constitutional framework.</p><p><a href=\"https://liberiaschooloflaw.com/areas-of-study/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Published subject list — Liberia School of Law (reference only)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both legal validity and moral merit. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Jurisprudence studies the nature, authority and interpretation of law. Legal positivist approaches commonly distinguish legal validity from moral merit. Natural-law approaches give moral reasoning a foundational role in understanding law. These broad traditions contain disagreements and should not be reduced to slogans.</li><li>Separate legal validity from moral merit.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal validity</td><td>Whether a norm qualifies as law under the relevant criteria</td></tr><tr><td>Moral merit</td><td>Whether a norm is ethically justified</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Legal validity</summary><p>Whether a norm qualifies as law under the relevant criteria</p></details><details><summary>Moral merit</summary><p>Whether a norm is ethically justified</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What distinction is central to many positivist accounts?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Legal validity versus moral merit</li><li>Paper versus ink</li><li>Only courts versus offices</li><li>Civil claims versus shipping</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A norm status and ethical evaluation can be distinguished.</p></details><p><strong>What is characteristic of natural-law approaches?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A foundational role for moral reasoning in understanding law</li><li>Complete rejection of ethical questions</li><li>The claim that every rule is a contract</li><li>A system of land surveying</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The tradition examines law in relation to moral principles.</p></details><p><strong>What improves a jurisprudence essay?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Defined concepts, reasons, counterarguments and application</li><li>Only slogans</li><li>Only unsupported names</li><li>Only a guaranteed conclusion</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> An essay should explain and evaluate competing reasoning.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Legal validity and Moral merit.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 900-word comparative jurisprudence essay on the fictional unfair rule, presenting and criticising both approaches.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M20:Negotiation, Mediation and Arbitration": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 20</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: NEGOTIATION, MEDIATION AND ARBITRATION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jurisprudence and Dispute Resolution</td><td>Negotiation, Mediation and Arbitration</td><td>Foundation legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain negotiation, mediation and arbitration using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Negotiation is direct effort to reach agreement. Mediation uses a neutral facilitator to support voluntary agreement. Arbitration involves a tribunal determination under the applicable agreement and law. They are alternatives with different roles, limits and legal effects.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mediation</td><td>Facilitated effort toward voluntary agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Arbitration</td><td>Adjudication through an authorised private tribunal process</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify authority to settle, interests, evidence, alternatives and constraints. A mediator does not automatically impose a binding award. An arbitration clause needs analysis of scope, validity and applicable procedure. Confidentiality cannot be assumed without checking the rules or agreement. Settlement must not conceal coercion or unlawfully override protections. Family violence and serious power imbalances may require a different approach and professional safeguards.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a customer and supplier disagree about defective chairs. Prepare interests, evidence, possible remedies and alternatives before discussing settlement. A mediator helps them explore terms; an arbitrator may decide if lawfully empowered. Write any proposed agreement clearly, including performance and unresolved points. Do not assume signing any document removes all rights or guarantees enforcement.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Chapter 64 on arbitration and identify any applicable agreement and current procedural rules; distinguish mediation as a separate process.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both mediation and arbitration. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Negotiation is direct effort to reach agreement. Mediation uses a neutral facilitator to support voluntary agreement. Arbitration involves a tribunal determination under the applicable agreement and law. They are alternatives with different roles, limits and legal effects.</li><li>Separate mediation from arbitration.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mediation</td><td>Facilitated effort toward voluntary agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Arbitration</td><td>Adjudication through an authorised private tribunal process</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Mediation</summary><p>Facilitated effort toward voluntary agreement</p></details><details><summary>Arbitration</summary><p>Adjudication through an authorised private tribunal process</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Who ordinarily facilitates rather than imposes the parties agreement?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A mediator</li><li>An authorised arbitrator deciding an award</li><li>A judge giving judgment</li><li>A party acting alone</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Mediation centres on voluntary agreement.</p></details><p><strong>What should be checked before relying on an arbitration clause?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Its scope, validity and applicable procedure</li><li>Only its font</li><li>Only the room size</li><li>Only the contract length</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> An arbitration process requires legal authority and relevant coverage.</p></details><p><strong>Why prepare alternatives before negotiation?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They help evaluate proposed settlement terms</li><li>They guarantee a settlement</li><li>They replace all evidence</li><li>They require disclosure of secrets</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A party needs to compare an offer with realistic options.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Mediation and Arbitration.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Conduct a fictional supplier negotiation and write a settlement term sheet plus a comparison of mediation and arbitration.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M21:Liberian Case Brief and Research Portfolio": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 21 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Liberian Case Brief and Research Portfolio</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>Use one published Supreme Court opinion located through the Judiciary. Produce a case brief and a research log. Do not invent a case citation.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>A one-page case brief with the issue, procedural history, holding and reasons</li><li>A five-entry primary-source research log with pinpoint references and status checks</li><li>A 600-word IRAC analysis of a changed fictional fact and one counterargument</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>", "M21:Contracts and Land Due-Diligence Portfolio": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 21 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Contracts and Land Due-Diligence Portfolio</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>A fictional cooperative wishes to lease a rural workspace and buy equipment. Land authority and delivery terms are disputed.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>A land-category and authority checklist with unresolved title questions</li><li>A lease term sheet and equipment-contract formation timeline</li><li>A 700-word remedies memo distinguishing assumptions from verified rules</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>", "M21:Rights, Employment and Evidence Memorandum": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 21 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Rights, Employment and Evidence Memorandum</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>A fictional worker is dismissed following an accusation based on a cropped message. There is a disputed medical record.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>An employment chronology and Decent Work Act applicability analysis</li><li>An evidence table addressing relevance, authenticity and missing context</li><li>A 900-word balanced memorandum identifying rights, procedures and remedies to research</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>", "M21:Moot Court Capstone and Reasoned Opinion": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 21 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Moot Court Capstone and Reasoned Opinion</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>Represent each side in a fictional dispute about a supplier contract, corporate authority and defective performance. Then act as the classroom decision-maker.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>A 1,200-word memorial with issues, primary authorities, record references and requested relief</li><li>Two five-minute oral submissions responding to three challenging bench questions each</li><li>A 900-word reasoned opinion and self-assessment against the rubric</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>", "M22:Liberian Courts, Jurisdiction and Access to Justice": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 22</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: LIBERIAN COURTS, JURISDICTION AND ACCESS TO JUSTICE</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied The Liberian Legal System</td><td>Liberian Courts, Jurisdiction and Access to Justice</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain liberian courts, jurisdiction and access to justice using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Original jurisdiction is authority to hear a matter at first instance; appellate jurisdiction is authority to review a lower decision. Subject-matter, territorial and personal jurisdiction answer different questions. Access to justice also involves practical ability to understand and use a lawful process.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Original jurisdiction</td><td>Power to determine a matter at first instance</td></tr><tr><td>Appellate jurisdiction</td><td>Power to review a lower decision</td></tr><tr><td>Access to justice</td><td>Practical and lawful ability to seek a remedy</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Start a forum analysis with the kind of dispute, parties and remedy. A circuit court, magistrate court and specialised court are not interchangeable merely because they are nearby. The Constitution places judicial power within the judiciary and provides the Supreme Court’s appellate role; legislation determines the competence of subordinate courts.</p><p>Separate an official’s job title from a court’s adjudicative power. A ministry may investigate or administer a statute without being the final judicial forum. A mediator facilitates settlement and does not acquire judicial authority through the parties’ convenience. Map possible routes, then verify the enabling text, procedural entry requirements and available review. Legal merit alone does not cure an incompetent forum. An access plan should identify language, records, transport and lawful assistance without promising a particular result.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a Gbarnga trader brings a disputed equipment contract to a local office because it is closer than a court. The officer suggests asking a chief to decide it. A weak answer treats distance as jurisdiction. A strong answer identifies the contracting parties, requested relief and place of performance; checks the competent court under Liberian law; and distinguishes voluntary mediation from a binding judicial decision. If the transaction has a customary-land component, analyse that component separately instead of assuming one institution can decide everything.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Judiciary Law with constitutional Articles 65 and 66. Locate the jurisdiction provisions for the relevant trial court rather than importing foreign monetary limits.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Organise the answer by subject matter, parties, territory and remedy. Give a sourced forum conclusion and identify any fact that prevents a final jurisdiction determination.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Original jurisdiction is authority to hear a matter at first instance; appellate jurisdiction is authority to review a lower decision. Subject-matter, territorial and personal jurisdiction answer different questions. Access to justice also involves practical ability to understand and use a lawful process.</li><li>Separate original jurisdiction from appellate jurisdiction.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Original jurisdiction</td><td>Power to determine a matter at first instance</td></tr><tr><td>Appellate jurisdiction</td><td>Power to review a lower decision</td></tr><tr><td>Access to justice</td><td>Practical and lawful ability to seek a remedy</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Original jurisdiction</summary><p>Power to determine a matter at first instance</p></details><details><summary>Appellate jurisdiction</summary><p>Power to review a lower decision</p></details><details><summary>Access to justice</summary><p>Practical and lawful ability to seek a remedy</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A nearby office offers to decide a contract claim. What establishes its adjudicative competence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An applicable legal grant of jurisdiction</li><li>Its convenient location alone</li><li>The consent of one claimant alone</li><li>Its role in collecting local taxes</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Convenience and administrative responsibility do not themselves create judicial power.</p></details><p><strong>Which issue differs from whether a claimant has a strong contract case?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether performance was defective</li><li>Whether the selected court has jurisdiction</li><li>Whether delivery was agreed</li><li>Whether payment was made</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Forum competence is independent of the merits of the underlying claim.</p></details><p><strong>What is a sound access-to-justice plan for a learner’s fictional case?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Replace all procedural requirements with a complaint letter</li><li>Ask an unrelated administrator to issue judgment</li><li>Identify lawful assistance, required records and practical barriers</li><li>Promise that court access guarantees victory</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> An access plan removes practical barriers while respecting competent procedures.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Original jurisdiction and Appellate jurisdiction.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Allocate the trader’s issues among three hypothetical institutions and challenge each allocation with its required source.</li><li>Rewrite an inaccessible filing checklist into plain language without changing its legal requirements.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a two-page forum map for the trader. For each possible institution record its legal source, jurisdiction question, entry documents and review route. Add an accessibility plan.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M22:Judicial Precedent, Ratio and Distinguishing Cases": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 22</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: JUDICIAL PRECEDENT, RATIO AND DISTINGUISHING CASES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied The Liberian Legal System</td><td>Judicial Precedent, Ratio and Distinguishing Cases</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain judicial precedent, ratio and distinguishing cases using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The ratio decidendi is reasoning necessary to the decision on its material issues. Obiter observations are comments not necessary to that result. Distinguishing explains why a material difference prevents a prior rule from resolving the present issue in the same way.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ratio decidendi</td><td>Reasoning necessary to decide a material issue</td></tr><tr><td>Obiter dictum</td><td>Observation not necessary to the result</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguishing</td><td>Explaining a material difference limiting an earlier analogy</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>A case is more than its final sentence. First identify what the court had to decide, the facts it treated as material, and the legal proposition connecting those facts to the outcome. Then assess the court’s place in the relevant legal hierarchy and later treatment of the proposition. A sentence quoted out of context may describe a party’s argument rather than the court’s holding.</p><p>Distinguishing requires a legally significant difference, not simply a different county, year or party name. State the old rule, identify the changed fact, and explain why the rule’s reasoning depends on that fact. An analogy does the reverse: explain why the important similarities justify applying the reasoning. A foreign judgment may help develop an argument, but its persuasive value must not be presented as a binding Liberian holding. Never manufacture a local citation to make a classroom example appear authoritative.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional exercise: Published Case A, obtained by the learner, concerns a signed guarantee. The classroom variation contains no guarantee and the supplier dealt only with the company. The weak response repeats the earlier result because both files involve debt. The strong response asks whether personal authorisation was material to the actual decision, accurately states that decision’s reasoning, and tests whether the absence of a guarantee changes the liability analysis. Case A is a placeholder for the real opinion the learner selects, not an invented Liberian case.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate a published Liberian Supreme Court opinion through the Judiciary. Read the material facts, issue, disposition and reasons together; record a verifiable pinpoint citation.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>State the rule in context, connect it to the court’s material facts, and explain the proposed analogy or distinction. Cite the actual opinion rather than a textbook’s unsupported summary.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The ratio decidendi is reasoning necessary to the decision on its material issues. Obiter observations are comments not necessary to that result. Distinguishing explains why a material difference prevents a prior rule from resolving the present issue in the same way.</li><li>Separate ratio decidendi from obiter dictum.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ratio decidendi</td><td>Reasoning necessary to decide a material issue</td></tr><tr><td>Obiter dictum</td><td>Observation not necessary to the result</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguishing</td><td>Explaining a material difference limiting an earlier analogy</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Ratio decidendi</summary><p>Reasoning necessary to decide a material issue</p></details><details><summary>Obiter dictum</summary><p>Observation not necessary to the result</p></details><details><summary>Distinguishing</summary><p>Explaining a material difference limiting an earlier analogy</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A quoted passage merely records counsel’s submission. How should it be used?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Assume it overrules every earlier case</li><li>Identify it as an argument, not automatically the court’s holding</li><li>Present it as binding because it is printed in a judgment</li><li>Treat it as a newly enacted statute</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> The role of a passage within the reasons determines what it actually establishes.</p></details><p><strong>Which difference most plausibly supports distinguishing a guarantee case?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The judgment uses an older font</li><li>The classroom parties have different names</li><li>The new transaction lacks the personal guarantee material to the earlier reasoning</li><li>The new debtor lives in another county</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> A distinction must change a fact relevant to the legal reasoning.</p></details><p><strong>What should accompany an analogy to a foreign judgment?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An invented Liberian case number</li><li>A claim that all common-law courts are interchangeable</li><li>An assertion that English language alone makes it binding</li><li>A clear account of its jurisdiction and persuasive status</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Comparative reasoning must disclose the authority’s actual status.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Ratio decidendi and Obiter dictum.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Mark a public judgment in three colours: facts, party arguments and court reasoning.</li><li>Exchange a case brief with a partner and test whether its claimed ratio is supported by the cited passage.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write an 800-word precedent analysis using one verified Supreme Court opinion. Include one analogy, one proposed distinction and a paragraph identifying what the opinion does not decide.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M23:Predictive Legal Memoranda and Opinion Writing": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 23</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: PREDICTIVE LEGAL MEMORANDA AND OPINION WRITING</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Legal Research and Writing</td><td>Predictive Legal Memoranda and Opinion Writing</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain predictive legal memoranda and opinion writing using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A predictive memorandum evaluates likely legal outcomes for its reader, including weaknesses and uncertainty. An advocacy submission argues a party’s position. Both require accurate authority, but their purpose and tone differ.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Predictive memorandum</td><td>Balanced analysis of likely outcomes and uncertainty</td></tr><tr><td>Advocacy submission</td><td>Reasoned presentation supporting a party’s position</td></tr><tr><td>Material fact</td><td>A fact capable of affecting a legal element or result</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Frame a neutral question that includes the legally material facts. “Was the dismissal unlawful?” is less useful than a question identifying the employment relationship, alleged reason and disputed procedure. A memorandum normally separates question, short answer, relevant facts, governing rules, application and conclusion. Headings should expose the reasoning rather than disguise gaps in it.</p><p>Apply each element or procedural requirement to evidence supporting and opposing the proposed conclusion. A counterargument belongs in the discussion, not in a footnote pretending it does not exist. Distinguish an unknown fact from an uncertain rule: the former calls for factual investigation, the latter for additional authority or candid qualification. A reasoned opinion also explains the route from issue to disposition. Neither document should promise an outcome unsupported by the record. Update the short answer after the analysis is complete.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a Paynesville worker was dismissed after a disputed stock shortage, but the employer’s written notice and the employee’s response have not been obtained. A weak memo guarantees reinstatement after quoting a foreign video. A strong memo identifies the applicable Liberian employment framework, separates proof of shortage from the decision process, examines the worker’s contrary account and concludes conditionally. Its research section identifies which documents and current provisions would change that assessment.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the legal problem-solving guide and a verified Liberian authority relevant to a fictional issue. Keep the memo’s research log beside its draft.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.monash.edu/student-academic-success/excel-at-writing/annotated-assessment-samples/law/law-legal-problem-solving-irac\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal problem solving with IRAC — Monash University</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Use a question tied to material facts, a rule for each issue and a developed application. Conclude with the strongest supported answer and the exact limits of confidence.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A predictive memorandum evaluates likely legal outcomes for its reader, including weaknesses and uncertainty. An advocacy submission argues a party’s position. Both require accurate authority, but their purpose and tone differ.</li><li>Separate predictive memorandum from advocacy submission.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Predictive memorandum</td><td>Balanced analysis of likely outcomes and uncertainty</td></tr><tr><td>Advocacy submission</td><td>Reasoned presentation supporting a party’s position</td></tr><tr><td>Material fact</td><td>A fact capable of affecting a legal element or result</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Predictive memorandum</summary><p>Balanced analysis of likely outcomes and uncertainty</p></details><details><summary>Advocacy submission</summary><p>Reasoned presentation supporting a party’s position</p></details><details><summary>Material fact</summary><p>A fact capable of affecting a legal element or result</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What distinguishes a predictive memo from a one-sided advocacy draft?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It avoids primary authority</li><li>It must support whichever party pays the writer</li><li>It evaluates relevant weaknesses and competing outcomes candidly</li><li>It omits all conclusions</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Predictive advice requires balanced assessment, even when an adverse argument is strong.</p></details><p><strong>The notice of dismissal is missing. How should the memo treat the asserted reason?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Assume the worker’s summary is a final court finding</li><li>Invent a notice matching the desired result</li><li>Replace the notice with a foreign policy template</li><li>Identify it as an evidential gap requiring the actual record</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> The analysis should disclose the difference between available evidence and an assumption.</p></details><p><strong>When is the best time to finalise the memo’s short answer?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>After the substantive analysis has tested the evidence and rules</li><li>Before reading any relevant authority</li><li>Once a desired outcome is chosen</li><li>After deleting all counterarguments</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The short answer should accurately reflect the completed reasoning.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Predictive memorandum and Advocacy submission.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Convert a partisan complaint into a neutral memo question and fact statement.</li><li>Peer-review a memo paragraph by identifying its rule, application, counterargument and unsupported assumption.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,000-word predictive memo for the dismissal scenario with a neutral question, brief answer, issue headings, counterargument and a research-and-evidence gaps table.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M23:Client Interviewing, Case Planning and File Management": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 23</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CLIENT INTERVIEWING, CASE PLANNING AND FILE MANAGEMENT</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Legal Research and Writing</td><td>Client Interviewing, Case Planning and File Management</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain client interviewing, case planning and file management using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A legal interview gathers a reliable account, clarifies the person’s objective and identifies records, deadlines and conflicts. Case planning turns that account into an issues-and-actions file. Confidentiality is an ethical duty; legal privilege is a separate rule requiring its own conditions.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Open question</td><td>Question inviting an account in the speaker’s own words</td></tr><tr><td>Chronology</td><td>Dated sequence with identified sources</td></tr><tr><td>Confidentiality</td><td>Duty to protect information, distinct from evidential privilege</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Begin a simulation by explaining the learner’s role and limitations. Check who seeks assistance, who else is involved and whether conflicting interests require referral before collecting unnecessary sensitive details. Use open questions for the initial account, focused questions for dates and documents, and a summary to test understanding. Avoid suggesting the answer through a leading question.</p><p>Separate what the speaker saw, what another person reported and what the speaker inferred. Build a chronology with the source for each entry. A file should include an issues list, document index, research tasks and a calendar based on verified local requirements; a foreign video cannot establish a Liberian filing deadline. Keep originals intact, note copies and versions, restrict access and record lawful consent for any recording. A plan is useful only if it assigns a next action to the information gap it is meant to resolve.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a Buchanan tenant says “the owner cheated me last month” and produces a cropped receipt. The weak interviewer accepts the accusation and drafts a claim immediately. The strong interviewer asks when payment was made, who received it, what was agreed, whether there is a complete receipt and what outcome is sought. The file labels the alleged conduct as an allegation, preserves the cropped image and seeks the original. The learner explains that a TIH course does not authorise private legal practice.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Liberian professional ethics code and court rules relevant to record handling. This lesson uses a simulated interview, not unsupervised legal representation.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/code-of-moral-and-professional-ethic/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Code of Moral and Professional Ethics — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Turn the facts into an issues-and-evidence plan. Explain why each missing document or question matters, and keep professional-authorisation limits distinct from interviewing skills.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A legal interview gathers a reliable account, clarifies the person’s objective and identifies records, deadlines and conflicts. Case planning turns that account into an issues-and-actions file. Confidentiality is an ethical duty; legal privilege is a separate rule requiring its own conditions.</li><li>Separate open question from chronology.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Open question</td><td>Question inviting an account in the speaker’s own words</td></tr><tr><td>Chronology</td><td>Dated sequence with identified sources</td></tr><tr><td>Confidentiality</td><td>Duty to protect information, distinct from evidential privilege</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Open question</summary><p>Question inviting an account in the speaker’s own words</p></details><details><summary>Chronology</summary><p>Dated sequence with identified sources</p></details><details><summary>Confidentiality</summary><p>Duty to protect information, distinct from evidential privilege</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which initial question best supports an unprompted account?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>“The landlord lied, did he not?”</li><li>“You want me to guarantee compensation, correct?”</li><li>“Why did the other party commit fraud?”</li><li>“Please describe what happened in your own words.”</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> An open question gathers the speaker’s account without presupposing wrongdoing.</p></details><p><strong>A speaker repeats what a neighbour told them. How should the file record it?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>As a reported account with the neighbour identified as its source</li><li>As the speaker’s direct observation</li><li>As a judicial finding</li><li>As verified documentary proof</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Source attribution helps distinguish knowledge from inference or hearsay.</p></details><p><strong>What is the sound response to a cropped receipt?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Replace it with a newly written receipt</li><li>Preserve it and seek the complete original and surrounding records</li><li>Edit the crop to hide its limitations</li><li>Assume it proves every contract term</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Integrity and completeness must be investigated without altering the available evidence.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Open question and Chronology.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Practise open, focused and confirming questions using an entirely fictional tenant file.</li><li>Create a document index that distinguishes originals, copies, edited files and missing records.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Conduct a 15-minute fictional interview. Produce a chronology, a document index, a conflict-check note and a one-page action plan with five targeted factual questions.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M24:Judicial Review and Constitutional Remedies": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 24</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: JUDICIAL REVIEW AND CONSTITUTIONAL REMEDIES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Constitutional Law</td><td>Judicial Review and Constitutional Remedies</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain judicial review and constitutional remedies using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Judicial review examines a public act against the governing legal limits. A merits appeal may ask a reviewing body to reconsider a result under a particular statutory scheme. A constitutional remedy responds to an established constitutional problem through a competent process.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legality review</td><td>Examination of power and legal limits</td></tr><tr><td>Merits review</td><td>Reconsideration of substance under an available review scheme</td></tr><tr><td>Remedy</td><td>Legally authorised response to an established wrong</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the challenged act, its maker and its claimed source of power. Test statutory competence, procedural duties and applicable rights separately. A decision can involve a lawful objective yet be made by the wrong official or through a defective procedure. Equally, disagreement with a policy does not itself establish a constitutional violation.</p><p>A remedy must match the defect and the court’s powers. Setting aside an act, requiring a lawful hearing, restraining threatened action and compensating a loss are different requests with different foundations. Consider who is entitled to bring the challenge, what record supports it and whether a specialised review route applies. Do not copy foreign writ names or time limits into a Liberian answer without local authority. Explain what the proposed order would do and what it would leave to the responsible institution.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a licensing office cancels a Monrovia clinic’s permission after an inspection but refuses to identify the legal power or disclose the relied-on findings. A weak answer declares that all inspections are unconstitutional. A strong answer investigates the enabling law, the cancellation procedure and the affected interests. It distinguishes the authority to inspect from the authority to cancel and proposes a procedurally appropriate challenge while reserving any merits conclusion until the inspection record is available.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read constitutional Articles 2, 20, 65 and 66 alongside the Judiciary Law and applicable court rules. Identify the appropriate local procedure and remedy for a challenged public act.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Divide the answer into power, procedure, rights and remedy. Connect the requested order to the identified defect and acknowledge any unresolved route or standing question.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Judicial review examines a public act against the governing legal limits. A merits appeal may ask a reviewing body to reconsider a result under a particular statutory scheme. A constitutional remedy responds to an established constitutional problem through a competent process.</li><li>Separate legality review from merits review.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legality review</td><td>Examination of power and legal limits</td></tr><tr><td>Merits review</td><td>Reconsideration of substance under an available review scheme</td></tr><tr><td>Remedy</td><td>Legally authorised response to an established wrong</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Legality review</summary><p>Examination of power and legal limits</p></details><details><summary>Merits review</summary><p>Reconsideration of substance under an available review scheme</p></details><details><summary>Remedy</summary><p>Legally authorised response to an established wrong</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which question is central to a legality challenge?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether the decision-maker acted within its lawful power</li><li>Whether the learner prefers another policy</li><li>Whether all inspections are unpopular</li><li>Whether the office has a modern building</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Judicial review requires a legal defect, not mere policy disagreement.</p></details><p><strong>Why separate inspection authority from cancellation authority?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Cancellation is never reviewable</li><li>Different acts may require different legal powers and procedures</li><li>One official always has every related power</li><li>Inspection automatically proves every alleged breach</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> An enabling statute must support the particular act being challenged.</p></details><p><strong>A requested order would require a new lawful hearing. What must be distinguished?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A hearing from automatic compensation</li><li>A hearing from abolishing the regulator</li><li>Restoring a process from guaranteeing a favourable substantive result</li><li>A hearing from a source of legislation</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Procedural relief need not settle the eventual merits.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Legality review and Merits review.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Sort the clinic’s allegations into jurisdiction, procedure, evidence and policy disagreements.</li><li>Draft two alternative remedies and explain the legal and practical difference between them.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 900-word review memorandum and a draft requested-relief section for the clinic, citing verified local sources and listing unresolved procedural questions.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M24:Constitutional Amendment and Democratic Participation": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 24</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CONSTITUTIONAL AMENDMENT AND DEMOCRATIC PARTICIPATION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Constitutional Law</td><td>Constitutional Amendment and Democratic Participation</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain constitutional amendment and democratic participation using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>An ordinary Act operates within the Constitution; an amendment changes the constitutional framework through its prescribed process. Democratic participation includes lawful expression, association, representation and scrutiny of public decisions.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ordinary legislation</td><td>An Act made within the constitutional framework</td></tr><tr><td>Constitutional amendment</td><td>Change adopted through prescribed constitutional procedures</td></tr><tr><td>Participation</td><td>Lawful involvement in public deliberation and institutional processes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Separate the power to propose change from the steps needed to make it legally effective. A speech, petition, legislative proposal and completed constitutional amendment have different status. Identify the constitutional actor responsible for each stage, the formal requirements and the point at which a proposed change would take effect. Popular support cannot by itself replace a prescribed legal procedure.</p><p>Participation also requires distinguishing political disagreement from a supported legal complaint. Explain which right, institution and process are involved. A restriction on an assembly or publication must be analysed against the relevant constitutional text and lawful procedures, not merely against the preferences of its critics. In an election problem, keep voter eligibility, candidate eligibility, administration, results and a legal challenge separate. Check the current governing instruments instead of treating campaign statements as law.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: an organisation in Kakata gathers signatures supporting a change to a constitutional rule, and a speaker announces that the Constitution has already changed. The weak answer accepts the announcement as an amendment. The strong answer identifies the signatures as one form of participation, reads the amendment provisions, determines which formal steps remain, and explains why the existing constitutional text continues to govern until lawful change occurs. It does not invent a referendum result or a numeric threshold.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Articles 91–93 on amendment with relevant provisions on legislative authority, expression and association. Verify current electoral legislation and official notices before discussing an actual election.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Distinguish proposal, lawful process and effective change. Cite the Liberian text for each required step; use comparative video examples only to illuminate the distinction.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>An ordinary Act operates within the Constitution; an amendment changes the constitutional framework through its prescribed process. Democratic participation includes lawful expression, association, representation and scrutiny of public decisions.</li><li>Separate ordinary legislation from constitutional amendment.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ordinary legislation</td><td>An Act made within the constitutional framework</td></tr><tr><td>Constitutional amendment</td><td>Change adopted through prescribed constitutional procedures</td></tr><tr><td>Participation</td><td>Lawful involvement in public deliberation and institutional processes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Ordinary legislation</summary><p>An Act made within the constitutional framework</p></details><details><summary>Constitutional amendment</summary><p>Change adopted through prescribed constitutional procedures</p></details><details><summary>Participation</summary><p>Lawful involvement in public deliberation and institutional processes</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why does strong public support not itself complete an amendment?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A petition automatically repeals the Constitution</li><li>The prescribed constitutional procedure must still be satisfied</li><li>Public participation is always unlawful</li><li>An ordinary official can waive every formal step</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Legitimacy of support and legal effectiveness of amendment are distinct questions.</p></details><p><strong>Which document is the starting point for an amendment-process analysis?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A foreign referendum manual alone</li><li>A summary of social-media reactions</li><li>The constitutional amendment provisions</li><li>A campaign poster alone</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> The Constitution defines the required institutional route.</p></details><p><strong>An election problem combines registration and a results challenge. What improves the analysis?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Treat every disagreement as one identical claim</li><li>Assume candidate eligibility proves the result</li><li>Replace official records with speeches</li><li>Separate the legal issues and governing processes for each stage</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Different election stages can involve different duties, records and remedies.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Ordinary legislation and Constitutional amendment.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Place a petition, bill, referendum proposal and effective amendment on a status timeline.</li><li>Role-play a civic-information session distinguishing a political argument from a legal claim.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a sourced amendment-process diagram and a 700-word public explainer correcting the speaker’s announcement without discouraging lawful participation.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M25:Interlocutory Applications, Injunctions and Case Management": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 25</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: INTERLOCUTORY APPLICATIONS, INJUNCTIONS AND CASE MANAGEMENT</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Civil Procedure</td><td>Interlocutory Applications, Injunctions and Case Management</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain interlocutory applications, injunctions and case management using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>An interlocutory application seeks an order during a proceeding rather than its final determination. An injunction can restrain or require conduct under the governing law. Case management organises the steps necessary for a fair and efficient decision.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interim relief</td><td>Protection during proceedings under the applicable test</td></tr><tr><td>Final relief</td><td>Remedy following determination of the substantive claim</td></tr><tr><td>Case management</td><td>Organisation of procedural steps and evidence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Begin with the purpose of the proposed order: preserve property, protect an evidential record, prevent a threatened wrong or organise disclosure. Identify the court’s power and the elements of the local test. Evidence of urgency is different from an assertion that the applicant will eventually win. Address the effect on the respondent and any safeguards the law requires.</p><p>An interim order should be sufficiently specific to comply with and supervise. “Do justice” is not an operative command. Distinguish a temporary protective measure from a final remedy after proof. Explain notice, supporting evidence and the hearing process from current Liberian sources; an emergency does not erase the need to investigate lawful safeguards. A timetable should connect issues to pleadings, records, witnesses and hearing dates rather than simply postpone unresolved questions.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: two parties dispute machinery in a rented workshop, and one plans to move it before the dispute is heard. A weak application asks the court to award permanent ownership immediately because removal sounds unfair. A strong application specifies what property and conduct are at risk, supplies the available evidence, identifies the lawful interim test and requests a defined protective order while leaving disputed title for proper determination.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate provisional-relief and motion provisions in the Civil Procedure Law and relevant court rules. Verify the applicable test, notice requirements and authority for the precise application.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Identify the interim test, apply each requirement to evidence, and draft a precise order. Explain why the requested measure does not prematurely decide a contested merits issue.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>An interlocutory application seeks an order during a proceeding rather than its final determination. An injunction can restrain or require conduct under the governing law. Case management organises the steps necessary for a fair and efficient decision.</li><li>Separate interim relief from final relief.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interim relief</td><td>Protection during proceedings under the applicable test</td></tr><tr><td>Final relief</td><td>Remedy following determination of the substantive claim</td></tr><tr><td>Case management</td><td>Organisation of procedural steps and evidence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Interim relief</summary><p>Protection during proceedings under the applicable test</p></details><details><summary>Final relief</summary><p>Remedy following determination of the substantive claim</p></details><details><summary>Case management</summary><p>Organisation of procedural steps and evidence</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What distinguishes urgency from proof of the underlying claim?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Urgency removes all jurisdiction questions</li><li>Urgency creates a final judgment</li><li>Urgency concerns immediate risk; merits require their own supported determination</li><li>Urgency automatically proves ownership</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Interim protection and final entitlement are separate analytical questions.</p></details><p><strong>Which proposed injunction is easier to assess and supervise?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A request that everyone act fairly</li><li>An order transferring all disputed assets without analysis</li><li>An order copied from an unrelated foreign form</li><li>An order defining the identified machinery and restrained removal conduct</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Specific relief exposes its scope, legal basis and practical consequences.</p></details><p><strong>Why address the respondent’s position in an interim application?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The applicable test and safeguards require assessment of competing interests</li><li>The applicant must abandon every claim</li><li>The respondent decides jurisdiction</li><li>An interim order is always a voluntary contract</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A balanced application considers both risk and lawful procedural protection.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Interim relief and Final relief.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Convert three vague requests into specific proposed interim orders.</li><li>Build a case timetable linking each step to a disputed issue and its necessary evidence.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a fictional interim-relief outline with the requested order, legal test, evidence table, respondent’s counterargument and proposed procedural safeguards.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M25:Judgment Enforcement, Execution and Appeal Records": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 25</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: JUDGMENT ENFORCEMENT, EXECUTION AND APPEAL RECORDS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Civil Procedure</td><td>Judgment Enforcement, Execution and Appeal Records</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain judgment enforcement, execution and appeal records using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A judgment determines legal rights or obligations; execution is the lawful process for enforcing it. An appeal record documents the matters required for review. A stay suspends specified action under its legal terms.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Execution</td><td>Lawful enforcement of a judgment</td></tr><tr><td>Stay</td><td>Legally effective suspension within its stated scope</td></tr><tr><td>Appeal record</td><td>Documents and matters required for review</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Read the operative part of the judgment before planning enforcement. Identify the obligated party, precise relief, conditions and relevant procedural status. A monetary judgment, possession order and direction to perform an act call for different enforcement analysis. Information about assets does not itself authorise their seizure. Third-party ownership and applicable exemptions or protections must be assessed.</p><p>Keep an appeal’s existence separate from the effect of a valid stay or other order. Locate current requirements for initiating review and preparing the record, and identify the exact alleged errors. Enforcement through a competent court is different from taking property privately. Preserve the judgment, service records, payment history and relevant subsequent orders; missing documents can affect both execution and review. Never import a foreign bailiff’s powers or a filing deadline without Liberian authority.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a supplier wins a payment judgment against a company and asks friends to seize its director’s private motorbike. The company says it has “appealed”, but provides no filing or stay record. A weak answer approves seizure because a debt exists. A strong answer checks the judgment debtor, lawful execution route, ownership of the proposed asset and actual appellate status. It distinguishes a company debt from a director’s personal liability and asks for the relevant orders before reaching a conclusion.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Civil Procedure Law and court rules governing judgments, enforcement and appeals. Do not assume that filing an appeal automatically produces a stay.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Read the operative judgment, identify the debtor and remedy, then analyse execution and review separately. State which filing, ownership or order remains unverified.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A judgment determines legal rights or obligations; execution is the lawful process for enforcing it. An appeal record documents the matters required for review. A stay suspends specified action under its legal terms.</li><li>Separate execution from stay.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Execution</td><td>Lawful enforcement of a judgment</td></tr><tr><td>Stay</td><td>Legally effective suspension within its stated scope</td></tr><tr><td>Appeal record</td><td>Documents and matters required for review</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Execution</summary><p>Lawful enforcement of a judgment</p></details><details><summary>Stay</summary><p>Legally effective suspension within its stated scope</p></details><details><summary>Appeal record</summary><p>Documents and matters required for review</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A judgment names the company as debtor. What must be checked before targeting a director’s property?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The director’s association with the company alone</li><li>Whether the motorbike is easy to find</li><li>Whether the supplier dislikes the director</li><li>A legal basis for reaching that asset and the director’s liability</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> The identity of the judgment debtor and asset ownership matter to execution.</p></details><p><strong>What does an assertion that an appeal was filed establish by itself?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A claim requiring verification of the filing and any effective stay</li><li>Automatic suspension of every enforcement step</li><li>Automatic reversal of the judgment</li><li>Personal liability of every shareholder</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Filing status and its legal consequences must be proved from the actual procedure and orders.</p></details><p><strong>Why preserve a payment history after judgment?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It replaces the appeal record</li><li>It helps establish the remaining enforceable obligation</li><li>It changes the judgment into legislation</li><li>It authorises private seizure</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Execution should address the obligation still outstanding rather than an unsupported original figure.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Execution and Stay.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Annotate a fictional judgment to distinguish reasons from operative relief.</li><li>Audit a mock enforcement file for a missing ownership record, payment and subsequent order.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare an enforcement-and-review checklist for the supplier, including asset ownership, operative relief, orders, service records and disputed legal grounds.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M26:Contract Terms, Interpretation and Exclusion Clauses": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 26</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CONTRACT TERMS, INTERPRETATION AND EXCLUSION CLAUSES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Contracts</td><td>Contract Terms, Interpretation and Exclusion Clauses</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain contract terms, interpretation and exclusion clauses using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>An express term is stated by the parties; an implied term arises through an applicable legal rule or recognised basis. Incorporation asks whether a term forms part of the agreement. Interpretation asks what an established term means.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Incorporation</td><td>Whether wording became part of the agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Interpretation</td><td>Meaning of an established contractual term</td></tr><tr><td>Exclusion clause</td><td>Wording limiting responsibility subject to applicable law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the agreement’s documents and chronology before reading one clause in isolation. A limitation printed after acceptance may raise an incorporation question before any question about its meaning. Distinguish a promise, representation, condition and allocation of risk using the governing legal framework. Read definitions, cross-references and the contract as a whole.</p><p>An exclusion clause does not automatically answer every claim. Establish whether it became part of the contract, what conduct or loss it covers and whether applicable law limits its effect. “The seller is not liable” is different from a defined limitation tied to a particular event and remedy. An ambiguity calls for a reasoned interpretation supported by the transaction and legal rules, not a rewrite creating whichever term a party now wants. Keep interpretive uncertainty separate from a missing agreement document.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a Robertsport buyer agrees an equipment order by email. After payment, an invoice states “all liability excluded”, and the seller relies on it when the equipment fails. A weak answer assumes invoice wording controls because it looks official. A strong answer reconstructs formation, asks when and how the wording was communicated and accepted, examines its scope and any mandatory local rules, and distinguishes incorporation from interpretation and enforceability.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the Liberian Commercial Code for a transaction within its scope and verify local authority relevant to interpretation and incorporated terms. Do not treat foreign consumer legislation as Liberian law.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.moci.gov.lr/sites/default/files/documents/Commercial%20Code%20-%20Title%207%20-%20Liberian%20Code%20of%20Laws%20Revised.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian Commercial Code 2010, Title 7 — Ministry of Commerce and Industry (check amendments)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Answer incorporation, interpretation and legal effect in that order. Apply the actual wording to the alleged breach instead of assuming all exclusions have the same scope.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>An express term is stated by the parties; an implied term arises through an applicable legal rule or recognised basis. Incorporation asks whether a term forms part of the agreement. Interpretation asks what an established term means.</li><li>Separate incorporation from interpretation.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Incorporation</td><td>Whether wording became part of the agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Interpretation</td><td>Meaning of an established contractual term</td></tr><tr><td>Exclusion clause</td><td>Wording limiting responsibility subject to applicable law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Incorporation</summary><p>Whether wording became part of the agreement</p></details><details><summary>Interpretation</summary><p>Meaning of an established contractual term</p></details><details><summary>Exclusion clause</summary><p>Wording limiting responsibility subject to applicable law</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A limitation first appears on an invoice after acceptance. Which issue arises before its meaning?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether it was incorporated into the agreement</li><li>Whether every invoice changes the law</li><li>Whether all seller liability disappears</li><li>Whether the buyer’s complaint is criminal</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A term must form part of the contract before its scope can determine obligations.</p></details><p><strong>What is the strongest method of interpreting a disputed clause?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Replace missing words with a foreign statute automatically</li><li>Read its wording, definitions, context and applicable legal rules together</li><li>Use the clause title alone</li><li>Choose whichever meaning benefits the author</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Interpretation is a reasoned examination of an established agreement.</p></details><p><strong>An exclusion mentions delay but the claim concerns defective goods. What must be analysed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether delay and defects are identical</li><li>Whether the invoice paper is signed in blue</li><li>Whether the wording actually covers that type of breach and loss</li><li>Whether any exclusion covers every possible claim</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> The clause’s scope must be connected to the specific alleged wrong.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Incorporation and Interpretation.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Sort a fictional email chain into formation, later administration and proposed variation.</li><li>Redraft an ambiguous limitation and identify which enforceability questions drafting alone cannot settle.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write an 800-word clause analysis and draft an alternative term that clearly identifies delivery, inspection, responsibility and available remedies without claiming guaranteed enforceability.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M26:Mistake, Misrepresentation, Duress and Undue Influence": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 26</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: MISTAKE, MISREPRESENTATION, DURESS AND UNDUE INFLUENCE</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Contracts</td><td>Mistake, Misrepresentation, Duress and Undue Influence</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain mistake, misrepresentation, duress and undue influence using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Mistake concerns a relevant erroneous belief; misrepresentation concerns a legally significant false statement inducing agreement. Duress involves improper pressure under the applicable test, while undue influence concerns abuse of an influential relationship.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Misrepresentation</td><td>Legally significant false statement inducing agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Duress</td><td>Improper pressure assessed under the governing test</td></tr><tr><td>Rescission</td><td>Remedy seeking to unwind a transaction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Begin with the asserted defect and facts proving it. Not every inaccurate prediction is a false statement of existing fact, and not every hard bargain is legally improper pressure. Identify the statement or conduct, its maker, the recipient’s knowledge, its effect on agreement and the applicable legal standard. Separate a party’s later disappointment from a defect present in formation.</p><p>The legal consequence depends on the recognised category and remedy. Rescission aims to unwind a transaction; damages address a legally recoverable loss. They must not be treated as interchangeable or automatic. Consider timing, affirmation, third-party interests and practical restoration under the governing rules. Distinguish honest disagreement about quality from an evidence-supported allegation of deception. Describe unproved fraud as an allegation, not an established criminal finding.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a seller states that a workshop generator has recently been rebuilt, while an available repair record suggests no rebuild. The buyer also alleges pressure because delivery was urgently needed. A weak answer calls every urgent contract void. A strong answer identifies the factual representation, checks its accuracy and material effect, distinguishes commercial urgency from the applicable duress test, and evaluates possible remedies without promising either rescission or a fixed sum.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Research applicable Liberian rules and judgments on consent defects and remedies. The comparative video explains categories rather than supplying a local outcome.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.moci.gov.lr/sites/default/files/documents/Commercial%20Code%20-%20Title%207%20-%20Liberian%20Code%20of%20Laws%20Revised.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian Commercial Code 2010, Title 7 — Ministry of Commerce and Industry (check amendments)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Avoid a generic “unfair contract” conclusion. Identify the particular consent defect, test its elements and explain the conditions and limitations of the requested remedy.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Mistake concerns a relevant erroneous belief; misrepresentation concerns a legally significant false statement inducing agreement. Duress involves improper pressure under the applicable test, while undue influence concerns abuse of an influential relationship.</li><li>Separate misrepresentation from duress.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Misrepresentation</td><td>Legally significant false statement inducing agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Duress</td><td>Improper pressure assessed under the governing test</td></tr><tr><td>Rescission</td><td>Remedy seeking to unwind a transaction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Misrepresentation</summary><p>Legally significant false statement inducing agreement</p></details><details><summary>Duress</summary><p>Improper pressure assessed under the governing test</p></details><details><summary>Rescission</summary><p>Remedy seeking to unwind a transaction</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What most directly supports investigating misrepresentation in the generator sale?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The fact that the seller made a profit</li><li>Evidence about the rebuild statement, its accuracy and reliance</li><li>The buyer’s disappointment alone</li><li>Any urgent need for delivery</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> A misrepresentation analysis requires a relevant statement and its effect, not mere dissatisfaction.</p></details><p><strong>Why is commercial urgency not automatically duress?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Only profitable contracts involve pressure</li><li>All hurried agreements are unenforceable</li><li>The governing test requires analysis of the nature and impropriety of pressure</li><li>Urgency proves every element of fraud</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Practical need and legally improper pressure are distinct concepts.</p></details><p><strong>What distinguishes rescission from damages?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Both always produce the same monetary award</li><li>Rescission is an automatic criminal conviction</li><li>Damages erase all third-party rights</li><li>Unwinding a transaction differs from compensation for recoverable loss</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Different remedies require their own legal and factual foundations.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Misrepresentation and Duress.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Separate hard bargaining, a factual representation and an alleged threat in three fictional negotiations.</li><li>Argue rescission and compensation separately, identifying the evidence each proposed remedy needs.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Produce a consent-defects matrix for the generator sale, with one column each for elements, supporting facts, contrary facts, missing evidence and possible remedies.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M27:Vicarious Liability, Occupiers and Nuisance": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 27</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: VICARIOUS LIABILITY, OCCUPIERS AND NUISANCE</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Torts</td><td>Vicarious Liability, Occupiers and Nuisance</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain vicarious liability, occupiers and nuisance using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Direct liability concerns a person’s own legally wrongful conduct. Vicarious liability concerns responsibility for another’s conduct under a recognised relationship and connection test. Occupier and nuisance questions concern control of premises or interference with another’s legally protected use.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct liability</td><td>Responsibility for one’s own legally wrongful conduct</td></tr><tr><td>Vicarious liability</td><td>Responsibility for another’s conduct under a recognised legal test</td></tr><tr><td>Control of premises</td><td>Relevant authority over a place or risk, distinct from bare ownership</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Keep relationship, conduct and connection separate. Proof that someone works for a business does not alone determine whether the business is responsible for every act they perform. Identify the worker’s actual role, the assigned activity and the conduct causing the alleged harm, then research the governing connection test. A principal’s own negligent instructions may raise direct liability independently.</p><p>Premises cases require attention to who controlled the relevant area, who encountered the risk and what precaution the applicable duty demanded. Nuisance analysis asks about the character and legal significance of an interference rather than treating every inconvenience as actionable. The claimant must connect the defendant to the relevant conduct and prove the legally required harm. Avoid collapsing a landowner, occupier, contractor and employee into one undifferentiated “responsible person”.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a delivery driver damages a neighbour’s gate while using a company truck. The driver claims to have been delivering goods; the business says the trip was entirely personal. Separately, runoff from the warehouse repeatedly enters the neighbour’s yard. A weak answer makes the owner automatically liable for all losses. A strong answer investigates the trip’s purpose and authority, identifies who controlled the drainage, analyses the separate legal bases and avoids recovering the same loss twice.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Find Liberian judgments relevant to civil responsibility and the particular claim. Use procedural and evidence provisions to organise proof; a foreign tort test must be checked against local authority.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Name each proposed defendant and legal basis. Link control, relationship and conduct to evidence before considering causation, defences and recoverable loss.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Direct liability concerns a person’s own legally wrongful conduct. Vicarious liability concerns responsibility for another’s conduct under a recognised relationship and connection test. Occupier and nuisance questions concern control of premises or interference with another’s legally protected use.</li><li>Separate direct liability from vicarious liability.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct liability</td><td>Responsibility for one’s own legally wrongful conduct</td></tr><tr><td>Vicarious liability</td><td>Responsibility for another’s conduct under a recognised legal test</td></tr><tr><td>Control of premises</td><td>Relevant authority over a place or risk, distinct from bare ownership</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Direct liability</summary><p>Responsibility for one’s own legally wrongful conduct</p></details><details><summary>Vicarious liability</summary><p>Responsibility for another’s conduct under a recognised legal test</p></details><details><summary>Control of premises</summary><p>Relevant authority over a place or risk, distinct from bare ownership</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What is essential beyond proving that a driver is employed by a business?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The fact that the claimant prefers to sue the business</li><li>A rule that employers answer for every private act</li><li>The governing relationship and connection between the wrongful act and the work</li><li>The company’s logo on any vehicle alone</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Vicarious responsibility requires the legally relevant connection, not employment status alone.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish warehouse ownership from control of the drainage system?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Only owners can ever be liable</li><li>Only tenants can ever be liable</li><li>Control is never relevant to a civil claim</li><li>The person legally responsible for the relevant risk must be identified</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Different roles may carry different duties and require separate evidence.</p></details><p><strong>Which fact most directly tests the business’s personal-trip argument?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The delivery assignment, route and purpose of the journey</li><li>The driver’s favourite vehicle</li><li>The neighbour’s opinion of the company</li><li>The truck’s purchase price alone</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The factual connection to the business activity is central to the responsibility analysis.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Direct liability and Vicarious liability.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Draw a roles map distinguishing owner, occupier, contractor and driver.</li><li>Argue both versions of the truck journey using only the facts supported by the fictional delivery record.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create a responsibility-and-evidence matrix for the truck and drainage incidents. Write a 900-word analysis separating direct, vicarious and interference claims.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M27:Defamation, Privacy and Civil Damages": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 27</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: DEFAMATION, PRIVACY AND CIVIL DAMAGES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Torts</td><td>Defamation, Privacy and Civil Damages</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain defamation, privacy and civil damages using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Defamation concerns legally actionable injury to reputation through a communicated statement. Privacy concerns protection of personal information or interests under applicable law. Damages compensate legally recoverable loss and require a reasoned legal and evidential basis.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Defamation</td><td>Legally actionable injury to reputation through publication</td></tr><tr><td>Privacy</td><td>Protection of personal information or interests under applicable law</td></tr><tr><td>Loss schedule</td><td>Structured account of claimed harm and supporting evidence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the exact words or image, who was referred to, who received the publication and what meaning is alleged. Distinguish a factual assertion from opinion, an allegation from a finding, and publication to others from a private statement. Then research the elements and available defences in the governing Liberian framework. Criticism is not automatically unlawful merely because it is embarrassing.</p><p>Truth, protected occasions and other recognised defences require their own factual analysis; a disclaimer does not cure every publication. A privacy claim may address disclosure even where reputational falsity is absent, so it must not be treated as a duplicate defamation claim. For damages, separate proved financial loss, legally recognised non-financial harm and unsupported speculation. Preserve the original publication, date, audience evidence, responses and any correction. A larger demand is not stronger proof.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a local page names a tailor as a thief after hearing a disputed customer complaint. It also publishes the tailor’s private medical document. A weak answer treats both acts as one proven crime. A strong civil analysis separates the words and their publication from the private document’s disclosure, investigates truth and applicable protection, and builds a supported loss schedule. It identifies which constitutional and civil questions need current local authority.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the expression provisions of the Constitution and locate current Liberian authority on the specific civil claim. Verify any statutory change relevant to media or publication.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Separate each publication, cause of action and proposed defence. Explain how the record supports each element and avoid treating embarrassment as automatic proof of every claimed loss.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Defamation concerns legally actionable injury to reputation through a communicated statement. Privacy concerns protection of personal information or interests under applicable law. Damages compensate legally recoverable loss and require a reasoned legal and evidential basis.</li><li>Separate defamation from privacy.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Defamation</td><td>Legally actionable injury to reputation through publication</td></tr><tr><td>Privacy</td><td>Protection of personal information or interests under applicable law</td></tr><tr><td>Loss schedule</td><td>Structured account of claimed harm and supporting evidence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Defamation</summary><p>Legally actionable injury to reputation through publication</p></details><details><summary>Privacy</summary><p>Protection of personal information or interests under applicable law</p></details><details><summary>Loss schedule</summary><p>Structured account of claimed harm and supporting evidence</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why record the precise published words in a defamation analysis?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A general description always proves every element</li><li>All criticism has identical legal effect</li><li>The claimant’s chosen damages amount determines the meaning</li><li>Meaning, identification and defences depend on what was actually communicated</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> The actual publication is the starting point for analysing the alleged wrong.</p></details><p><strong>A private medical document is disclosed accurately. What issue may remain?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A distinct privacy or confidentiality question under applicable law</li><li>Falsity is automatically established</li><li>Accurate information can never involve legal protection</li><li>Every disclosure is a court order</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Accuracy and lawful handling of private information are separate matters.</p></details><p><strong>What improves a claim for business loss after publication?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The number of exclamation marks in the complaint</li><li>Records connecting the publication to legally recoverable financial harm</li><li>An unsupported round figure</li><li>A foreign damages award copied as a local entitlement</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Damages require an evidential connection and an applicable legal basis.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Defamation and Privacy.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Compare a factual accusation, an attributed allegation and a stated opinion in three fictional posts.</li><li>Build a loss schedule distinguishing supported items from matters requiring further evidence.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a balanced publication-risk memorandum and an evidence-based loss schedule for the tailor. Include an analysis of a correction and two plausible defences.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M28:Inchoate Offences: Attempt, Conspiracy and Participation": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 28</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: INCHOATE OFFENCES: ATTEMPT, CONSPIRACY AND PARTICIPATION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Criminal Law</td><td>Inchoate Offences: Attempt, Conspiracy and Participation</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain inchoate offences: attempt, conspiracy and participation using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>An inchoate offence addresses conduct directed towards another offence before that target offence is completed. Attempt and conspiracy are distinct legal categories. Participation concerns a person’s legally relevant contribution, which must be proved individually.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Preparation</td><td>Earlier planning or conduct before the legally relevant attempt threshold</td></tr><tr><td>Attempt</td><td>Incomplete offending meeting the applicable conduct and mental requirements</td></tr><tr><td>Conspiracy</td><td>Agreement-based offence as defined by governing law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>For attempt, distinguish intention, preparation and the conduct required by the governing statutory test. Failure to complete a target offence does not automatically eliminate criminal responsibility, but intention alone does not establish every inchoate offence. For conspiracy, identify the alleged agreement, its unlawful object and any further requirement imposed by the applicable law.</p><p>Analyse each accused separately. Presence, friendship or association is not a substitute for evidence of the required agreement, assistance or mental element. An abandoned plan, intervention or claimed withdrawal may raise a separate question that must be tested under the local rules rather than a foreign mnemonic. Build an elements chart using the actual statutory text, record evidence for and against each element, and distinguish the target offence from the alleged incomplete offence.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: two people discuss taking a locked generator from a workshop. One purchases ordinary tools, another later approaches the door, and a third is simply nearby. The weak answer convicts all three because they know each other. A strong analysis separates the alleged agreement, each person’s intention and acts, the statutory threshold for attempt, and the evidence of participation. It does not assume that buying tools alone is either sufficient or irrelevant under every possible legal test.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Penal Law provisions on incomplete offences and participation, checking amendments and the precise elements of the alleged offence.</p><p><a href=\"https://assets.tobaccocontrollaws.org/uploads/legislation/Liberia/Liberia-Penal-Law-native.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Penal Law, Title 26 — primary statute reproduction (check amendments)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Start with the named statutory offence, distinguish preparation from the required conduct and address each accused individually. Avoid substituting suspicion for an elements analysis.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>An inchoate offence addresses conduct directed towards another offence before that target offence is completed. Attempt and conspiracy are distinct legal categories. Participation concerns a person’s legally relevant contribution, which must be proved individually.</li><li>Separate preparation from attempt.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Preparation</td><td>Earlier planning or conduct before the legally relevant attempt threshold</td></tr><tr><td>Attempt</td><td>Incomplete offending meeting the applicable conduct and mental requirements</td></tr><tr><td>Conspiracy</td><td>Agreement-based offence as defined by governing law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Preparation</summary><p>Earlier planning or conduct before the legally relevant attempt threshold</p></details><details><summary>Attempt</summary><p>Incomplete offending meeting the applicable conduct and mental requirements</p></details><details><summary>Conspiracy</summary><p>Agreement-based offence as defined by governing law</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why does an unsuccessful plan still require legal analysis?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An incomplete offence may exist if its statutory elements are proved</li><li>Failure to complete the target offence always eliminates liability</li><li>Discussion alone proves every criminal charge</li><li>All incomplete conduct has one universal legal test</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Attempt and conspiracy have distinct requirements that do not depend solely on completion.</p></details><p><strong>Which method avoids collective guilt by association?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Ignore evidence favourable to a bystander</li><li>Analyse each person’s acts, knowledge and required mental element</li><li>Assign the same role to everyone at the scene</li><li>Treat friendship as proof of agreement</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Criminal responsibility must be supported for the individual accused.</p></details><p><strong>What distinguishes an attempt question from an agreement-based conspiracy question?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An attempt always requires three people</li><li>A conspiracy can never involve intention</li><li>The statutory conduct threshold differs from proof of the required agreement</li><li>Both are proved only by a completed theft</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> The categories require different elements and evidence.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Preparation and Attempt.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Place fictional acts on a timeline and identify the facts relevant to the statutory attempt threshold.</li><li>Draft opposing arguments about whether the record supports a particular alleged agreement.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Produce separate elements-and-evidence charts for the three people. Write a 700-word comparison of the strongest prosecution and defence arguments for each.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M28:Property Offences, Dishonesty and Proof": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 28</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: PROPERTY OFFENCES, DISHONESTY AND PROOF</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Criminal Law</td><td>Property Offences, Dishonesty and Proof</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain property offences, dishonesty and proof using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Property offences differ according to conduct, entitlement, consent, mental elements and any additional circumstances. An entrusted-property allegation differs from an unauthorised taking. A failed commercial promise is not automatically a proved criminal deception.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Custody</td><td>Holding an asset, distinct from ownership or permission to use it</td></tr><tr><td>Consent or authority</td><td>Legally relevant permission whose scope must be established</td></tr><tr><td>Criminal proof</td><td>Proof of the statutory elements under the required burden</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the property, the person with the relevant interest and the accused’s role. Ask how possession was obtained, what authority or consent existed, what conduct changed that position and which mental element the statute requires. Ownership, custody and authority to use an asset are different matters. An accounting shortfall is a fact to investigate, not a complete offence definition.</p><p>Where deception is alleged, identify the actual representation, its falsity and the causal role it played. A broken promise may support a civil issue without proving dishonest intention at the legally relevant time. Where force or threats are alleged, examine the specific offence and additional required facts. Trace records, permission, movement of funds and competing explanations. Keep the criminal burden of proof distinct from a business’s internal disciplinary conclusion.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a cooperative treasurer transfers money from a fund to a supplier, and equipment never arrives. Members accuse the treasurer of stealing. The weak answer equates loss with guilt. The strong answer checks the treasurer’s authority, supplier agreement, bank trail, knowledge and alleged personal benefit. It separates possible breach, fraud by another party, error and any evidence-supported entrusted-property offence before stating a conditional conclusion.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate the Penal Law provisions governing the alleged taking, deception or entrusted-property offence. Verify the wording rather than importing foreign offence labels.</p><p><a href=\"https://assets.tobaccocontrollaws.org/uploads/legislation/Liberia/Liberia-Penal-Law-native.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Penal Law, Title 26 — primary statute reproduction (check amendments)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Use the actual statutory elements as headings. Separate loss from wrongful conduct, and address authority, consent, mental state and alternative explanations with record-based reasoning.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Property offences differ according to conduct, entitlement, consent, mental elements and any additional circumstances. An entrusted-property allegation differs from an unauthorised taking. A failed commercial promise is not automatically a proved criminal deception.</li><li>Separate custody from consent or authority.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Custody</td><td>Holding an asset, distinct from ownership or permission to use it</td></tr><tr><td>Consent or authority</td><td>Legally relevant permission whose scope must be established</td></tr><tr><td>Criminal proof</td><td>Proof of the statutory elements under the required burden</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Custody</summary><p>Holding an asset, distinct from ownership or permission to use it</p></details><details><summary>Consent or authority</summary><p>Legally relevant permission whose scope must be established</p></details><details><summary>Criminal proof</summary><p>Proof of the statutory elements under the required burden</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why is a financial loss not sufficient by itself to prove a property offence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A missing item removes the presumption of innocence</li><li>The statutory conduct and mental elements still require evidence</li><li>Every failed purchase is theft</li><li>The complainant’s allegation fixes the offence</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Loss may have several explanations and does not substitute for offence elements.</p></details><p><strong>What matters when a person originally received property lawfully?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A rule that all custody proves ownership</li><li>The asset’s price alone</li><li>The scope of their authority and the later alleged misuse</li><li>A rule that lawful receipt prevents every possible offence</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Entrusted property requires analysis of permission, subsequent conduct and the governing offence.</p></details><p><strong>A supplier’s promise later fails. What must be checked before alleging criminal deception?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The failure alone as conclusive criminal proof</li><li>Whether the buyer would prefer a criminal process</li><li>Whether the supplier owns a business card</li><li>The relevant representation, mental element and evidence at the legally relevant time</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Civil non-performance and criminal deception are distinct questions.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Custody and Consent or authority.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Trace a fictional cooperative payment from authorisation to recipient and identify unresolved steps.</li><li>Compare a civil delivery dispute with an evidence-supported alleged deception using an elements table.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a financial-record request list and a 900-word elements analysis for the cooperative. Explain which facts would distinguish civil non-performance from an alleged offence.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M29:Charging, Disclosure and Pre-Trial Applications": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 29</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CHARGING, DISCLOSURE AND PRE-TRIAL APPLICATIONS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Criminal Procedure</td><td>Charging, Disclosure and Pre-Trial Applications</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain charging, disclosure and pre-trial applications using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A charge states an alleged offence through the applicable process; it is not a finding of guilt. Disclosure concerns access to legally required material. Pre-trial applications address issues that may need determination before the substantive hearing.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Charge</td><td>Formal allegation through the applicable process</td></tr><tr><td>Disclosure</td><td>Access to material required by governing procedural duties</td></tr><tr><td>Pre-trial application</td><td>Request to resolve a defined issue before substantive determination</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Read the charging document for the alleged conduct, date, parties and offence particulars required by law. Distinguish a police narrative, an indictment or other authorised charging instrument, and a judicial determination. Any defect must be analysed against the governing requirements and available remedy rather than assumed to end the case automatically.</p><p>Create a disclosure and evidence plan identifying statements, records, exhibits and potentially favourable material. Availability of an item does not establish admissibility or reliability. A pre-trial application should name its legal issue, requested order and supporting record; it should not ask the court to accept an unsupported account as the final verdict. Check local procedure for objections, notice, counsel and access to material. Do not import a foreign prosecution form or discovery timetable into Liberia.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: an accused is charged over a missing warehouse item. The investigator’s summary mentions a stock count but omits a witness statement placing the accused elsewhere. A weak response demands instant acquittal without examining the record. A strong response identifies the charge’s required particulars, requests the relevant complete material through lawful procedure, preserves the potentially favourable statement and explains the specific pre-trial issue requiring decision.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Criminal Procedure Law with Article 21 and applicable court rules. Identify the current charging and pre-trial procedures for the proposed offence.</p><p><a href=\"https://natlex.ilo.org/dyn/natlex2/natlex2/files/download/42875/LBR42875.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Criminal Procedure Law, Title 2 — primary code reproduction hosted by ILO (check amendments)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Distinguish charging sufficiency, disclosure duties and the requested pre-trial decision. Connect each issue to local authority and a specific part of the record.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A charge states an alleged offence through the applicable process; it is not a finding of guilt. Disclosure concerns access to legally required material. Pre-trial applications address issues that may need determination before the substantive hearing.</li><li>Separate charge from disclosure.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Charge</td><td>Formal allegation through the applicable process</td></tr><tr><td>Disclosure</td><td>Access to material required by governing procedural duties</td></tr><tr><td>Pre-trial application</td><td>Request to resolve a defined issue before substantive determination</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Charge</summary><p>Formal allegation through the applicable process</p></details><details><summary>Disclosure</summary><p>Access to material required by governing procedural duties</p></details><details><summary>Pre-trial application</summary><p>Request to resolve a defined issue before substantive determination</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What does a valid charging document establish?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Conclusive reliability of every witness</li><li>Automatic defeat of all defences</li><li>The formal allegation to be determined through lawful proceedings</li><li>A final conviction</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> A charge defines the allegation rather than proving its truth.</p></details><p><strong>Why identify a potentially favourable omitted statement?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It automatically makes every other record inadmissible</li><li>It authorises editing the investigator’s original file</li><li>It guarantees a particular verdict</li><li>It may affect preparation, proof and relevant procedural duties</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Relevant contrary material should be investigated through the applicable process.</p></details><p><strong>What makes a pre-trial application legally focused?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A defined issue, governing authority, supporting record and requested order</li><li>A repetition of every accusation with no requested relief</li><li>A foreign form presented as local law</li><li>A demand to skip the entire evidential process</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A precise application helps the court identify what it is lawfully being asked to decide.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Charge and Disclosure.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Identify allegations, evidence descriptions and conclusions in a fictional investigator’s summary.</li><li>Draft a focused disclosure request that explains the relevance of each requested record.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a mock pre-trial file: charge checklist, disclosure request outline, favourable-evidence note and a concise application for a specified lawful order.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M29:Criminal Appeals, Review and Wrongful-Conviction Safeguards": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 29</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CRIMINAL APPEALS, REVIEW AND WRONGFUL-CONVICTION SAFEGUARDS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Criminal Procedure</td><td>Criminal Appeals, Review and Wrongful-Conviction Safeguards</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain criminal appeals, review and wrongful-conviction safeguards using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>An appeal tests an identified decision through an authorised review process. Grounds may concern law, procedure or another recognised review issue. A claim of wrongful conviction requires careful handling of the judgment, record and evidence rather than a public assertion alone.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ground of appeal</td><td>Recognised basis for challenging a decision</td></tr><tr><td>Materiality</td><td>Significance of an alleged error to the outcome</td></tr><tr><td>Review record</td><td>Relevant documents, evidence and rulings supporting the challenge</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Begin with the judgment and the rule governing review. Identify the alleged error, where it appears in the record and how it could affect the outcome under the applicable standard. An appeal is not automatically a fresh trial with unrestricted new evidence. Separate disagreement with a witness from a legally recognised ground for intervention.</p><p>A responsible review file records preservation of issues, relevant rulings, exhibits and any missing transcript. If new material appears, determine the proper procedural route and how its origin and reliability can be assessed. Avoid promising release merely because an inconsistency exists. Protect the dignity and confidential information of accused persons and witnesses, and distinguish an allegation of error from a final appellate determination. Legal assistance must be provided by authorised persons within the applicable rules.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a learner reads a public conviction judgment and notices that an admitted exhibit appears inconsistent with the court’s account of a key date. A weak commentary declares innocence immediately. A strong review note obtains the complete relevant record, identifies the possible factual or legal error and its materiality, considers whether the issue was raised, and researches the correct local review route. The note states that an inconsistency requires investigation rather than proving a remedy automatically.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the local provisions and court rules on criminal appeals and review with constitutional protections. Verify the available route, record requirements and current deadlines.</p><p><a href=\"https://natlex.ilo.org/dyn/natlex2/natlex2/files/download/42875/LBR42875.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Criminal Procedure Law, Title 2 — primary code reproduction hosted by ILO (check amendments)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>State a precise proposed ground, cite the record and explain materiality under the local review framework. Identify which remedial conclusion depends on further record or authority.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>An appeal tests an identified decision through an authorised review process. Grounds may concern law, procedure or another recognised review issue. A claim of wrongful conviction requires careful handling of the judgment, record and evidence rather than a public assertion alone.</li><li>Separate ground of appeal from materiality.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ground of appeal</td><td>Recognised basis for challenging a decision</td></tr><tr><td>Materiality</td><td>Significance of an alleged error to the outcome</td></tr><tr><td>Review record</td><td>Relevant documents, evidence and rulings supporting the challenge</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Ground of appeal</summary><p>Recognised basis for challenging a decision</p></details><details><summary>Materiality</summary><p>Significance of an alleged error to the outcome</p></details><details><summary>Review record</summary><p>Relevant documents, evidence and rulings supporting the challenge</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which product best supports a criminal appeal issue?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An unsupported declaration of innocence</li><li>A new accusation against every witness</li><li>A demand that all appeals become fresh trials</li><li>A record-based ground identifying the alleged error and its possible material effect</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Review requires a recognised ground connected to the decision and governing standard.</p></details><p><strong>Why check whether an issue was raised in the original proceeding?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Preservation and available review routes may affect how it can be considered</li><li>An unraised issue proves the accused is guilty</li><li>All errors become irrelevant once judgment is entered</li><li>Preservation rules are identical worldwide</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The procedural history can affect the lawful review analysis.</p></details><p><strong>New evidence appears after judgment. What should be examined?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether any new item automatically reverses conviction</li><li>Its provenance, reliability and the authorised route for presenting it</li><li>Whether social-media circulation makes it admissible</li><li>Whether a learner can insert it into the old record privately</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> New material must be handled through the appropriate process and evidential safeguards.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Ground of appeal and Materiality.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Convert three vague complaints about a trial into possible grounds tied to a fictional record.</li><li>Prepare a provenance checklist for a document discovered after judgment.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,000-word mock appellate issues memorandum using a public decision or a fictional record. Include grounds, record pinpoints, counterarguments and the procedural questions to verify.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M30:Witness Examination, Expert Evidence and Credibility": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 30</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: WITNESS EXAMINATION, EXPERT EVIDENCE AND CREDIBILITY</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Evidence</td><td>Witness Examination, Expert Evidence and Credibility</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain witness examination, expert evidence and credibility using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A factual witness gives evidence within legally permitted knowledge; an expert offers specialised opinion within the governing rules. Credibility concerns reliability and truthfulness. The weight of an opinion depends on its basis, method and fit to the issue, not credentials alone.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Expert opinion</td><td>Specialised assessment within the relevant rules and expertise</td></tr><tr><td>Credibility</td><td>Reliability and truthfulness assessed from relevant evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>Persuasive value after considering basis, limits and competing proof</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Begin with what the witness can properly address. Direct observation, inference and reported information require different treatment. A factual witness should not be turned into an expert merely because a question asks for a technical conclusion. For an expert, investigate qualifications, the material reviewed, assumptions, method and limits. Identify whether the opinion answers a relevant issue and whether its legal admission conditions are met.</p><p>Examination should expose the basis of the account rather than reward confidence or dramatic presentation. Compare contemporaneous records and competing explanations without assuming every inconsistency is deliberate dishonesty. Cross-examination can test knowledge, method and assumptions, but questions are not evidence unless the governing process makes the resulting answer admissible. Separate admissibility from the persuasive weight the fact-finder may assign after hearing the evidence.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a road-incident claim includes an engineer’s report based on a photograph taken after repairs, and a passenger’s direct account of the collision. A weak answer accepts the engineer’s conclusion as final because of the job title. A strong analysis identifies the engineer’s assumptions and missing measurements, separates those limits from the passenger’s opportunity to observe, and prepares targeted questions about both accounts. It does not substitute the learner’s personal guess for technical evidence.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the evidence provisions and relevant Liberian rulings on witness competence, opinions and examination. Verify the applicable rule for the particular proceeding.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Identify the witness’s proper subject, the governing admission rule and the basis for assessing weight. Tie each proposed examination question to a specific factual or methodological issue.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A factual witness gives evidence within legally permitted knowledge; an expert offers specialised opinion within the governing rules. Credibility concerns reliability and truthfulness. The weight of an opinion depends on its basis, method and fit to the issue, not credentials alone.</li><li>Separate expert opinion from credibility.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Expert opinion</td><td>Specialised assessment within the relevant rules and expertise</td></tr><tr><td>Credibility</td><td>Reliability and truthfulness assessed from relevant evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>Persuasive value after considering basis, limits and competing proof</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Expert opinion</summary><p>Specialised assessment within the relevant rules and expertise</p></details><details><summary>Credibility</summary><p>Reliability and truthfulness assessed from relevant evidence</p></details><details><summary>Weight</summary><p>Persuasive value after considering basis, limits and competing proof</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why is an expert’s professional title not sufficient by itself?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The opinion’s relevant basis, method and limits must also be examined</li><li>Experts never provide useful evidence</li><li>Credentials automatically decide the legal issue</li><li>A technical opinion replaces every factual record</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Expertise must be connected to a supported opinion relevant to the dispute.</p></details><p><strong>Which question directly tests the engineer’s assumptions?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>“Is your report printed professionally?”</li><li>“What condition of the road existed when the photograph was taken?”</li><li>“Do you prefer this claimant?”</li><li>“Would you agree the court should always accept experts?”</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> The timing and condition of the source material may affect the opinion’s reliability.</p></details><p><strong>What distinguishes an admissibility decision from evidential weight?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Weak evidence is automatically a constitutional amendment</li><li>Weight determines the court’s original jurisdiction</li><li>Permission to receive evidence differs from how persuasive it is</li><li>Every admitted item is conclusive</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> An admitted item may still have substantial limitations in proving a fact.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Expert opinion and Credibility.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Label a report’s observations, assumptions and opinions in separate columns.</li><li>Practise a short examination that tests the photograph’s provenance and the passenger’s opportunity to observe.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create a witness matrix and ten focused examination questions for the passenger and engineer. Add an 800-word note separating admission issues from weight.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M30:Privilege, Improperly Obtained Evidence and Objections": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 30</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: PRIVILEGE, IMPROPERLY OBTAINED EVIDENCE AND OBJECTIONS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Evidence</td><td>Privilege, Improperly Obtained Evidence and Objections</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain privilege, improperly obtained evidence and objections using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Privilege can protect specified communications or information from compelled disclosure under its conditions. Confidentiality is a broader duty concerning information handling. An objection asks the court to determine a defined legal problem with offered evidence or procedure.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Privilege</td><td>Conditional legal protection against compelled disclosure</td></tr><tr><td>Confidentiality</td><td>Duty concerning handling and disclosure of information</td></tr><tr><td>Objection</td><td>Focused request for a ruling on a legal evidence or procedure issue</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify who holds a claimed protection, the relationship in which the communication arose, its purpose and any relevant disclosure or exception. Merely labelling a document “confidential” does not settle legal privilege. Equally, an ethical duty may persist even where a particular evidence rule does not prevent disclosure. Assess the applicable Liberian framework rather than copying a foreign privilege test.</p><p>Where evidence was allegedly obtained improperly, separate the method of obtaining it, constitutional or statutory rights and the legal consequence for its use. A violation may raise remedies or accountability without producing the same exclusion rule in every jurisdiction. An objection should specify the item, rule, reason and requested ruling; argumentative outrage is not a substitute. Preserve the objection and decision as required for review. Never recommend secretly obtaining protected information to improve a classroom case.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a former assistant shares a legal-advice email and an unrelated public invoice from an office file. A weak response treats everything in the folder as either automatically privileged or automatically usable. A strong response analyses each item, its purpose, who holds any protection and how disclosure occurred; examines separate confidentiality and evidential questions; and identifies a lawful objection or referral rather than exploiting potentially protected material.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Liberian ethics code, constitutional safeguards and evidence rules relevant to the issue. Distinguish ethical confidentiality from any legally recognised privilege or exclusion rule.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/code-of-moral-and-professional-ethic/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Code of Moral and Professional Ethics — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Analyse each protection and item separately. Explain the distinction between an ethical handling duty, an admissibility question and an available remedy for improper conduct.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Privilege can protect specified communications or information from compelled disclosure under its conditions. Confidentiality is a broader duty concerning information handling. An objection asks the court to determine a defined legal problem with offered evidence or procedure.</li><li>Separate privilege from confidentiality.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Privilege</td><td>Conditional legal protection against compelled disclosure</td></tr><tr><td>Confidentiality</td><td>Duty concerning handling and disclosure of information</td></tr><tr><td>Objection</td><td>Focused request for a ruling on a legal evidence or procedure issue</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Privilege</summary><p>Conditional legal protection against compelled disclosure</p></details><details><summary>Confidentiality</summary><p>Duty concerning handling and disclosure of information</p></details><details><summary>Objection</summary><p>Focused request for a ruling on a legal evidence or procedure issue</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why does a “confidential” label not by itself establish legal privilege?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Public invoices always become legal advice in an office file</li><li>The governing protection has conditions that must be proved</li><li>All labels automatically create judicial immunity</li><li>No confidential information can ever be protected</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> The legal basis and communication’s function determine the privilege analysis.</p></details><p><strong>How should two different documents from the same folder be assessed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>As both admissible whenever an assistant releases them</li><li>As both permanently excluded without further inquiry</li><li>Individually according to their purpose, source and applicable protections</li><li>As legally identical because their physical location is shared</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Storage location alone does not determine legal status.</p></details><p><strong>What makes an evidential objection properly focused?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Accuse every witness of dishonesty</li><li>Insist all improperly obtained evidence has one worldwide rule</li><li>Replace legal grounds with indignation</li><li>Identify the item, applicable rule, basis and requested ruling</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> A court needs a defined legal question and requested response.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Privilege and Confidentiality.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Sort fictional communications into public information, confidential information and items requiring a privilege analysis.</li><li>Practise a concise objection that states its legal basis without revealing unnecessary protected content.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a privilege-and-objection memorandum for the two documents. Include an ethical handling plan and explain why their location in one file does not settle their status.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M31:Leases, Easements and Co-Ownership": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 31</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: LEASES, EASEMENTS AND CO-OWNERSHIP</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Property and Land</td><td>Leases, Easements and Co-Ownership</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain leases, easements and co-ownership using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A lease grants a legally defined right to use or possess property under its terms. An easement concerns a recognised use right over another property. Co-ownership involves shared legally recognised interests, whose powers and obligations require their own analysis.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lease</td><td>Defined right of use or possession under a lawful arrangement</td></tr><tr><td>Easement</td><td>Recognised use right affecting another property</td></tr><tr><td>Co-ownership</td><td>Shared interests whose powers depend on the governing form</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Distinguish title, possession and permitted use. A tenant’s physical control does not make them the owner, and a licence to cross land is not automatically an easement or transfer. Identify the person granting the right, their authority, the land category, form and scope of the arrangement, and any legally required process.</p><p>For a lease, examine parties, premises, duration, rent, repair responsibilities, termination and dispute provisions. For an access right, establish location, purpose, users and conditions instead of assuming unrestricted use. Where interests are shared, determine the legal form, consent required for dealing with the asset and responsibility for income, expenses and disputes. A foreign category such as a particular concurrent estate must be tied to local recognition before it becomes the rule in a Liberian answer.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: two relatives share an inherited building. One grants a five-year workshop lease and promises the tenant unlimited access across a neighbour’s plot. A weak answer assumes the signature binds every owner and neighbour. A strong answer checks the signer’s interest and authority, the co-owners’ rights, the lease terms and the independent legal basis for access. It separates use of the building from a claimed right over someone else’s land.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Land Rights Act and relevant Liberian property and civil authorities. Determine which locally recognised interest or permission is asserted before borrowing foreign estate terminology.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=79:land-rights-act&amp;start=40\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Land Rights Act 2018 — Liberia Land Authority</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Use separate headings for title, authority and each asserted use right. Explain which local legal form supports the arrangement and where further recognition or consent must be verified.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A lease grants a legally defined right to use or possess property under its terms. An easement concerns a recognised use right over another property. Co-ownership involves shared legally recognised interests, whose powers and obligations require their own analysis.</li><li>Separate lease from easement.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lease</td><td>Defined right of use or possession under a lawful arrangement</td></tr><tr><td>Easement</td><td>Recognised use right affecting another property</td></tr><tr><td>Co-ownership</td><td>Shared interests whose powers depend on the governing form</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Lease</summary><p>Defined right of use or possession under a lawful arrangement</p></details><details><summary>Easement</summary><p>Recognised use right affecting another property</p></details><details><summary>Co-ownership</summary><p>Shared interests whose powers depend on the governing form</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A tenant occupies a building under a lease. What must not be assumed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The lease needs an identifiable landlord</li><li>The permitted use may have conditions</li><li>Possession alone proves ownership of the building</li><li>Possession may arise under agreed terms</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Possession, title and authority to grant further rights are distinct.</p></details><p><strong>Why analyse a promised path over neighbouring land separately?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A lease automatically creates rights over every neighbouring plot</li><li>All access permission transfers land ownership</li><li>The tenant’s need determines the neighbour’s legal obligations</li><li>The landlord needs a lawful basis for granting that independent use right</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> An agreement about one property does not establish authority over another.</p></details><p><strong>One co-owner signs a long lease. What must be checked?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Their powers and any legally required consent of other interest holders</li><li>Whether one signature always binds all co-owners</li><li>Whether shared ownership eliminates every lease</li><li>Whether the tenant likes the premises</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Authority depends on the recognised ownership arrangement and applicable rules.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Lease and Easement.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Draw a map of the building, neighbour’s plot and asserted access route, marking distinct rights.</li><li>Negotiate a fictional lease checklist while maintaining a separate list of unresolved title and authority questions.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a lease-and-access term sheet and an 800-word authority memorandum. Identify the documents needed before assuming the arrangement binds co-owners or neighbours.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M31:Land Transactions, Surveys and Community Consent": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 31</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: LAND TRANSACTIONS, SURVEYS AND COMMUNITY CONSENT</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Property and Land</td><td>Land Transactions, Surveys and Community Consent</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain land transactions, surveys and community consent using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A survey describes and locates land; it is not by itself proof of title or authority to transfer. Due diligence investigates rights, boundaries, restrictions and competing claims. Community consent requires the process and representation recognised by applicable law.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Description and location of land, distinct from title</td></tr><tr><td>Due diligence</td><td>Documented investigation of rights, restrictions and risk</td></tr><tr><td>Community authority</td><td>Legally recognised power and process to act for the community</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Check the seller’s identity and legal interest, the land category, source documents and any overlapping rights. Reconcile a survey plan with the physical site and document descriptions; a precise drawing of disputed land does not settle who may convey it. Verify whether the purported representative has authority and whether required community decisions and participation occurred.</p><p>A transaction file should distinguish completed checks from assurances still awaiting evidence. Record the original source, authenticity questions, public or institutional checks, boundary observations and unresolved claims. Check the separate role of planning, environmental or other approvals rather than treating land permission as universal operational approval. Document gaps before commitment and avoid promising “guaranteed title” because one intermediary produced a deed. Use qualified local professionals for real transactions.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: an investor proposes a training centre near a customary community and receives a survey plus a letter signed by one spokesperson. Several residents dispute the boundary and say no community decision occurred. A weak answer concludes that the professional-looking plan proves a valid sale. A strong answer verifies the land category, community representation, actual participation, overlapping claims and source documents, while separating the proposed centre’s regulatory permissions from land rights.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Land Rights Act and Liberia Land Authority guidance on document vetting and land services. For customary land, verify the applicable community governance and participation requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=79:land-rights-act&amp;start=40\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Land Rights Act 2018 — Liberia Land Authority</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Treat survey, title, authority, participation and regulatory permissions as distinct checks. Explain how each missing item changes the transaction risk rather than issuing a blanket guarantee.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A survey describes and locates land; it is not by itself proof of title or authority to transfer. Due diligence investigates rights, boundaries, restrictions and competing claims. Community consent requires the process and representation recognised by applicable law.</li><li>Separate survey from due diligence.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Description and location of land, distinct from title</td></tr><tr><td>Due diligence</td><td>Documented investigation of rights, restrictions and risk</td></tr><tr><td>Community authority</td><td>Legally recognised power and process to act for the community</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Survey</summary><p>Description and location of land, distinct from title</p></details><details><summary>Due diligence</summary><p>Documented investigation of rights, restrictions and risk</p></details><details><summary>Community authority</summary><p>Legally recognised power and process to act for the community</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What does a survey plan establish without further title investigation?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Conclusive ownership in every circumstance</li><li>Authority of any spokesperson to sell</li><li>Automatic planning and environmental approval</li><li>A description or location that still needs to be linked to lawful rights</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> A survey and lawful title address different questions.</p></details><p><strong>Why investigate the spokesperson’s mandate?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Community representation and required participation must have a lawful basis</li><li>One signature always proves every resident consented</li><li>Customary land has no legal safeguards</li><li>An investor can choose any representative to bind the community</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The legitimacy of authority cannot be inferred solely from a signed letter.</p></details><p><strong>A boundary is disputed despite matching document names. What should happen?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Promise that a course certificate validates the transfer</li><li>Investigate the overlapping claims and reconcile records with the site</li><li>Ignore the dispute because names match</li><li>Alter the survey to remove the neighbour</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Document consistency does not eliminate a competing-rights issue.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Survey and Due diligence.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Compare a fictional survey, deed description and boundary sketch to identify inconsistencies.</li><li>Simulate a community-information meeting that records questions and unresolved representation issues without manufacturing consent.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Build a 12-item transaction checklist and a stop/go issues register for the training centre. Assign a legal or factual reason to each unresolved check.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M32:Wills, Intestacy and Administration of Estates": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 32</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: WILLS, INTESTACY AND ADMINISTRATION OF ESTATES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Family Law and Estates</td><td>Wills, Intestacy and Administration of Estates</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain wills, intestacy and administration of estates using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A will is a testamentary instrument whose validity depends on governing requirements. Intestacy concerns property passing without an effective testamentary disposition. Estate administration gathers assets, resolves obligations and distributes according to lawful authority.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Testate disposition</td><td>Transfer directed by an effective testamentary instrument</td></tr><tr><td>Intestacy</td><td>Passing of property without an effective testamentary disposition</td></tr><tr><td>Estate administration</td><td>Lawful collection, management and distribution of the estate</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Distinguish making a will, proving it through the relevant process and acting as an authorised representative. The existence of a document headed “will” does not establish capacity, execution or the scope of its effect. Identify the property the decedent could dispose of and any legally relevant claims or restrictions. A relative’s expectation is different from a verified entitlement.</p><p>An estate file needs an inventory, liabilities record, title questions, the claimed testamentary documents and the representative’s authority. Do not distribute disputed property merely because a family meeting supports a particular outcome. Intestate shares, priority of representatives, notice and court procedure come from current Liberian law, not a foreign video. Explain which issue can be analysed now and which requires a probate ruling, verified record or authorised professional assistance.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a family in Greenville finds two documents said to be wills, one unsigned, while the decedent’s land ownership and a lender’s claim are disputed. A weak answer divides everything equally by personal preference. A strong answer identifies the competing testamentary instruments, the execution and capacity questions, the property actually in the estate, outstanding obligations and the need for lawful administration. It avoids inventing local inheritance fractions.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Decedents Estates Law, Title 8, including the provisions relevant to testamentary and intestate administration. Check amendments and applicable protections before applying any distribution rule.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=45:decedents-estates-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Decedents Estates Law, Title 8 — Liberia Land Authority</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Organise the answer around instrument validity, estate property, obligations, representative authority and distribution. Use the local statute for the exact rule instead of inventing fractions.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A will is a testamentary instrument whose validity depends on governing requirements. Intestacy concerns property passing without an effective testamentary disposition. Estate administration gathers assets, resolves obligations and distributes according to lawful authority.</li><li>Separate testate disposition from intestacy.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Testate disposition</td><td>Transfer directed by an effective testamentary instrument</td></tr><tr><td>Intestacy</td><td>Passing of property without an effective testamentary disposition</td></tr><tr><td>Estate administration</td><td>Lawful collection, management and distribution of the estate</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Testate disposition</summary><p>Transfer directed by an effective testamentary instrument</p></details><details><summary>Intestacy</summary><p>Passing of property without an effective testamentary disposition</p></details><details><summary>Estate administration</summary><p>Lawful collection, management and distribution of the estate</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why does the heading “will” not settle validity?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Execution, capacity and other governing requirements still need examination</li><li>All handwritten documents are automatically valid wills</li><li>Every will must be treated as intestacy</li><li>Family agreement alone replaces all legal requirements</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Testamentary status depends on the applicable law and relevant facts.</p></details><p><strong>Which item must be resolved before distributing claimed land?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Only whether a foreign video shows equal shares</li><li>Whether and to what extent the property belongs to the estate</li><li>Only which relative arrives first</li><li>Only whether the land is valuable</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Administration cannot assume ownership of disputed assets.</p></details><p><strong>What distinguishes a representative’s authority from a family member’s expectation?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Expectation automatically creates representative powers</li><li>An inventory alone appoints an administrator</li><li>A lawful appointment or recognised basis is needed to administer assets</li><li>Every relative may dispose of every asset immediately</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Administration and beneficial entitlement are separate legal questions.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Testate disposition and Intestacy.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Classify fictional assets as established estate property, disputed title or property requiring further documentation.</li><li>Build a probate-file checklist that keeps representative authority separate from proposed beneficiary shares.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare an estate inventory and a 1,000-word issues memorandum for the family. Separate will validity, ownership, liabilities, representation and distribution questions.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M32:Trusts, Fiduciary Administration and Beneficiary Protection": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 32</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: TRUSTS, FIDUCIARY ADMINISTRATION AND BENEFICIARY PROTECTION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Family Law and Estates</td><td>Trusts, Fiduciary Administration and Beneficiary Protection</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain trusts, fiduciary administration and beneficiary protection using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A trust separates administration of property from beneficial interests under a recognised legal arrangement. A fiduciary must act within the governing duties and purpose, accounting for decisions and handling conflicts. A beneficiary’s interest differs from an administrator’s personal ownership.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal administration</td><td>Authority to manage property within governing powers</td></tr><tr><td>Beneficial interest</td><td>Interest held for the person protected by the arrangement</td></tr><tr><td>Fiduciary conflict</td><td>Personal or competing interest affecting a duty owed to another</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the instrument or other legal basis, the property, beneficiaries, purpose and powers. A promise to “look after money” is not enough to establish every element of a legally recognised trust. Distinguish a trustee’s administrative discretion from unrestricted personal use. Decisions should connect to authorised purposes and be supported by records.</p><p>Investigate duties of loyalty, care, accounting and lawful distribution within the applicable framework. A conflict is not cured merely because a transaction appears profitable. Keep separate records for property administered for others, identify fees or expenses requiring authorisation and record information owed to beneficiaries. Available relief for breach depends on the duty, conduct, loss and procedure; it is not automatically a fixed monetary award or criminal conviction.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: an adult is appointed to manage a fund for two children’s education. They lend part of it to their own business and later say the arrangement may earn a profit. A weak answer treats potential profit as complete justification. A strong answer checks the governing instrument, investment powers, conflict rules and accounting records, considers the beneficiaries’ protection and distinguishes authorised administration from an unapproved personal benefit.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the Decedents Estates Law and verified Liberian authority relevant to the proposed arrangement. Check whether the legal relationship and trustee powers are recognised before assuming a foreign trust model applies.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=45:decedents-estates-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Decedents Estates Law, Title 8 — Liberia Land Authority</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Establish the arrangement, identify its powers and duties, apply them to the conflicted act and explain possible relief with evidential and procedural limits.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A trust separates administration of property from beneficial interests under a recognised legal arrangement. A fiduciary must act within the governing duties and purpose, accounting for decisions and handling conflicts. A beneficiary’s interest differs from an administrator’s personal ownership.</li><li>Separate legal administration from beneficial interest.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal administration</td><td>Authority to manage property within governing powers</td></tr><tr><td>Beneficial interest</td><td>Interest held for the person protected by the arrangement</td></tr><tr><td>Fiduciary conflict</td><td>Personal or competing interest affecting a duty owed to another</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Legal administration</summary><p>Authority to manage property within governing powers</p></details><details><summary>Beneficial interest</summary><p>Interest held for the person protected by the arrangement</p></details><details><summary>Fiduciary conflict</summary><p>Personal or competing interest affecting a duty owed to another</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why does possible profit not itself justify a trustee’s loan to their own business?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A business loan can never involve a conflict</li><li>Authority and conflict duties must be analysed independently of the hoped-for return</li><li>All profitable transactions automatically satisfy fiduciary duties</li><li>Beneficiaries have no interest in administration</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Fiduciary responsibility concerns lawful purpose, powers and loyalty as well as financial result.</p></details><p><strong>What must be established before applying a foreign trust model locally?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Only whether the administrator is respected</li><li>A rule that every promise creates an identical trust</li><li>The recognised Liberian legal basis and governing arrangement</li><li>Only whether the document uses the word trust</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Legal recognition and the instrument’s terms determine the relationship.</p></details><p><strong>Which record supports accountable fiduciary administration?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A single personal account with no transaction explanations</li><li>Only verbal assurances to one relative</li><li>Only the administrator’s projected profit</li><li>Separate asset, decision, income and expenditure records</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Beneficiaries and lawful oversight need a traceable administration record.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Legal administration and Beneficial interest.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Audit a fictional education fund’s ledger for missing authorisation and mixing of property.</li><li>Draft beneficiary questions that seek an account without prematurely alleging a proved offence.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a fiduciary-accounting checklist and an 800-word conflict-and-remedies memorandum. Identify both records required and legal questions that remain unresolved.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M33:Wages, Working Time, Leave and Occupational Safety": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 33</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: WAGES, WORKING TIME, LEAVE AND OCCUPATIONAL SAFETY</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Labour Law</td><td>Wages, Working Time, Leave and Occupational Safety</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain wages, working time, leave and occupational safety using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Remuneration terms describe what work is paid and how; working-time and leave rules regulate conditions under the applicable framework. Occupational safety requires analysis of risks, duties and protective measures in the actual workplace.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gross and net pay</td><td>Pay before and after legally relevant deductions</td></tr><tr><td>Leave entitlement</td><td>Protected absence subject to the applicable conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Risk control</td><td>Measure reducing exposure to a workplace hazard</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Separate contractual promises from statutory minimum protections and identify which law covers the relationship. A pay analysis should reconcile agreed wages, hours or output, authorised deductions, payment records and the legal basis for any extra work. Gross pay, net pay and outstanding entitlement are different figures. Do not infer a current local rate from an old foreign lecture.</p><p>For leave, identify the qualifying circumstances, notice or evidence requirements and relevant entitlement rather than assuming every absence is misconduct. For safety, describe the hazard, exposed persons, feasible control measures and allocation of responsibilities. Protective equipment is one measure and does not necessarily remove a preventable hazard at its source. Preserve incident and medical records lawfully, protect privacy and separate a safety complaint from a concluded compensation claim.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a small workshop records a machine operator as absent during a medical visit, deducts pay and requires unguarded machinery work afterward. A weak answer discusses wages only. A strong answer checks the employment terms, time and pay records, leave rules, medical evidence and safety duties separately. It identifies immediate risk controls and a lawful dispute route without promising a specific compensation amount.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Decent Work Act provisions relevant to remuneration, working time, leave and safety. Verify current regulations and applicability; foreign rates or hour limits are not Liberian rules.</p><p><a href=\"https://csa.gov.lr/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Decent-Work-Act-2015.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Decent Work Act 2015 — Civil Service Agency</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Use separate headings for remuneration, leave and safety. Reconcile the records and apply the correct local requirements instead of importing rates or treating every issue as one grievance.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Remuneration terms describe what work is paid and how; working-time and leave rules regulate conditions under the applicable framework. Occupational safety requires analysis of risks, duties and protective measures in the actual workplace.</li><li>Separate gross and net pay from leave entitlement.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gross and net pay</td><td>Pay before and after legally relevant deductions</td></tr><tr><td>Leave entitlement</td><td>Protected absence subject to the applicable conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Risk control</td><td>Measure reducing exposure to a workplace hazard</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Gross and net pay</summary><p>Pay before and after legally relevant deductions</p></details><details><summary>Leave entitlement</summary><p>Protected absence subject to the applicable conditions</p></details><details><summary>Risk control</summary><p>Measure reducing exposure to a workplace hazard</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What distinguishes gross from net pay in a wage dispute?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Any deduction requested by an employer is automatically lawful</li><li>A payroll heading establishes the applicable statutory rate</li><li>The legal and recorded basis of deductions must be reconciled</li><li>Net pay always equals every promised gross amount</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Accurate analysis separates promised remuneration, deductions and actual payment.</p></details><p><strong>Why investigate a medical absence separately from misconduct?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Every medical absence automatically defeats all discipline</li><li>Medical records need not be handled confidentially</li><li>A payroll deduction proves criminal wrongdoing</li><li>The applicable leave conditions and evidence may change its legal treatment</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Leave rights and disciplinary allegations require their own rules and facts.</p></details><p><strong>What is a stronger safety plan than providing equipment alone?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Identify and control the actual hazard and residual risk</li><li>Assume protective equipment removes every employer duty</li><li>Treat a signed waiver as universal permission for unsafe work</li><li>Wait for a judgment before recording an obvious hazard</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Safety analysis considers the source of risk and effective controls under the governing duties.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Gross and net pay and Leave entitlement.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Reconcile a fictional timesheet with wages, deductions and payments, marking unresolved differences.</li><li>Rank workplace hazards by the controls required and identify records supporting a lawful safety complaint.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Produce a pay-reconciliation table, leave-issues note and workplace-risk checklist for the operator. Explain which current provisions must be checked for each claimed entitlement.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M33:Trade Unions, Collective Bargaining and Labour Institutions": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 33</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: TRADE UNIONS, COLLECTIVE BARGAINING AND LABOUR INSTITUTIONS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Labour Law</td><td>Trade Unions, Collective Bargaining and Labour Institutions</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain trade unions, collective bargaining and labour institutions using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Collective bargaining concerns negotiation through legally relevant worker and employer representation. A collective agreement differs from an individual employment contract. Labour institutions may have specialised powers that must be distinguished from informal negotiation.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Collective bargaining</td><td>Negotiation through legally relevant representation</td></tr><tr><td>Collective agreement</td><td>Instrument with the legal effect given by its terms and governing law</td></tr><tr><td>Representation mandate</td><td>Authority to negotiate or act for a defined group</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the represented group, the basis of representation, the issues open for bargaining and any required recognition or procedure. A representative’s mandate matters: an agreement cannot be assumed to bind everyone merely because a meeting occurred. Distinguish proposing terms, reaching agreement and making those terms legally effective under the relevant framework.</p><p>Collective terms interact with individual contracts and statutory protections. An individual worker’s preference does not alone decide the legal effect of a collective instrument, and negotiation does not authorise departure from non-waivable rights. Map grievance stages, specialised institutions and available review, using the current local statute. Examine the lawful conditions governing industrial action rather than treating every work stoppage as automatically lawful or unlawful. Keep retaliation allegations tied to evidence and the applicable protections.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: workers at a transport cooperative appoint two spokespersons to discuss rosters. Management says their meeting proves all workers surrendered future leave claims. A weak answer accepts either side’s blanket claim. A strong analysis checks the representatives’ mandate, the actual agreed text, applicable statutory rights and the process giving an instrument legal effect. It prepares a grievance route and distinguishes a proposal from a binding agreement.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Decent Work Act provisions relevant to association, collective relations and labour dispute institutions. Verify the applicable recognition and dispute procedures.</p><p><a href=\"https://csa.gov.lr/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Decent-Work-Act-2015.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Decent Work Act 2015 — Civil Service Agency</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Separate mandate, negotiation status, legal effect and dispute route. Explain which statutory protections qualify the parties’ bargaining choices.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Collective bargaining concerns negotiation through legally relevant worker and employer representation. A collective agreement differs from an individual employment contract. Labour institutions may have specialised powers that must be distinguished from informal negotiation.</li><li>Separate collective bargaining from collective agreement.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Collective bargaining</td><td>Negotiation through legally relevant representation</td></tr><tr><td>Collective agreement</td><td>Instrument with the legal effect given by its terms and governing law</td></tr><tr><td>Representation mandate</td><td>Authority to negotiate or act for a defined group</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Collective bargaining</summary><p>Negotiation through legally relevant representation</p></details><details><summary>Collective agreement</summary><p>Instrument with the legal effect given by its terms and governing law</p></details><details><summary>Representation mandate</summary><p>Authority to negotiate or act for a defined group</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why examine the spokespersons’ mandate?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Any meeting authorises surrender of every right</li><li>Every individual preference overrides all collective rules</li><li>A mandate replaces every statutory requirement</li><li>Authority to represent and bind a defined group must be established</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Representation and the effect of an agreement require legal and factual support.</p></details><p><strong>What separates proposed roster terms from an effective collective instrument?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Agreement and any applicable recognition or formal requirements</li><li>A proposal’s page count alone</li><li>A manager’s belief that discussions ended</li><li>The attendance of one worker</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Negotiation status must be distinguished from legal effectiveness.</p></details><p><strong>Why investigate statutory leave protections before accepting a waiver?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Leave is never a legal issue</li><li>Some protections may limit what can lawfully be negotiated or waived</li><li>Every proposed waiver is valid if management wants it</li><li>All collective agreements automatically repeal legislation</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> The governing statute constrains the effect of contractual and collective terms.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Collective bargaining and Collective agreement.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Role-play a bargaining session using a written mandate and clearly recorded provisional terms.</li><li>Compare an individual complaint, collective grievance and specialised institutional decision on a process chart.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a bargaining agenda, mandate statement and grievance-process map. Write a 700-word analysis of the claimed surrender of leave rights.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M34:Company Formation, Directors and Corporate Compliance": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 34</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: COMPANY FORMATION, DIRECTORS AND CORPORATE COMPLIANCE</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Corporations, Agency and Partnership</td><td>Company Formation, Directors and Corporate Compliance</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain company formation, directors and corporate compliance using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Incorporation creates a recognised entity through the governing process. Directors exercise powers subject to duties and the organisation’s lawful framework. Corporate compliance concerns obligations beyond simply possessing incorporation documents.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Incorporation</td><td>Creation of an entity through a legally recognised process</td></tr><tr><td>Director’s authority</td><td>Power to act within the governing corporate framework</td></tr><tr><td>Corporate compliance</td><td>Fulfilment of applicable ongoing and operational obligations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the entity form, constituent documents, registers and allocation of decision-making powers. A founder’s title does not necessarily authorise every transaction after incorporation. Distinguish board decisions, officer authority and shareholder matters by the governing rules and documents. Record who approved an act, any conflict and the basis for the decision.</p><p>Duties and conflicts require analysis of the particular role and act. An interested director’s transaction cannot be assessed only by whether it ultimately made money. Separate authority, proper purpose, disclosure and applicable approval requirements. Incorporation also does not automatically confer every operating licence, tax registration or professional permission. A compliance calendar should identify the actual obligation, responsible person and verified timing, without importing another country’s filing dates.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a newly formed education company has incorporation papers but no record of approving a director’s lease with their own property business. It also advertises a regulated service. The weak answer calls everything lawful because the company exists. A strong answer checks the decision powers, conflict and transaction record, then separately investigates the approvals needed for the advertised activity. It distinguishes legal identity from authority and regulatory compliance.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate the Associations Law and amendments in the Liberian Registry library. Distinguish incorporation from applicable domestic business, licensing, tax and sector requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.liscr.com/onlinelibrary/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian corporate and maritime legislation — Registry library</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Address entity status, transaction authority, duties and separate regulatory obligations. Link each conclusion to the relevant documents and local legal source.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Incorporation creates a recognised entity through the governing process. Directors exercise powers subject to duties and the organisation’s lawful framework. Corporate compliance concerns obligations beyond simply possessing incorporation documents.</li><li>Separate incorporation from director’s authority.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Incorporation</td><td>Creation of an entity through a legally recognised process</td></tr><tr><td>Director’s authority</td><td>Power to act within the governing corporate framework</td></tr><tr><td>Corporate compliance</td><td>Fulfilment of applicable ongoing and operational obligations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Incorporation</summary><p>Creation of an entity through a legally recognised process</p></details><details><summary>Director’s authority</summary><p>Power to act within the governing corporate framework</p></details><details><summary>Corporate compliance</summary><p>Fulfilment of applicable ongoing and operational obligations</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why does incorporation not settle permission to offer a regulated service?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Separate operating and professional requirements may apply</li><li>Incorporation automatically grants every licence</li><li>Only the founder’s confidence matters</li><li>Every registered company is exempt from tax</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Entity status and activity-specific compliance are distinct legal questions.</p></details><p><strong>A director contracts with their own business. What must be analysed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether the other directors like the proposal</li><li>Authority, conflict duties and applicable disclosure or approval</li><li>Only whether the company might profit</li><li>Only the director’s ownership percentage</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Related-party dealings require attention to powers and duties, not outcome alone.</p></details><p><strong>What supports an accountable corporate decision?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An undated verbal assurance</li><li>An incorporation certificate used as unlimited transaction authority</li><li>A record of the competent decision, basis and relevant conflict handling</li><li>A founder’s job title alone</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Decision records help establish who acted and under what lawful power.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Incorporation and Director’s authority.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Map a fictional company’s founder, shareholders, directors and officers to their distinct roles.</li><li>Audit a board minute for missing conflict disclosure, authority and decision evidence.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create a company-document checklist, a conflict-aware board resolution outline and a sourced compliance calendar for the education company.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M34:Partnership Liability, Agency Authority and Business Dissolution": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 34</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: PARTNERSHIP LIABILITY, AGENCY AUTHORITY AND BUSINESS DISSOLUTION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Corporations, Agency and Partnership</td><td>Partnership Liability, Agency Authority and Business Dissolution</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain partnership liability, agency authority and business dissolution using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A partnership is a legally relevant business relationship whose classification and consequences depend on governing law. Dissolution concerns ending the relationship; winding up concerns settling its affairs. Agency authority remains important when partners or representatives transact with third parties.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Partnership classification</td><td>Legal character of the business relationship</td></tr><tr><td>Dissolution</td><td>Ending the relationship according to the governing framework</td></tr><tr><td>Winding up</td><td>Settling outstanding affairs, assets and obligations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Examine how the enterprise was formed, its participants, contributions, business activity and governing agreement. A shared office or one joint project does not by itself establish every legal feature of a partnership. Distinguish actual authority, representations to outsiders and personal undertakings when identifying who is bound by a transaction.</p><p>Ending operations does not automatically erase unpaid obligations. Identify assets, contracts, liabilities, creditor rights and authority to complete remaining business. Record notices, pending work and final accounts under the applicable rules. A partner’s private withdrawal from a group message may differ from a legally effective change communicated as required. Separate internal allocation of loss from an outsider’s enforceable rights, and do not promise that a dissolution announcement cancels debts.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: three designers trade together in Monrovia. One orders printing supplies, another leaves and posts “the business is closed”, while customers still await work. The weak answer assumes the post eliminates all liability. The strong answer checks the legal relationship, ordering authority, remaining contracts and debts, and the steps needed for winding up. It distinguishes the partners’ internal disagreement from supplier and customer rights.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Find the applicable Liberian rules on partnerships, authority and winding up. Examine the arrangement and transaction rather than assuming one foreign partnership form applies.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.liscr.com/onlinelibrary/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian corporate and maritime legislation — Registry library</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Establish the legal relationship and authority first. Then distinguish dissolution, continuing third-party obligations and the lawful process for settling the enterprise’s affairs.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A partnership is a legally relevant business relationship whose classification and consequences depend on governing law. Dissolution concerns ending the relationship; winding up concerns settling its affairs. Agency authority remains important when partners or representatives transact with third parties.</li><li>Separate partnership classification from dissolution.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Partnership classification</td><td>Legal character of the business relationship</td></tr><tr><td>Dissolution</td><td>Ending the relationship according to the governing framework</td></tr><tr><td>Winding up</td><td>Settling outstanding affairs, assets and obligations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Partnership classification</summary><p>Legal character of the business relationship</p></details><details><summary>Dissolution</summary><p>Ending the relationship according to the governing framework</p></details><details><summary>Winding up</summary><p>Settling outstanding affairs, assets and obligations</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why does announcing closure not automatically cancel business debts?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A closed office proves every debt was paid</li><li>Outstanding obligations and winding-up duties still require legal resolution</li><li>All creditors lose rights once a post is published</li><li>Internal disagreements repeal contracts</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Dissolution and discharge of third-party obligations are distinct matters.</p></details><p><strong>What distinguishes internal sharing of a loss from liability to a supplier?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Every partner has identical liability in all legal forms</li><li>A supplier must accept any later private allocation</li><li>The supplier’s rights require their own governing legal and authority analysis</li><li>An internal agreement always eliminates outsider rights</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Internal arrangements and externally enforceable obligations may differ.</p></details><p><strong>A participant privately withdraws from the venture. What should be checked?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether any message automatically erases all responsibility</li><li>Whether withdrawal converts every contract into a gift</li><li>Whether remaining participants may ignore all records</li><li>The applicable requirements and effect on existing and later transactions</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> The legal effect of withdrawal depends on the relationship, process and relevant transaction timing.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Partnership classification and Dissolution.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Allocate the designers’ unfinished transactions to an authorised winding-up task list.</li><li>Argue a supplier’s position separately from the participants’ proposed internal loss-sharing arrangement.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a dissolution-and-winding-up plan with an authority matrix, creditor list, outstanding work record and issues requiring verified local advice.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M35:Sale of Goods, Consumer Contracts and Commercial Risk": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 35</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: SALE OF GOODS, CONSUMER CONTRACTS AND COMMERCIAL RISK</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Commercial Payments and Security</td><td>Sale of Goods, Consumer Contracts and Commercial Risk</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain sale of goods, consumer contracts and commercial risk using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A sale-of-goods transaction concerns specified goods and obligations under the applicable contract and law. Title, possession and risk of loss are different concepts. Inspection, conformity and remedies require attention to the actual delivery and contractual framework.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Title</td><td>Legal ownership, distinct from possession</td></tr><tr><td>Risk of loss</td><td>Allocation of responsibility for specified loss under governing rules</td></tr><tr><td>Conformity</td><td>Whether supplied goods meet legally relevant obligations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify whether the transaction concerns goods, services or a mixture and which rules apply. Establish description, quantity, quality, price, delivery terms and any legally relevant warranties or conditions. A transfer of possession does not always resolve title or allocate every risk. Read the governing provisions and agreement together before concluding who bears a loss in transit.</p><p>Document delivery, inspection, acceptance or rejection and the alleged non-conformity. Distinguish a damaged item from a late but conforming delivery and separate the remedy each allegation supports. A consumer’s practical vulnerability may be relevant to applicable protections, but dissatisfaction alone does not create an unlimited refund rule. Preserve purchase records, notices, condition evidence and efforts to limit further loss. Any proposed risk clause needs its own incorporation and legal-effect analysis.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a cooperative orders solar batteries delivered to Tubmanburg. Some arrive damaged; the seller says risk passed when the carrier collected them, while the cooperative cites a delivery promise. A weak answer assumes physical collection settles every issue. A strong answer identifies the applicable sales framework, agreed delivery and risk terms, carrier records and inspection evidence, then separately analyses conformity and available remedies.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Liberian Commercial Code provisions applicable to the transaction. Verify any separate consumer or sector rules rather than importing foreign sales legislation.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.moci.gov.lr/sites/default/files/documents/Commercial%20Code%20-%20Title%207%20-%20Liberian%20Code%20of%20Laws%20Revised.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian Commercial Code 2010, Title 7 — Ministry of Commerce and Industry (check amendments)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Identify the transaction’s classification and obligations, then separate title, possession, risk, conformity and remedies. Use a timeline to connect each disputed event to the governing rule.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A sale-of-goods transaction concerns specified goods and obligations under the applicable contract and law. Title, possession and risk of loss are different concepts. Inspection, conformity and remedies require attention to the actual delivery and contractual framework.</li><li>Separate title from risk of loss.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Title</td><td>Legal ownership, distinct from possession</td></tr><tr><td>Risk of loss</td><td>Allocation of responsibility for specified loss under governing rules</td></tr><tr><td>Conformity</td><td>Whether supplied goods meet legally relevant obligations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Title</summary><p>Legal ownership, distinct from possession</p></details><details><summary>Risk of loss</summary><p>Allocation of responsibility for specified loss under governing rules</p></details><details><summary>Conformity</summary><p>Whether supplied goods meet legally relevant obligations</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why does carrier collection not by itself settle responsibility for damaged batteries?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Every buyer bears all loss before delivery</li><li>A delivery promise is never legally relevant</li><li>Risk depends on the applicable law and agreed terms, distinct from possession</li><li>The carrier becomes owner of every shipment</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Title, possession and risk must be analysed separately.</p></details><p><strong>What supports a claim that delivered goods were non-conforming?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The buyer’s dissatisfaction alone</li><li>Only the seller’s advertisement colour</li><li>A foreign automatic-refund slogan</li><li>The agreed or legally required standard and reliable condition evidence</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> A conformity claim connects the actual obligation to the supplied goods.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish damaged goods from late delivery?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The breaches, evidence and available remedies may differ</li><li>Only damaged goods can involve a contract</li><li>Delay automatically proves all quality defects</li><li>Both claims always produce an identical award</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Different obligations require separate application and remedy analysis.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Title and Risk of loss.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Allocate fictional shipment losses under two different clearly stated contract clauses, reserving local enforceability questions.</li><li>Prepare an inspection record that separates observations from assumptions about when damage occurred.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create a delivery-and-risk timeline and a 1,000-word sales remedies memorandum. Include the exact contract wording and missing carrier or inspection records.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M35:Insolvency, Creditors and Priority of Claims": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 35</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: INSOLVENCY, CREDITORS AND PRIORITY OF CLAIMS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Commercial Payments and Security</td><td>Insolvency, Creditors and Priority of Claims</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain insolvency, creditors and priority of claims using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Financial distress may involve inability to meet obligations or another legally defined insolvency condition. A secured claim differs from an unsecured claim. Reorganisation, liquidation and ordinary individual debt enforcement serve different purposes under their governing regimes.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Secured claim</td><td>Claim supported by a legally effective security interest</td></tr><tr><td>Priority</td><td>Legally determined ranking for specified purposes</td></tr><tr><td>Collective process</td><td>Procedure addressing the debtor’s affairs beyond one creditor’s demand</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Start by identifying the debtor’s legal form, financial facts and applicable process. A cash shortage, a disputed debt and statutory insolvency are not identical. Examine assets, liabilities, security documents and any current proceeding before recommending an individual collection route. A priority claim must arise from the governing law, not simply from the creditor’s urgency.</p><p>For security, distinguish attachment or underlying validity, required registration or other perfection steps, priority and lawful enforcement. A creditor’s entry in a register is important but does not replace every underlying requirement. A collective proceeding may affect individual remedies, transactions and distribution; verify the actual rules rather than importing a foreign “waterfall”. Consider transactions benefiting insiders, related-party payments and available review while describing unproved wrongdoing as an allegation.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a small manufacturer cannot pay suppliers and wages, while a lender claims equipment security and an owner has recently withdrawn funds. A weak answer pays creditors in the order they complain. A strong answer identifies the applicable process, verifies assets and the security claim, checks legally recognised priorities and examines the owner’s transaction. It does not assume either complete lender priority or equal distribution before the governing conditions are established.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the Commercial Code and Associations Law materials relevant to the debtor and proceeding. Confirm any current insolvency or sector-specific framework before asserting a priority or enforcement power.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.moci.gov.lr/sites/default/files/documents/Commercial%20Code%20-%20Title%207%20-%20Liberian%20Code%20of%20Laws%20Revised.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian Commercial Code 2010, Title 7 — Ministry of Commerce and Industry (check amendments)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Identify the debtor and process, validate each asserted right and source any ranking rule. Avoid replacing the local framework with a memorised foreign distribution order.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Financial distress may involve inability to meet obligations or another legally defined insolvency condition. A secured claim differs from an unsecured claim. Reorganisation, liquidation and ordinary individual debt enforcement serve different purposes under their governing regimes.</li><li>Separate secured claim from priority.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Secured claim</td><td>Claim supported by a legally effective security interest</td></tr><tr><td>Priority</td><td>Legally determined ranking for specified purposes</td></tr><tr><td>Collective process</td><td>Procedure addressing the debtor’s affairs beyond one creditor’s demand</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Secured claim</summary><p>Claim supported by a legally effective security interest</p></details><details><summary>Priority</summary><p>Legally determined ranking for specified purposes</p></details><details><summary>Collective process</summary><p>Procedure addressing the debtor’s affairs beyond one creditor’s demand</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why does a creditor’s urgency not establish legal priority?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The first complaint always receives payment first</li><li>Every secured notice guarantees unlimited recovery</li><li>A debtor may choose to ignore all creditors</li><li>Ranking comes from the applicable law and legally effective rights</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Priority requires an authority-based examination of the claim and process.</p></details><p><strong>What must be checked beyond a collateral registry entry?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The underlying agreement, validity, scope and legally relevant priority conditions</li><li>Only the registry logo</li><li>A rule that registration cures every defect</li><li>Whether the lender wants the asset urgently</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Registration and the creation or scope of the secured right are separate questions.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish a cash shortage from statutory insolvency?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Only creditors may define insolvency privately</li><li>The applicable legal condition and process require their own criteria</li><li>Any delayed invoice proves every insolvency requirement</li><li>A profitable product eliminates every debt issue</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Financial facts must be tested against the governing legal framework.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Secured claim and Priority.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Build a fictional creditor schedule that distinguishes disputed, secured and unsecured claims without prematurely ranking them.</li><li>Compare an owner withdrawal with an ordinary supplier payment and list the facts required for a lawful review.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a creditor-and-assets schedule, a security-validation checklist and a 900-word options note comparing individual enforcement with an applicable collective route.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M36:Public Procurement, Concessions and Accountability": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 36</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: PUBLIC PROCUREMENT, CONCESSIONS AND ACCOUNTABILITY</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Administrative Law, Legislation and Tax</td><td>Public Procurement, Concessions and Accountability</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain public procurement, concessions and accountability using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Public procurement concerns acquisition through a legally regulated public process. A concession may grant specified rights or responsibilities under a distinct arrangement. Accountability requires traceable authority, fair procedures and a record connecting criteria to decisions.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Procurement</td><td>Regulated public acquisition process</td></tr><tr><td>Concession</td><td>Distinct grant or arrangement requiring its own applicable analysis</td></tr><tr><td>Evaluation record</td><td>Documented application of lawful criteria to submitted evidence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the procuring entity, authorised decision-makers, transaction type and governing instrument. Distinguish planning, solicitation, evaluation, award and contract administration. A procurement’s lawful objective does not remove its process requirements. Set criteria transparently as required, assess responsive evidence against those criteria and record the reasons for decisions.</p><p>Competition and disclosure rules must be read with lawful exceptions rather than reduced to “always choose the lowest price”. An emergency or single-source method requires its own legal basis and supporting facts. Examine conflicts, communications, approval records and the applicable complaint or review process. A concession involving a public resource may raise additional rights, consultation and sector questions. Avoid quoting an outdated numeric threshold without verifying current regulations.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a public office seeks classroom furniture and one evaluator has a financial interest in a bidder. A cheaper offer also fails a stated specification. A weak answer selects it solely on price or privately awards the contract to the interested evaluator’s preferred firm. A strong answer identifies the current rules, documented requirements, conflict handling and lawful evaluation method; explains the proposed decision with evidence; and preserves the review record.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the PPCC’s official legal documents, including its 2026 instruments, and verify commencement and applicability to the transaction. Do not rely solely on the earlier 2010 framework.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.ppcc.gov.lr/publications/document-type/legal-documents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Current procurement legal documents, including 2026 instruments — PPCC Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Map each decision to its competent actor, governing requirement and record. Analyse conflicts and exceptions explicitly, and distinguish an award decision from contract performance.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Public procurement concerns acquisition through a legally regulated public process. A concession may grant specified rights or responsibilities under a distinct arrangement. Accountability requires traceable authority, fair procedures and a record connecting criteria to decisions.</li><li>Separate procurement from concession.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Procurement</td><td>Regulated public acquisition process</td></tr><tr><td>Concession</td><td>Distinct grant or arrangement requiring its own applicable analysis</td></tr><tr><td>Evaluation record</td><td>Documented application of lawful criteria to submitted evidence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Procurement</summary><p>Regulated public acquisition process</p></details><details><summary>Concession</summary><p>Distinct grant or arrangement requiring its own applicable analysis</p></details><details><summary>Evaluation record</summary><p>Documented application of lawful criteria to submitted evidence</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why is “always choose the lowest price” an incomplete procurement rule?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The lawful criteria, responsiveness and governing procedure must also be applied</li><li>Price can never be considered</li><li>An evaluator’s private interest controls the award</li><li>Specifications become irrelevant after bids arrive</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Value and lawful responsiveness require more than an isolated price comparison.</p></details><p><strong>An evaluator has an interest in a bidder. What should be addressed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A rule that all public officers may privately select relatives</li><li>The conflict and applicable disclosure or participation safeguards</li><li>Only whether the bidder is cheap</li><li>Only the evaluator’s seniority</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Decision integrity requires handling the relevant conflict through the governing process.</p></details><p><strong>A public office claims an emergency exception. What is required?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A foreign threshold copied as local law</li><li>A promise that no record will be kept</li><li>The current legal basis and evidence satisfying the exception’s conditions</li><li>An emergency label that automatically removes all safeguards</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Exceptions must be supported under the applicable framework.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Procurement and Concession.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Evaluate three fictional bids against a stated lawful criteria matrix without changing criteria afterward.</li><li>Build an audit trail identifying approvals, conflict records, reasons and available complaint steps.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Produce an evaluation matrix, conflict-handling note and 900-word accountability memorandum using the current official PPCC source list.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M36:Tax Administration, Assessments and Objections": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 36</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: TAX ADMINISTRATION, ASSESSMENTS AND OBJECTIONS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Administrative Law, Legislation and Tax</td><td>Tax Administration, Assessments and Objections</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain tax administration, assessments and objections using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>An assessment determines a tax obligation through the applicable legal process. An objection challenges a specified aspect through an authorised route. Tax liability, administrative penalty, interest and enforcement questions should be distinguished rather than added together without authority.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Assessment</td><td>Legally made determination of a tax obligation</td></tr><tr><td>Objection</td><td>Authorised challenge to a specified determination</td></tr><tr><td>Reconciliation</td><td>Connection between source records, figures and asserted adjustments</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Start with the legal basis, relevant taxpayer and taxable facts. A business’s gross receipts, taxable income and available deductions are different concepts determined by the applicable regime. Reconcile source records to the assessment and identify whether the disagreement concerns facts, classification, calculation or interpretation. Each claim needs evidence and a relevant legal rule.</p><p>An objection should identify the challenged determination, period, grounds, supporting documents and requested correction. Verify current requirements and any effect on payment or enforcement; do not assume every challenge automatically suspends collection. Preserve correspondence and proof of filing, and identify the next review stage through the governing local process. Avoid asserting an exemption, deduction or rate from a foreign tutorial or an obsolete summary.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a shop in Voinjama receives an assessment including sales it says were cancelled and goods it says were purchased for resale. A weak response simply refuses payment. A strong response reconciles the invoices, cancellation and purchase records, identifies the applicable tax rules, explains each disputed adjustment and follows the verified objection route. It keeps the shop’s factual account separate from an established entitlement to a deduction.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the Liberia Revenue Authority tax-law and amendment collection. Verify the taxpayer, relevant period, tax type, assessment basis and current objection procedure.</p><p><a href=\"https://revenue.lra.gov.lr/tax-laws-and-amendments/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Tax laws and amendments — Liberia Revenue Authority</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>State the tax type and period, separate disputed adjustments and apply the exact local provisions to reconciled records. Identify procedural uncertainty before giving a remedial conclusion.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>An assessment determines a tax obligation through the applicable legal process. An objection challenges a specified aspect through an authorised route. Tax liability, administrative penalty, interest and enforcement questions should be distinguished rather than added together without authority.</li><li>Separate assessment from objection.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Assessment</td><td>Legally made determination of a tax obligation</td></tr><tr><td>Objection</td><td>Authorised challenge to a specified determination</td></tr><tr><td>Reconciliation</td><td>Connection between source records, figures and asserted adjustments</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Assessment</summary><p>Legally made determination of a tax obligation</p></details><details><summary>Objection</summary><p>Authorised challenge to a specified determination</p></details><details><summary>Reconciliation</summary><p>Connection between source records, figures and asserted adjustments</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What improves an objection to cancelled sales in an assessment?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An assumption that every invoice cancellation creates an exemption</li><li>Records identifying the transactions and the applicable treatment</li><li>A blanket refusal with no reasons</li><li>A foreign tax rate copied into the response</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> A supported objection links the disputed facts to the governing tax rule.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish tax, penalty and interest?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A tax objection automatically abolishes every penalty</li><li>Only interest can ever be disputed</li><li>Each component requires its own applicable basis and calculation</li><li>They are always identical figures</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Different components may raise different factual and legal issues.</p></details><p><strong>What must be checked before assuming an objection stops collection?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The taxpayer’s personal expectation alone</li><li>A universal rule that all challenges suspend payment</li><li>The fact that a letter has been drafted</li><li>The effect provided by the applicable local procedure and any relevant order</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> An objection’s procedural and enforcement consequences require verification.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Assessment and Objection.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Reconcile a fictional ledger with an assessment and mark factual versus legal disagreements.</li><li>Draft a concise objection paragraph that links one claimed adjustment to its supporting records and governing provision.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a tax-record reconciliation and a fictional objection outline with grounds, sources, evidence, requested correction and a verified procedural checklist.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M37:Regional Human-Rights Systems and Complaint Procedures": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 37</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: REGIONAL HUMAN-RIGHTS SYSTEMS AND COMPLAINT PROCEDURES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Human Rights and International Law</td><td>Regional Human-Rights Systems and Complaint Procedures</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain regional human-rights systems and complaint procedures using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Regional human-rights mechanisms supervise obligations within their particular legal instruments. A communication to a commission differs from an ordinary domestic lawsuit. Jurisdiction, admissibility, merits and remedy are separate stages of analysis.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jurisdiction</td><td>The body’s competence over the relevant claim</td></tr><tr><td>Admissibility</td><td>Conditions for considering a communication</td></tr><tr><td>Merits</td><td>Whether the alleged violation is established under the governing instrument</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the right, alleged conduct, responsible state and applicable instrument. Determine which regional body is competent and what legal relationship makes the claim relevant to it. Do not confuse the African Commission with the African Court or assume every institution has identical access rules. Verify party-specific treaty and declaration status where relevant.</p><p>A factual complaint should distinguish observed events, allegations, sources and the legal characterisation proposed. Analyse domestic remedies and any recognised exceptions under the applicable guidance; “international” does not mean domestic procedures never matter. Record other proceedings, relevant timing, identification and requested measures accurately. An admissible complaint has not automatically succeeded on the merits, and a recommendation or decision must be described according to the issuing body’s actual powers.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a community alleges a public restriction violated a protected right and wishes to “send it to the African Court” immediately. A weak answer assumes any regional body must hear it. A strong response identifies the governing right and evidence, distinguishes institutions and their access conditions, researches available domestic remedies and the relevant admissibility rules, and prepares a balanced communication outline without guaranteeing a finding.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the African Commission’s official communication guidance and current rules. Verify the relevant treaty status, competent institution and admissibility conditions for Liberia.</p><p><a href=\"https://achpr.au.int/en/guidelines-submitting-complaints\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Communication guidance — African Commission on Human and Peoples’ Rights</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Analyse the institution, instrument, jurisdiction, admissibility and merits in sequence. Keep the domestic-remedies record and requested measures distinct from an unsupported promise of relief.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Regional human-rights mechanisms supervise obligations within their particular legal instruments. A communication to a commission differs from an ordinary domestic lawsuit. Jurisdiction, admissibility, merits and remedy are separate stages of analysis.</li><li>Separate jurisdiction from admissibility.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jurisdiction</td><td>The body’s competence over the relevant claim</td></tr><tr><td>Admissibility</td><td>Conditions for considering a communication</td></tr><tr><td>Merits</td><td>Whether the alleged violation is established under the governing instrument</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Jurisdiction</summary><p>The body’s competence over the relevant claim</p></details><details><summary>Admissibility</summary><p>Conditions for considering a communication</p></details><details><summary>Merits</summary><p>Whether the alleged violation is established under the governing instrument</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why distinguish the African Commission from the African Court?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Every complaint automatically becomes a domestic appeal</li><li>Only the complainant’s preferred institution matters</li><li>Their powers, access conditions and procedures are not interchangeable</li><li>Their names create identical jurisdiction</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Regional institutions must be analysed under their own governing instruments.</p></details><p><strong>What should an account of domestic remedies contain?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A blanket claim that international filing removes every local requirement</li><li>A fabricated court result</li><li>Only the community’s dissatisfaction</li><li>The actual procedures used, outcomes and legally relevant exceptions to investigate</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Admissibility analysis requires a verifiable remedies record and applicable conditions.</p></details><p><strong>What does an admissibility conclusion not establish automatically?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Success on the merits of the alleged violation</li><li>The need to identify the competent body</li><li>The relevance of procedural conditions</li><li>The importance of accurate facts</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Permission to consider a complaint differs from deciding that a violation occurred.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Jurisdiction and Admissibility.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Compare two regional bodies on a powers-and-access table using official sources.</li><li>Turn an emotional fictional rights account into a sourced chronology that labels allegations and established facts.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a fictional regional communication outline with a jurisdiction and admissibility checklist, domestic-remedies chronology, evidence table and precise requested measures.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M37:Conflict of Laws, Jurisdiction and Foreign Judgments": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 37</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CONFLICT OF LAWS, JURISDICTION AND FOREIGN JUDGMENTS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Human Rights and International Law</td><td>Conflict of Laws, Jurisdiction and Foreign Judgments</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain conflict of laws, jurisdiction and foreign judgments using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Conflict of laws addresses which forum and law apply to a private dispute with cross-border connections and how foreign decisions are recognised or enforced. Governing law, jurisdiction and enforcement are distinct questions.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Governing law</td><td>Law used to determine specified substantive issues</td></tr><tr><td>Jurisdiction</td><td>Competence of the proposed forum</td></tr><tr><td>Recognition and enforcement</td><td>Legal effect and execution of a foreign decision through the applicable route</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Map the parties, places of formation and performance, location of property, relevant events and available agreement clauses. A choice-of-law clause does not necessarily confer jurisdiction on a particular court, and a forum clause must be tested for its scope and legal effect. Mandatory rules or other recognised limits may remain relevant under the governing framework.</p><p>A foreign judgment’s existence does not establish its immediate enforceability against assets in Liberia. Identify the competent recognition or enforcement route and applicable conditions, including legally relevant finality, jurisdiction, notice or other grounds to examine. Do not retry the merits through a process that does not authorise it. Keep the authentic judgment, service record, contract and asset information separate. International arbitration awards require their own analysis rather than being labelled ordinary foreign court judgments.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a Liberian importer and a foreign supplier choose the supplier’s national law but say nothing about forum. A foreign judgment is later presented against property in Monrovia. A weak answer assumes the law clause settles every court and enforcement question. A strong answer separates governing law, forum competence and recognition, obtains the actual judgment and notice record, and investigates the authorised local route before predicting whether an asset can lawfully be reached.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Research the relevant Liberian statutes, judgments and treaty status for the cross-border transaction. A foreign choice-of-law rule is comparative until its local relevance is established.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.moci.gov.lr/sites/default/files/documents/Commercial%20Code%20-%20Title%207%20-%20Liberian%20Code%20of%20Laws%20Revised.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian Commercial Code 2010, Title 7 — Ministry of Commerce and Industry (check amendments)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Use separate headings for forum, governing law and recognition. Identify the transaction’s connections and the local source governing each question.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Conflict of laws addresses which forum and law apply to a private dispute with cross-border connections and how foreign decisions are recognised or enforced. Governing law, jurisdiction and enforcement are distinct questions.</li><li>Separate governing law from jurisdiction.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Governing law</td><td>Law used to determine specified substantive issues</td></tr><tr><td>Jurisdiction</td><td>Competence of the proposed forum</td></tr><tr><td>Recognition and enforcement</td><td>Legal effect and execution of a foreign decision through the applicable route</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Governing law</summary><p>Law used to determine specified substantive issues</p></details><details><summary>Jurisdiction</summary><p>Competence of the proposed forum</p></details><details><summary>Recognition and enforcement</summary><p>Legal effect and execution of a foreign decision through the applicable route</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why does a governing-law clause not necessarily choose a court?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Every law clause automatically names a particular courtroom</li><li>Courts cannot hear cross-border disputes</li><li>A contract eliminates all mandatory procedural rules</li><li>Substantive law and forum competence are distinct questions</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Choice of law does not by itself settle jurisdiction.</p></details><p><strong>A creditor holds a foreign judgment against assets in Monrovia. What comes next?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Investigate the competent local recognition or enforcement process</li><li>Private seizure based solely on the foreign document</li><li>Automatic reopening of every merits issue</li><li>An assumption that all foreign decisions are void</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Foreign judgments require analysis of their local legal effect and authorised enforcement.</p></details><p><strong>Which record helps examine fair notice in the foreign proceeding?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The debtor’s nationality alone</li><li>The authentic service and participation record</li><li>The creditor’s preferred summary alone</li><li>A copied unrelated contract</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Recognition conditions must be tested against evidence of the actual proceeding.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Governing law and Jurisdiction.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Map a fictional transaction’s country connections without assuming one resolves every issue.</li><li>Compare two contract clauses to identify whether each addresses law, forum, both or neither.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,000-word cross-border issues memo and draft separate governing-law and forum clauses, explaining limits that drafting alone cannot settle.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M38:Bills of Lading, Charterparties and Maritime Claims": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 38</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: BILLS OF LADING, CHARTERPARTIES AND MARITIME CLAIMS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Maritime and Insurance Law</td><td>Bills of Lading, Charterparties and Maritime Claims</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain bills of lading, charterparties and maritime claims using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A bill of lading can serve legally relevant receipt, contract-evidence and document functions depending on its terms and governing law. A charterparty concerns an arrangement for use or employment of a vessel. Cargo interests, carriers, owners and charterers may occupy different legal roles.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bill of lading</td><td>Transport document with functions determined by law and wording</td></tr><tr><td>Charterparty</td><td>Contract concerning use or employment of a vessel</td></tr><tr><td>Carrier responsibility</td><td>Obligations of the legally relevant carriage party</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the actual contracting parties and documents before allocating responsibility. A ship’s flag does not alone answer who promised carriage or bears a particular cargo loss. Read the bill’s descriptions, signatures, reservations and incorporated terms alongside the charter arrangement where relevant. Distinguish ownership of a vessel from performance of carriage obligations.</p><p>For a claim, establish cargo condition, receipt, handling, delivery and the alleged breach, with a timeline supported by records. Examine notice, limitation, defences and forum only under the applicable framework and verified terms; foreign seminar examples are not local deadlines. A charter dispute about hire or use of the vessel may differ from a cargo claimant’s rights. Keep each party’s obligation and each loss separate to avoid turning every shipping participant into an identical defendant.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: equipment arrives at the Freeport damaged. The importer holds a bill of lading signed for a carrier, while a separate owner-charterer dispute concerns delayed hire. A weak answer treats the flag-state registry as the contracting carrier. A strong answer maps the bill and charter parties, checks receipt and delivery condition records, identifies the importer’s actual contractual route and reserves any notice or limitation conclusion pending the governing documents and law.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Liberian Maritime Law and current relevant materials in the Registry library. Check the contract and applicable international instrument rather than assuming foreign shipping rules control.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.liscr.com/onlinelibrary/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian corporate and maritime legislation — Registry library</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Map documents and parties before naming a cause of action. Apply the governing carriage or charter rules to the supported timeline and keep separate contractual claims distinct.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A bill of lading can serve legally relevant receipt, contract-evidence and document functions depending on its terms and governing law. A charterparty concerns an arrangement for use or employment of a vessel. Cargo interests, carriers, owners and charterers may occupy different legal roles.</li><li>Separate bill of lading from charterparty.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bill of lading</td><td>Transport document with functions determined by law and wording</td></tr><tr><td>Charterparty</td><td>Contract concerning use or employment of a vessel</td></tr><tr><td>Carrier responsibility</td><td>Obligations of the legally relevant carriage party</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Bill of lading</summary><p>Transport document with functions determined by law and wording</p></details><details><summary>Charterparty</summary><p>Contract concerning use or employment of a vessel</p></details><details><summary>Carrier responsibility</summary><p>Obligations of the legally relevant carriage party</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why does a vessel’s flag not alone identify the contractual carrier?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Regulatory registration and carriage obligations are distinct legal connections</li><li>The flag automatically signs every bill of lading</li><li>All owners, charterers and carriers have identical obligations</li><li>A ship’s nationality cancels all contracts</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Maritime responsibility requires identification of the actual role and legal undertaking.</p></details><p><strong>What should be obtained before asserting a cargo notice deadline?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A universal deadline assumed for all shipping</li><li>The applicable local or international framework and relevant contract terms</li><li>Only a foreign seminar’s example</li><li>Only the importer’s recollection</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Notice and limitation depend on the governing rules and transaction.</p></details><p><strong>Why separate an owner-charterer hire dispute from a cargo claim?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A charter disagreement proves cargo damage</li><li>The registry is automatically responsible for both</li><li>Different parties, obligations and remedies may be involved</li><li>Cargo interests always owe the vessel’s hire</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Separate maritime relationships require their own legal analysis.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Bill of lading and Charterparty.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Allocate fictional owner, carrier, charterer and cargo interests to the documents they signed or received.</li><li>Compare cargo-condition records at receipt and delivery, identifying what they prove and what they leave uncertain.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a shipping roles map, cargo-condition timeline and 900-word claim outline distinguishing the importer’s issue from the charterer’s dispute.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M38:Insurance Claims, Disclosure and Subrogation": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 38</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: INSURANCE CLAIMS, DISCLOSURE AND SUBROGATION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Maritime and Insurance Law</td><td>Insurance Claims, Disclosure and Subrogation</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain insurance claims, disclosure and subrogation using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>An insurance claim seeks the benefit provided by an applicable policy for an insured event. Indemnity compensates a covered loss within its terms. Subrogation concerns an insurer’s legally recognised recovery position after payment; it is distinct from unlimited ownership of every insured claim.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Risk and benefit within the insuring terms and applicable law</td></tr><tr><td>Indemnity</td><td>Compensation of covered loss within the relevant framework</td></tr><tr><td>Subrogation</td><td>Legally recognised recovery position linked to an insurer’s payment</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Analyse the insuring clause, subject matter, period, conditions, exclusions and alleged event in sequence. Premium payment alone does not establish that every loss is covered. Identify what was disclosed at formation and what information or notice the policy and law require for the claim. A disputed omission should be tested under the governing standard rather than treated as automatic forfeiture.</p><p>For recovery after payment, distinguish the insured’s loss, insurer’s payment and any remaining claim against another party. Avoid double recovery for the same compensated loss. Subrogation and contribution address different relationships and require their own conditions. Preserve the full policy, proposal information, incident record, loss calculation and correspondence. Do not assume that a foreign health-insurance lien operates identically under a Liberian property policy.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a delivery business claims for a damaged vehicle after an insured collision and also seeks payment from the other driver. The insurer disputes a policy condition and later offers a partial payment. A weak answer adds both demands together as unrestricted profit. A strong answer investigates coverage and conditions, reconciles the actual loss and payment, and examines any lawful recovery rights while separating compensated and uncompensated amounts.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Liberia Insurance Act and the relevant policy. Verify current regulation, coverage terms and applicable local authority before asserting a claim or recovery right.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.cbl.org.lr/sites/default/files/documents/INSURANCE_ACT.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberia Insurance Act 2013 — Central Bank of Liberia (check current regulations)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Analyse coverage first, then the claim conditions and quantum, and finally recovery after payment. Keep policy interpretation separate from an assumed entitlement based on expectation.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>An insurance claim seeks the benefit provided by an applicable policy for an insured event. Indemnity compensates a covered loss within its terms. Subrogation concerns an insurer’s legally recognised recovery position after payment; it is distinct from unlimited ownership of every insured claim.</li><li>Separate coverage from indemnity.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Risk and benefit within the insuring terms and applicable law</td></tr><tr><td>Indemnity</td><td>Compensation of covered loss within the relevant framework</td></tr><tr><td>Subrogation</td><td>Legally recognised recovery position linked to an insurer’s payment</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Coverage</summary><p>Risk and benefit within the insuring terms and applicable law</p></details><details><summary>Indemnity</summary><p>Compensation of covered loss within the relevant framework</p></details><details><summary>Subrogation</summary><p>Legally recognised recovery position linked to an insurer’s payment</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why does paying a premium not prove coverage for every event?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Payment eliminates all policy wording</li><li>The insuring clause, conditions, exclusions and applicable law define the benefit</li><li>All insurance covers every possible loss</li><li>Only the claimant’s expectation controls coverage</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Coverage requires the relevant event and claim to fall within the governing terms.</p></details><p><strong>What must be reconciled when both an insurer and another party may pay?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Only which payer replies first</li><li>A universal rule that the insured owns every subrogation right</li><li>The actual loss, insurance payment and any remaining recoverable amount</li><li>A right to receive the full same loss repeatedly</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Recovery analysis must distinguish compensated and uncompensated loss.</p></details><p><strong>A disclosure omission is alleged. What should be examined?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Automatic forfeiture for any missing word</li><li>Automatic coverage despite every relevant omission</li><li>A foreign insurer’s preferred practice as Liberian law</li><li>The governing disclosure rule, material facts and legal consequence</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> The effect of an omission depends on the applicable legal and contractual framework.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Coverage and Indemnity.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Annotate a fictional policy into insuring clause, conditions, exclusions and claim procedure.</li><li>Prepare a loss-and-payment ledger that prevents counting the same compensated loss twice.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a coverage-and-claim checklist and a recovery reconciliation showing loss, policy limits, payment, remaining loss and rights requiring verification.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M39:Environmental Impact Assessment and Regulatory Enforcement": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 39</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: ENVIRONMENTAL IMPACT ASSESSMENT AND REGULATORY ENFORCEMENT</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Environmental and Intellectual Property Law</td><td>Environmental Impact Assessment and Regulatory Enforcement</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain environmental impact assessment and regulatory enforcement using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Environmental assessment investigates a proposed activity’s impacts and alternatives through the governing process. An authorisation may impose conditions. Regulatory enforcement addresses compliance or breach using legally available powers and procedures.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Assessment</td><td>Structured examination of impacts and alternatives under the governing process</td></tr><tr><td>Authorisation condition</td><td>Continuing obligation attached to a lawful permission</td></tr><tr><td>Compliance evidence</td><td>Records connecting actual operations to applicable requirements</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the activity, location, affected resources and people, and the responsible proponent and regulator. Assessment is more than a report title: examine baseline information, significant effects, alternatives, mitigation, consultation and the decision process required by law. A land right or company registration does not replace environmental requirements.</p><p>Conditions and monitoring turn an approval into continuing responsibilities. Compare the authorised activity with actual operations and record the evidence of any alleged breach. Enforcement may involve directions, sanctions or proceedings only within the regulator’s lawful powers and procedure. A complaint should distinguish measured harm, allegations and legal questions; a photograph can raise a concern without proving every element. Preserve sampling and inspection provenance, and identify urgent lawful protection where needed without promising a predetermined penalty.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a small processing facility near a river changes its output after an assessment and residents report discoloured water. A weak answer assumes the old approval permits any expansion or that a photograph proves every violation. A strong answer checks the assessed and authorised activity, conditions, actual changes, inspection and sampling evidence, and the appropriate EPA process. It separates regulatory compliance from a possible private damages claim.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Liberia’s Environmental Protection and Management Law and current EPA requirements relevant to the activity. Check the applicable assessment, authorisation, consultation and monitoring process.</p><p><a href=\"https://epa.gov.lr/epa-documents/the-environmental-protection-and-management-law-of-liberia/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Environmental Protection and Management Law — EPA Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Identify the assessed and authorised activity, compare actual operations with conditions, and analyse the regulator’s powers and procedure separately from any civil remedy.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Environmental assessment investigates a proposed activity’s impacts and alternatives through the governing process. An authorisation may impose conditions. Regulatory enforcement addresses compliance or breach using legally available powers and procedures.</li><li>Separate assessment from authorisation condition.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Assessment</td><td>Structured examination of impacts and alternatives under the governing process</td></tr><tr><td>Authorisation condition</td><td>Continuing obligation attached to a lawful permission</td></tr><tr><td>Compliance evidence</td><td>Records connecting actual operations to applicable requirements</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Assessment</summary><p>Structured examination of impacts and alternatives under the governing process</p></details><details><summary>Authorisation condition</summary><p>Continuing obligation attached to a lawful permission</p></details><details><summary>Compliance evidence</summary><p>Records connecting actual operations to applicable requirements</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why does an existing assessment not automatically authorise every later expansion?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Land ownership replaces environmental controls</li><li>Residents’ views automatically amend the permit</li><li>The approved scope and applicable change requirements must be checked</li><li>Any report permits all future activity</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> The authorised activity and its conditions determine the compliance question.</p></details><p><strong>What strengthens an environmental complaint beyond a photograph?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A larger unverified penalty demand</li><li>An assumption that colour alone proves every breach</li><li>A copied foreign enforcement order</li><li>Traceable inspection, sampling or other relevant evidence linked to the alleged effect</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Reliable provenance and connection to the legal requirement support the claim.</p></details><p><strong>Why separate regulatory enforcement from a private damages claim?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Different powers, parties, elements and remedies may apply</li><li>An EPA concern automatically fixes all compensation</li><li>Only a company may complain about harm</li><li>A permit eliminates every civil issue</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Distinct legal routes require their own supporting analysis.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Assessment and Authorisation condition.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Compare a fictional proposed project, permit conditions and actual changed operations in a three-column matrix.</li><li>Design a lawful evidence log recording source, location, method and limitations of an environmental observation.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare an assessment-and-conditions checklist, monitoring evidence plan and 900-word regulatory memorandum for the facility’s changed activity.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M39:Copyright, Trademarks and Patents in Practice": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 39</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: COPYRIGHT, TRADEMARKS AND PATENTS IN PRACTICE</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Environmental and Intellectual Property Law</td><td>Copyright, Trademarks and Patents in Practice</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain copyright, trademarks and patents in practice using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Copyright concerns protected expression; a trademark identifies relevant commercial origin; a patent concerns a qualifying invention under its legal requirements. Ownership, registration, licensing and infringement are separate issues.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Copyright</td><td>Protection of qualifying expression under applicable law</td></tr><tr><td>Trademark</td><td>Sign identifying relevant commercial origin</td></tr><tr><td>Patent</td><td>Right concerning a qualifying invention under the governing system</td></tr><tr><td>Licence</td><td>Permission within defined scope and conditions</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the subject matter before choosing a legal category. A business logo may raise trademark and artistic-expression questions; an underlying idea is not the same as the particular expression of it. An invention’s patentability cannot be assumed from novelty in everyday conversation. Examine who created the material, any employment or commissioned-work arrangement and the governing ownership rules.</p><p>A licence must permit the intended use within its scope, duration, territory and conditions. Buying a physical object or finding a file online does not necessarily transfer intellectual-property rights. Registration and enforceability depend on the particular right and applicable law; do not claim every category has identical registration requirements. For infringement, compare the asserted right, challenged use, permission and recognised limits or exceptions. A credit line is not a substitute for an effective licence.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a startup uses a downloaded photograph, a similar competitor name and a new device design. A weak answer registers one company name and claims all IP issues are settled. A strong answer separates copyright, trademark and possible patent questions, investigates creators and permissions, searches the relevant records and tests each proposed protection or use against the Liberian framework.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Liberia’s Intellectual Property Act in WIPO Lex and check later local instruments, regulations and treaty status relevant to the issue. WIPO’s general guide helps distinguish categories, not establish every local entitlement.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.wipo.int/wipolex/en/legislation/details/16994\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberia Intellectual Property Act 2016 — WIPO Lex (check amendments)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Classify each asset, establish ownership and permissions, then analyse protection and challenged use under the local framework. Keep licence terms and exceptions distinct from credit or possession.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Copyright concerns protected expression; a trademark identifies relevant commercial origin; a patent concerns a qualifying invention under its legal requirements. Ownership, registration, licensing and infringement are separate issues.</li><li>Separate copyright from trademark.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Copyright</td><td>Protection of qualifying expression under applicable law</td></tr><tr><td>Trademark</td><td>Sign identifying relevant commercial origin</td></tr><tr><td>Patent</td><td>Right concerning a qualifying invention under the governing system</td></tr><tr><td>Licence</td><td>Permission within defined scope and conditions</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Copyright</summary><p>Protection of qualifying expression under applicable law</p></details><details><summary>Trademark</summary><p>Sign identifying relevant commercial origin</p></details><details><summary>Patent</summary><p>Right concerning a qualifying invention under the governing system</p></details><details><summary>Licence</summary><p>Permission within defined scope and conditions</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why does buying a photograph file not necessarily allow every commercial use?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Every download transfers all copyright</li><li>A credit line always replaces permission</li><li>Company registration makes every image public property</li><li>The rights and licence scope must be established separately from possession</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Ownership of a copy and permission to exploit protected material are distinct.</p></details><p><strong>What is the first step before choosing an IP protection strategy?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Identify the subject matter and applicable category of right</li><li>Assume all IP is patented</li><li>Register any name and ignore every other issue</li><li>Treat an idea and its expression as identical</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Different forms of intellectual property protect different legally defined subject matter.</p></details><p><strong>Why examine a commissioned-work agreement?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An invoice automatically grants a patent</li><li>Ownership and permitted use may depend on the applicable law and agreed rights</li><li>Payment always transfers every right in every jurisdiction</li><li>The creator’s identity is never relevant</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Creation, ownership and licensing require their own legal and factual analysis.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Copyright and Trademark.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Sort ten fictional assets into potential IP categories and explain overlap or missing information.</li><li>Audit a sample licence for intended use, attribution, territory, duration and modification conditions.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare an IP audit with separate ownership, permission, registration and infringement columns for the startup’s three assets.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M40:Conflicts of Interest, Confidentiality and Client Money": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 40</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CONFLICTS OF INTEREST, CONFIDENTIALITY AND CLIENT MONEY</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Legal Ethics and Trial Advocacy</td><td>Conflicts of Interest, Confidentiality and Client Money</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain conflicts of interest, confidentiality and client money using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A conflict involves interests or duties that may impair proper professional responsibility. Confidentiality concerns protection of information. Client money is property handled for a client or another person, whose permitted use and records must be governed by the applicable duties.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Conflict</td><td>Competing interest or duty affecting proper responsibility</td></tr><tr><td>Informed consent</td><td>Consent meeting the applicable conditions where legally permitted</td></tr><tr><td>Client money</td><td>Property handled for another within governing authority and duties</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify who is or was the client, the matter, relevant duties and any personal or competing interest. A shared objective today does not guarantee that two parties’ interests will remain aligned. Determine whether an applicable rule permits informed consent or requires refusal or withdrawal; do not assume consent cures every conflict.</p><p>Confidentiality requires attention to recipients, channels, devices and indirect identification, not only removal of a name. Protect information learned in a professional relationship according to the governing duties and permitted exceptions. Funds held for another person cannot be treated as unrestricted working capital. Separate authority to receive money, permission to use it, accounting and repayment or disbursement. An emergency personal need does not create a right to borrow from a protected fund.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a lawyer is asked to advise both seller and buyer, while the buyer reveals a private concern about the seller’s documents. A deposit is received and the lawyer proposes using it temporarily for office rent. A weak answer accepts both arrangements because everyone trusts the lawyer. A strong answer separately checks conflicting duties, handling of information, any legally permissible consent and the authority and accounting for the deposit.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Judiciary’s Code of Moral and Professional Ethics and any applicable current rules. The exercises simulate professional issues; the course does not confer practising authority.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/code-of-moral-and-professional-ethic/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Code of Moral and Professional Ethics — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Separate the conflict, information and funds issues. Identify the applicable duty, available lawful response and a practical record showing how the duty will be met.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A conflict involves interests or duties that may impair proper professional responsibility. Confidentiality concerns protection of information. Client money is property handled for a client or another person, whose permitted use and records must be governed by the applicable duties.</li><li>Separate conflict from informed consent.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Conflict</td><td>Competing interest or duty affecting proper responsibility</td></tr><tr><td>Informed consent</td><td>Consent meeting the applicable conditions where legally permitted</td></tr><tr><td>Client money</td><td>Property handled for another within governing authority and duties</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Conflict</summary><p>Competing interest or duty affecting proper responsibility</p></details><details><summary>Informed consent</summary><p>Consent meeting the applicable conditions where legally permitted</p></details><details><summary>Client money</summary><p>Property handled for another within governing authority and duties</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why does trust between the parties not eliminate a professional conflict?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Duties and competing interests must still satisfy the applicable rules</li><li>Personal trust overrides every professional duty</li><li>Only written disputes can create conflicts</li><li>Consent is unnecessary whenever the parties are friendly</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A professional relationship needs an independent duties analysis.</p></details><p><strong>When is consent a sufficient response to a conflict?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whenever fees would otherwise be lost</li><li>Only where the governing rule permits it and its conditions are met</li><li>Whenever one party says the arrangement is convenient</li><li>For every conflict without disclosure</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Some conflicts or circumstances may not be cured by consent.</p></details><p><strong>Why is temporarily using a deposit for office rent problematic?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Temporary use never requires records</li><li>A future repayment promise automatically creates permission</li><li>The authority and duties for money held for another must be respected</li><li>Any professional may borrow all client funds privately</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Client property is not unrestricted personal or business working capital.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Conflict and Informed consent.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Run a fictional conflict check involving a current client, former client and personal business interest.</li><li>Design a deposit ledger and communications plan that preserve authority, traceability and confidentiality.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write an ethics decision note covering acceptance or referral, confidentiality safeguards and deposit handling. Include the relevant local rules and facts requiring clarification.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M40:Appellate Advocacy, Written Submissions and Oral Argument": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 40</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: APPELLATE ADVOCACY, WRITTEN SUBMISSIONS AND ORAL ARGUMENT</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Legal Ethics and Trial Advocacy</td><td>Appellate Advocacy, Written Submissions and Oral Argument</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain appellate advocacy, written submissions and oral argument using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Appellate advocacy presents a legally available challenge or defence of a decision through issues, standards, record and remedy. Written submissions develop the argument; oral argument addresses its decisive points and the court’s questions.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Review issue</td><td>Question the appellate process can lawfully determine</td></tr><tr><td>Record reference</td><td>Pinpoint identification of relevant material in the record</td></tr><tr><td>Requested relief</td><td>Specific lawful order sought from the reviewing court</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the review issue before retelling the entire dispute. Explain the governing rule and standard, the challenged reasoning or procedural act, the supporting record and why the alleged error matters. Distinguish correcting an error of law from asking a reviewing court to exercise powers it does not possess. Accurate record references and fair treatment of adverse authority are essential.</p><p>Oral argument is a conversation directed at the court’s decision, not a memorised speech immune to questions. Answer the question asked, state candid limits and return to the logical route to the requested relief. Concede an immaterial weakness rather than inventing evidence. A respondent should defend the actual decision on available grounds while respecting the record and procedure. Practise a concise opening, a response to a difficult question and a final statement of the lawful order sought.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a supplier challenges a judgment interpreting a delivery clause, but the record contains a later signed variation favourable to the respondent. A weak appeal ignores that document and asks the court to invent a new bargain. A strong submission identifies the review question, addresses the variation candidly, explains the governing interpretation argument and requests relief within the court’s powers. In oral practice the learner answers a bench question about the adverse document directly.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read current Liberian Supreme Court and other applicable appellate rules. Work from a public judgment or fictional record and verify local filing and presentation requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/court-rules/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Court rules — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Build a chain from reviewable issue to rule, record, materiality and relief. Rehearse direct answers to counterarguments rather than relying on recitation.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Appellate advocacy presents a legally available challenge or defence of a decision through issues, standards, record and remedy. Written submissions develop the argument; oral argument addresses its decisive points and the court’s questions.</li><li>Separate review issue from record reference.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Review issue</td><td>Question the appellate process can lawfully determine</td></tr><tr><td>Record reference</td><td>Pinpoint identification of relevant material in the record</td></tr><tr><td>Requested relief</td><td>Specific lawful order sought from the reviewing court</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Review issue</summary><p>Question the appellate process can lawfully determine</p></details><details><summary>Record reference</summary><p>Pinpoint identification of relevant material in the record</p></details><details><summary>Requested relief</summary><p>Specific lawful order sought from the reviewing court</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What makes an appellate issue more useful than a general complaint?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It assumes every appeal is an unrestricted new trial</li><li>It identifies a reviewable error, governing rule and material record</li><li>It repeats dissatisfaction without a legal ground</li><li>It asks the court to ignore adverse documents</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> An appellate argument must fit the authorised review process.</p></details><p><strong>How should an advocate address the signed variation adverse to their argument?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Invent a contradictory witness</li><li>Assume oral confidence removes the document</li><li>Explain its actual relevance and develop the argument candidly</li><li>Hide it from the record summary</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> Candour and accurate reasoning require engagement with adverse material.</p></details><p><strong>What is a sound response to a difficult bench question?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Continue a memorised speech without responding</li><li>Claim a source says something it does not</li><li>Promise facts outside the record</li><li>Answer it directly within the record and acknowledge any limit</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Oral advocacy should assist the court’s reasoning through accurate responses.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Review issue and Record reference.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Convert a fictional trial dispute into three candidate appellate issues and eliminate those unsupported by the record.</li><li>Run an oral-argument exercise where a partner interrupts with an adverse-authority or remedy question.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a 1,200-word mock appellate submission, a five-minute oral argument and written answers to five challenging bench questions.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M41:Legal Positivism, Natural Law and African Legal Pluralism": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 41</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: LEGAL POSITIVISM, NATURAL LAW AND AFRICAN LEGAL PLURALISM</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Jurisprudence and Dispute Resolution</td><td>Legal Positivism, Natural Law and African Legal Pluralism</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain legal positivism, natural law and african legal pluralism using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Legal positivist theories examine law’s social sources and institutional recognition. Natural-law theories connect law and moral reasoning in different ways. Legal pluralism studies interacting normative orders; it does not mean every claimed community norm overrides superior law.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal validity</td><td>Status under the relevant system of legal recognition</td></tr><tr><td>Moral evaluation</td><td>Reasoned assessment of justice or legitimacy</td></tr><tr><td>Legal pluralism</td><td>Study of interacting normative orders and their institutional relations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>State a theory carefully before evaluating it. Positivism does not require approving every valid rule morally; natural-law approaches are not one identical doctrine; pluralism is not an excuse to ignore constitutional safeguards. Distinguish the descriptive question “what is legally recognised?” from the evaluative question “is the rule just?” and the institutional question “who may change or apply it?”.</p><p>Use a concrete conflict to test each theory’s explanatory value. Identify statutory and customary claims, constitutional limits and affected interests, then explain how competing accounts of authority interpret the situation. Avoid treating “African law” as a single uniform set of customs. Communities, histories and recognised institutions differ. A strong essay fairly presents an opposing theory and states what evidence or authority would limit its argument rather than declaring one label the answer to every dispute.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a community practice excludes a group from participation in a local decision, while an affected person invokes a constitutional protection. A weak essay says either “custom is always superior” or “every custom is meaningless”. A strong essay separates recognition, moral evaluation and superior-law limits, explains how different theories frame the dispute, and returns to verified Liberian authority for any legal conclusion.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the Constitution and verified local legal materials to connect jurisprudential arguments with actual institutions. Theories help explain legal reasoning but do not themselves enact a local rule.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Define each theory, apply it to the same conflict and evaluate its strengths and limits. Keep the operative constitutional conclusion distinct from the essay’s moral or explanatory preference.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Legal positivist theories examine law’s social sources and institutional recognition. Natural-law theories connect law and moral reasoning in different ways. Legal pluralism studies interacting normative orders; it does not mean every claimed community norm overrides superior law.</li><li>Separate legal validity from moral evaluation.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal validity</td><td>Status under the relevant system of legal recognition</td></tr><tr><td>Moral evaluation</td><td>Reasoned assessment of justice or legitimacy</td></tr><tr><td>Legal pluralism</td><td>Study of interacting normative orders and their institutional relations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Legal validity</summary><p>Status under the relevant system of legal recognition</p></details><details><summary>Moral evaluation</summary><p>Reasoned assessment of justice or legitimacy</p></details><details><summary>Legal pluralism</summary><p>Study of interacting normative orders and their institutional relations</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why does legal positivism not necessarily endorse every recognised rule morally?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Every valid rule automatically resolves every moral question</li><li>Moral criticism creates legislation immediately</li><li>Describing legal validity differs from approving a rule’s justice</li><li>Positivism requires praise of every law</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: C.</strong> The theory’s source analysis and moral evaluation are distinct matters.</p></details><p><strong>What is a responsible pluralist analysis of a customary claim?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Assume every community has identical rules</li><li>Treat all customs as automatically supreme</li><li>Treat pluralism as permission to ignore rights</li><li>Identify the particular norm, institution, recognition and superior-law limits</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Pluralism examines specific interacting norms rather than a universal override.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish theory from the legal source deciding a Liberian claim?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A theoretical explanation does not itself enact or repeal the governing rule</li><li>An essay title is binding judicial authority</li><li>Every philosopher’s view is a local statute</li><li>No legal conclusion can ever use theory</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Jurisprudence informs reasoning while operative local authority remains necessary.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Legal validity and Moral evaluation.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Classify fictional statements as legal-status claims, moral evaluations or institutional questions.</li><li>Debate a community-rule dispute from two theories while jointly identifying the legal authority neither theory can replace.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 1,200-word comparative jurisprudence essay using the fictional practice. Present two theories fairly and distinguish their explanatory claims from the local legal conclusion.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M41:Arbitration Agreements, Awards and Settlement Enforcement": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Complete Liberian Law Programme • Module 41</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: ARBITRATION AGREEMENTS, AWARDS AND SETTLEMENT ENFORCEMENT</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applied Jurisprudence and Dispute Resolution</td><td>Arbitration Agreements, Awards and Settlement Enforcement</td><td>Applied legal studies</td><td>60–120 minutes including reading and exercises; longer seminars may need additional time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain arbitration agreements, awards and settlement enforcement using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>An arbitration agreement submits covered disputes to a legally recognised arbitral process. An award is the tribunal’s decision. Recognition, enforcement or challenge through a court is different from freely retrying the entire dispute on appeal.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Arbitration agreement</td><td>Legally effective submission of covered disputes to arbitration</td></tr><tr><td>Award</td><td>Decision issued through the arbitral process</td></tr><tr><td>Enforcement or challenge</td><td>Court process under the governing conditions and grounds</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Examine consent, capacity, parties, subject matter, scope and governing procedural terms. A dispute-resolution heading does not by itself prove a valid agreement covering every later dispute. Identify how the tribunal is constituted, notice is given and the parties can present their cases under the applicable framework. A settlement remains an agreement whose legal effect and enforcement require their own analysis.</p><p>An award must be distinguished from mediation advice, an expert report or a private invoice. Read its operative relief and the legal basis for recognition, enforcement or challenge. Review grounds and procedure come from the governing instruments; dissatisfaction with the result does not automatically create a full merits rehearing. For a foreign award, verify the applicable route and party-specific treaty position before invoking any convention. Keep the agreement, notices, tribunal record, award and payment information together.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a supplier contract contains an arbitration clause, but one party says the disputed later service order was outside it. After an award, that party refuses to pay and asks for an unrestricted new trial. A weak answer assumes the clause covers everything and all court review is impossible. A strong answer analyses scope, consent, the actual procedural record, operative relief and the local grounds and route for enforcement or challenge.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Civil Procedure Law provisions on arbitration and applicable court rules. For an international issue, verify treaty status and the relevant local enforcement framework.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 14 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Answer agreement scope, arbitral process and court effect separately. Use the actual award and local review grounds instead of treating arbitration as either unlimited immunity or an automatic new trial.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>An arbitration agreement submits covered disputes to a legally recognised arbitral process. An award is the tribunal’s decision. Recognition, enforcement or challenge through a court is different from freely retrying the entire dispute on appeal.</li><li>Separate arbitration agreement from award.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Arbitration agreement</td><td>Legally effective submission of covered disputes to arbitration</td></tr><tr><td>Award</td><td>Decision issued through the arbitral process</td></tr><tr><td>Enforcement or challenge</td><td>Court process under the governing conditions and grounds</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Arbitration agreement</summary><p>Legally effective submission of covered disputes to arbitration</p></details><details><summary>Award</summary><p>Decision issued through the arbitral process</p></details><details><summary>Enforcement or challenge</summary><p>Court process under the governing conditions and grounds</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why does an arbitration heading not establish coverage of every dispute?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Any heading automatically overrides every statutory limit</li><li>Every later transaction has identical parties and terms</li><li>Mediation and arbitration are always the same process</li><li>The clause’s consent, scope and legal validity must be analysed</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: D.</strong> Coverage depends on the actual agreement and applicable framework.</p></details><p><strong>What distinguishes an arbitral award from mediation advice?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An award is a decision through an arbitral process rather than a proposed voluntary resolution</li><li>Any mediator’s suggestion automatically becomes an award</li><li>Every private invoice is an arbitral decision</li><li>An award requires no governing agreement or procedure</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The nature and legal basis of the instrument determine its effect.</p></details><p><strong>A party dislikes an award’s result. What must be checked before court challenge?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The losing party’s preferred timetable alone</li><li>The legally recognised grounds and proper local procedure</li><li>A universal entitlement to an unrestricted new merits trial</li><li>A rule that no award can ever be questioned</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: B.</strong> Review or challenge is defined by the governing framework.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Arbitration agreement and Award.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Compare mediation, arbitration and court litigation clauses for consent, decision-making and enforcement.</li><li>Audit a fictional award file for missing agreement, notice, operative relief and payment records.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a dispute-resolution clause comparison and an award-file checklist. Write a 1,000-word memorandum distinguishing scope, process, enforcement and possible challenge.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M42:Civil and Criminal Litigation File": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 42 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Civil and Criminal Litigation File</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>Use two fictional files: a civil equipment claim and a contested criminal allegation. Keep their burdens, procedures and remedies separate.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>A civil pleading and provisional-relief outline with supporting evidence</li><li>A criminal charge-and-disclosure checklist and individual elements chart</li><li>A 1,200-word comparative procedure memorandum with verified local authorities</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>", "M42:Commercial, Employment and Regulatory Practice Portfolio": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 42 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Commercial, Employment and Regulatory Practice Portfolio</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>A fictional training enterprise plans to incorporate, employ staff, buy goods and bid for a public contract.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>A corporate authority and conflict checklist plus a sourced compliance calendar</li><li>Employment terms, pay-reconciliation and safety checklists</li><li>A sales risk matrix and current PPCC evaluation-and-accountability note</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>", "M42:Estates, Community Land and Rights Clinic Simulation": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 42 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Estates, Community Land and Rights Clinic Simulation</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>A fictional community seeks guidance concerning disputed inherited property, representation and a proposed development.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>A simulated interview chronology and confidentiality plan</li><li>An estates-and-land authority matrix and community-participation issues register</li><li>A 1,200-word balanced legal memorandum and a plain-language public explainer</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>", "M42:Professional Moot Court and Final Legal Opinion": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 42 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Professional Moot Court and Final Legal Opinion</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>Use a fictional appellate record involving a company supply contract, a disputed variation and a conflict of interest. Argue both sides before writing a reasoned opinion.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>Two 1,200-word appellate submissions with primary authorities and record pinpoints</li><li>Two seven-minute oral arguments plus answers to five bench questions per side</li><li>A 1,200-word reasoned opinion, eight-project portfolio index and reflective self-assessment</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>"};
if(typeof document!=="undefined"&&!document.getElementById("law-note-style")){var s=document.createElement("style");s.id="law-note-style";s.textContent=".law-note{font-size:1rem;line-height:1.8;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere}.law-note h3{color:#002868;font-size:1.4rem;line-height:1.4}.law-note h4{color:#002868;margin:1.8rem 0 .6rem}.law-banner{background:linear-gradient(120deg,#002868,#087a9e);color:white;border-radius:12px;padding:1rem;font-weight:700}.law-table-wrap{overflow-x:auto;margin:1rem 0}.law-note table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;min-width:280px}.law-note th,.law-note td{padding:.75rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}.law-note th{background:#eff6ff}.law-note details{border:1px solid #bfdbfe;border-radius:10px;padding:.8rem;margin:.7rem 0;background:#f8fbff}.law-note summary{cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;color:#002868;min-height:28px}.law-note a{color:#075985;text-decoration:underline}.law-callout{border-left:4px solid #e31e24;background:#fff7ed;padding:1rem;margin:1.5rem 0}.law-footnote{font-size:.85rem;color:#475569}@media(max-width:480px){.law-note h3{font-size:1.15rem}.law-note td,.law-note th{padding:.5rem}}@media print{.law-note details>*{display:block}.law-note{font-size:11pt}.law-callout{break-inside:avoid}}";document.head.appendChild(s);}
})();
